// profiles' "How it was fought" (profile name: [slug, version of data/battles/<slug>.json, [[phase title, text]]]).
var BATTLE_MAPS = {
  "Battle of Actium": ["battle-of-actium","fd8e5da727",[["Antony's fleet comes out and forms line","Octavian's fleet waited beyond the straits. Antony led his ships out through the straits and they formed a line, with Cleopatra's squadron behind them, and stayed quiet."],["Octavian steers to the right","After a short hesitation Octavian ordered his ships to steer to the right and pass the enemy's ships. Fearing that his line would be surrounded, Antony was forced to give the order to attack."],["Sosius attacks and Octavian's left stays out of range","Sosius opened the attack from Antony's left wing. Antony hoped to drive back Agrippa's wing at the north end of his line with his biggest ships, but Octavian's fleet stayed out of range."],["The fleets fight through the afternoon","The two lines closed after midday and fought on all afternoon. Antony's larger ships rained missiles on the smaller ones, which closed in, shot at the crews and drew off again, and neither side gained a clear result."],["Cleopatra's squadron sails for the open sea","Cleopatra's squadron, in the rear, took no part in the fighting and retreated to the open sea. A breeze sprang up in the right direction and the ships were soon out of sight."],["Antony follows the fleeing squadron","Antony had not seen the signal. Believing that it was mere panic and that all was lost, he followed the fleeing squadron, and Cleopatra's ships were soon out of sight."],["Some fight on into the night","Some of the ships fought on, and only long after nightfall, when many were blazing from the firebrands thrown upon them, was the work done."],["Antony escapes and the rest are taken","Antony moved to a smaller vessel with his flag and slipped away with a few ships as escort to help break through Octavian's lines. The ships left behind were captured or sunk."]]],
  "Battle of Adrianople": ["battle-of-adrianople","fccb84b4e9",[["The armies face each other below the hill","The Goths held a wagon circle on a hilltop, and the Romans, tired and thirsty after a march, came up facing it. Both sides drew up in battle order, and the Gothic cavalry was away."],["The shield-archers attack without orders","Units of the imperial guard began the fight on their own and attacked the Goths without support. They were easily pushed back."],["The left wing reaches the wagons","The shield-archers fell back while the Roman left wing pressed on until it reached the circle of wagons. By then, the accounts say, it was too late."],["The Gothic cavalry returns and surrounds the Romans","The Gothic cavalry came back from foraging and arrived to support the infantry. It surrounded the Roman troops, who were already in disarray after the failed assault."],["The Romans are driven back and routed","The Romans retreated to the base of the hill, where they could not manoeuvre in their heavy armour. Casualties and exhaustion led to a rout, and the cavalry kept up the attack until nightfall."]]],
  "Battle of Adwa": ["battle-of-adwa","6fe85b4f69",[["The Italian columns march out in parallel","Baratieri's columns advanced towards the Ethiopian positions to reach the crests of three mountains, Albertone on the left, Arimondi in the centre and Dabormida on the right, with Ellena's reserve following behind Arimondi."],["Albertone's column leaves its line","Albertone veered north instead of continuing west, then was led on by his guides as far as Mount Abba Gerima. When he saw the confusion he found himself cut off from the rest of the army."],["Ethiopian units charge Albertone's vanguard","Albertone's vanguard approached the Ethiopian outposts near Adwa and was charged by Ethiopian units coming from the Enda Garima and Gessoso mountains. The charge forced Albertone back to the western slopes of Mount Semaiata."],["Albertone's reserve regiment pushes them back","Faced with attacks on his left flank, Albertone committed his reserve regiment and pushed the Ethiopians back by about 1 km. His askaris then held their positions for over two hours, with the artillery inflicting heavy casualties."],["Dabormida is cut off in the valley","Dabormida pushed his brigade into the valley of Mariam Shewito to relieve Albertone, away from where it was meant to go. A gap of about two miles opened, and Ras Makonnen advanced into it."],["Menelik's reserve and the flanking troops attack","Menelik was persuaded to release his reserve of 25,000 men. His guard attacked Albertone's front while Taytu's troops attacked the right and Ras Mikael's troops the left."],["Albertone's brigade is routed","Outflanked, Albertone's brigade began a fighting retreat that soon turned into a rout. Its covering batteries were captured, and the askaris fled towards Arimondi's positions around Mount Belah."],["The Ethiopians split into two columns","After the rout the Ethiopian columns came down on Arimondi's position and split in two. One struck Mount Belah on the Italian right, and the other the junction on the Italian left, held by the 3rd Native Battalion, detached from Ellena."],["The Ethiopians take the spur and the left collapses","The column on the left occupied a rocky spur, Zebàn Daarò, north-west of Mount Belah and outflanked Arimondi's right. Bersaglieri sent to retake it were swept away, and on the far left the 3rd Native Battalion collapsed."],["Ellena's regiment is overwhelmed on the left","Baratieri ordered reinforcements from Ellena's reserve to stop the outflanking. Ellena sent the 5th Infantry Regiment under Colonel Nava to plug the gap on the left flank, but the advance of the Ethiopian units overwhelmed it."],["The centre gives way and Baratieri orders a retreat","Pressed at the front and outflanked on both sides, Arimondi's brigade gave way. Baratieri ordered a general retreat towards Adi Ugri and Adi Keyh, and Ras Mikael's Oromo cavalry cut down the retreating Italians."],["Dabormida's final assault is repulsed","Ethiopian troops that had beaten the other brigades moved into the valley. Dabormida then launched a final assault, which was repulsed with heavy losses, and Colonel Airaghi was mortally wounded."],["Dabormida's brigade is encircled","A retreat was ordered, but as the troops tried to withdraw towards the mouth of the valley they met large numbers of Ethiopian troops on the surrounding plain. The brigade found itself effectively encircled."],["A company holds the pass for the survivors","The route towards the Erarà heights stayed open because Lieutenant Rayneri's company repelled repeated Ethiopian attacks and secured the pass leading to the hill, which let many Italian survivors escape."]]],
  "Battle of Agincourt": ["battle-of-agincourt","5ca3cf4637",[["The two armies are drawn up","The English stood in three groups across the defile, with archers on the flanks and men-at-arms in the centre. The French stood in a vanguard and a main battle behind it, with dismounted men-at-arms in the wings, cavalry on the flanks of the vanguard and a mounted rearguard."],["The English move up and reset their stakes","Henry ordered his army forward and the stakes were pulled out of the ground, carried to the new position and set again to protect the archers."],["The longbowmen shoot from extreme range","The English longbowmen began to shoot at the French from extreme range, about 300 yards. The French cavalry did not charge until after this first volley."],["The French cavalry charge the archers","The French cavalry, disorganised and not at full numbers, charged towards the longbowmen. They could not outflank the archers because of the woodland, and could not charge through the stakes."],["The wounded horses break back through the French","The charge failed and the cavalry fell back. Wounded and panicking horses galloped through the advancing infantry, scattering and trampling it, while the vanguard walked on through the mud."],["The French men-at-arms reach the English line","The surviving French men-at-arms reached the front of the English line and pushed it back, while the longbowmen on the flanks went on shooting, now at close range."],["The archers drop their bows and attack","When their arrows ran out the archers dropped their bows and attacked the disordered, tired and wounded French men-at-arms with hatchets, swords, spears and the mallets they had used to drive in their stakes."],["The second line joins and the fight is lost in the press","The French second line joined the attack but was swallowed up, the narrow ground hampering it. The fighting lasted about three hours, until the leaders of the second line were killed or captured, as those of the first line had been."]]],
  "Battle of Ain Jalut": ["battle-of-ain-jalut","ecafbfd832",[["The Mongols hold the spring","Kitbuqa reached Ain Jalut first and camped by the spring, with Mount Gilboa covering one flank. Baibars, leading the Mamluk vanguard, sighted the camp from a height, while Qutuz hid the bulk of his army in the highlands."],["The Mongols advance and Baibars strikes and runs","The Mongols moved first. For many hours Baibars provoked them with hit-and-run attacks and kept most of his men out of the fight."],["Baibars feigns a final retreat","After another heavy Mongol assault Baibars and his men pretended to break and rode back towards the highlands. The aim was to draw the Mongols after them into an ambush."],["Kitbuqa follows with all his troops","Kitbuqa, already provoked by the constant flight of Baibars and his men, did not suspect a trick. He marched forward with all of his troops on the trail of the fleeing Mamluks."],["The trap closes","When the Mongols reached the highlands the hidden Mamluks came out, shooting arrows and charging with their cavalry. The Mongols found themselves surrounded."],["The Mongols fight to break out","The Mongol army fought aggressively to break out of the ring. Their attack almost destroyed the Mamluk left wing, which gave way before it."],["Qutuz throws away his helmet and charges","Qutuz saw the left wing almost destroyed. He threw away his combat helmet so that his warriors could recognise him, then rushed towards the weakened side, followed by his own unit."],["The Mongols are pushed back towards Baysan","The Mongols were pushed back by the Mamluk attack and fled to the neighbourhood of Baysan. Qutuz’s forces followed them eastwards across the field."],["The Mongols reorganise and counter-attack","The Mongols managed to reorganise and returned to the battlefield, where they made a successful counter-attack."],["The battle turns and Kitbuqa dies","The battle then shifted towards the Mamluks, who held the advantage of ground and morale. Some of the Mongols were forced to retreat, and Kitbuqa perished with almost all the rest of the army that remained in the region."]]],
  "Battle of Alarcos": ["battle-of-alarcos","d32b363645",[["The two armies form up","The Almohads formed around the hill of La Cabeza in a vanguard and a rearguard. The Castilian king placed his heavy cavalry in one compact group and kept the infantry and Military Orders to follow."],["The Castilian cavalry charges down","Alfonso chose to move first. His forces came down from their high position and the heavy cavalry charged across the lower plain into the front lines of the Muslims."],["The Bani Marin and Zenatas are dispersed","In a three hour engagement the knights first dispersed the Bani Marin and the Zenatas, who held the first line of the Almohad vanguard."],["The Hintata are attacked and the vizier killed","The knights then attacked the Hintata, the vizier's own guard behind the first line, and killed the vizier Abu Yahya, who commanded the Almohad vanguard."],["The Andalusi forces are routed","The knights then struggled against the al-Andalus forces of Ibn Sanadid and routed them, but they were fatigued and men were falling."],["The Almohad flank and rearguard surround the knights","As the Christians tired and fell, the Arab left under Yarmun enveloped their flank and rear. The Almohad rearguard under al-Mansur then attacked and surrounded the knights."],["Alfonso commits the remaining forces","Alfonso advanced with all his remaining forces into the melee and was assaulted from all sides under a rain of arrows. For some time he fought hand-to-hand."],["Alfonso is taken from the field","His bodyguard removed Alfonso from the action, almost by force, after he had fought in the melee, and together they fled to Toledo."],["The infantry and the Orders are destroyed","With the ring closed round the knights, the Castilian infantry was destroyed, together with most of the Orders which had supported them."],["López de Haro reaches Alarcos","López de Haro tried to force his way through the ring of enemy forces. He finally took refuge in the unfinished fortress of Alarcos with just a fraction of his knights."]]],
  "Battle of Alesia": ["battle-of-alesia","a4161d09ad",[["Two lines of fortification round Alesia","Caesar shut the Gauls into Alesia and built two lines of works, one facing the town and one facing outwards against a relief force. The Roman lines, with a camp of two legions on a hill beside them, stood ready."],["The relief force arrives and camps on a hill","The Gallic relief force reached the field and camped on a hill one mile from the Roman works. The next day the Gauls camped nearer the town."],["The relief force and the besieged attack together","The relief force arrived and attacked the outer line while the besieged Gauls attacked the inner line at the same moment. The combined attack failed."],["The Gauls attack again at night","The next day the Gauls attacked again at night, the relief force against the outer line and the besieged against the inner one."],["The Gauls withdraw at first light","At first light the relief force withdrew, fearing a Roman sally that would surround it. The besieged, delayed by having to fill trenches dug by the Romans, went back into the town."],["Sixty thousand Gauls attack the weak camp","The Gauls chose a weak point where a camp of two legions stood on bad ground and sent 60,000 men against it, attacking at noon. Vercingetorix sallied against the inner line at the same time."],["Labienus and Caesar come to the weak point","Caesar sent Labienus to support the weak area, then led fresh troops there himself. The attack on the inner line was repelled."],["Caesar marches to Labienus and sends the cavalry round","Caesar marched to help Labienus with four cohorts. He ordered part of the cavalry to follow him and part to leave the outer line and attack the relief force from the rear."],["Roman cavalry takes the Gauls in the rear","Part of the Roman cavalry left the outer line and appeared at the rear of the Gauls. The Roman troops advanced rapidly, the Gauls fled, and the cavalry intercepted them. The besieged Gauls were pulled back from the fortification."],["The Roman cavalry pursues at midnight","At midnight the Roman cavalry was sent after the Gauls who had fled. Many were killed and many escaped to the lands they had come from."]]],
  "Battle of Austerlitz": ["battle-of-austerlitz","66c07b6c94",[["The armies take their ground","Napoleon abandoned the Pratzen Heights and moved all his troops but the right flank into dead ground. The Allies planned four columns against the French right, with the Russian Guard in reserve and Bagration on their right."],["The Allies attack Telnitz and drive the French out","The first Allied lines attacked Telnitz, which the 3rd Line Regiment defended. Several heavy Allied charges evicted the French from the village and forced them onto the other side of the Goldbach."],["Davout's men retake Telnitz","The first men of Davout's corps arrived and threw the Allies out of Telnitz. Allied hussars attacked them and the Allies left the village again, and further Allied attacks out of Telnitz were checked by French artillery."],["The fight for Sokolnitz","The second and third Allied columns attacked Sokolnitz and its castle. The French were forced out, counterattacked and regained the village, and were thrown out again until Friant's division of Davout's corps retook it."],["The Tsar orders Kutuzov off the Pratzen Heights","Kutuzov's corps had halted on the Heights, but the Tsar ordered it to withdraw. That left the Allied centre weak."],["Soult's corps climbs the Heights","The mist was clearing and Napoleon ordered the attack. St. Hilaire's division led the climb, and as it ascended the sun tore the mist apart. The Russians on top of the Heights were stunned to see so many French troops coming."],["The Allies drive the French back down the slope","Allied commanders brought some delayed detachments of the fourth column into the struggle, and men of the second column joined them. After over an hour of fighting they forced the French to withdraw down the slopes."],["The French bayonet the Allies off the Heights","Gripped by desperation, St. Hilaire's men struck hard again and bayoneted the Allies out of the Heights, and the Allied IV Corps fell back from them."],["The Russian Guard counterattacks","Grand Duke Constantine's Guard counterattacked in Vandamme's section, forcing a bloody effort and taking a French standard. Napoleon sent in his heavy Guard cavalry, and with large masses of cavalry on both sides no victory was clear."],["The Russian Guard breaks","Drouet's division deployed on the flank and let the French cavalry rally behind it. The horse artillery of the Guard hurt the Russian cavalry and fusiliers, and the Russians broke and were pursued for about a quarter of a mile."],["Liechtenstein's cavalry is beaten in the north","Liechtenstein's heavy cavalry attacked Murat's lighter cavalry. Caffarelli's infantry halted the Russian assaults, and Murat sent in two cuirassier divisions, which won the long and bitter mêlée."],["Lannes drives Bagration off the field","On Napoleon's orders Lannes led his corps against Bagration's men and, after hard fighting, drove Bagration off the field. Napoleon called off a pursuit."],["Sokolnitz falls and the Allied army leaves the field","St. Hilaire's division and part of Davout's corps broke through at Sokolnitz. The commanders of the first two columns fled, and general panic seized the Allied army, which abandoned the field in all possible directions."]]],
  "Battle of Ayacucho": ["battle-of-ayacucho","aed8bd5b7b",[["The armies face each other","The independentist line formed an angle, with Córdova on the right, La Mar on the left, Miller's cavalry in the centre and Lara in reserve. The royalists stood on the heights of Condorcunca, with Valdés' vanguard on their right."],["The royalists come down the slope","Canterac's plan sent Valdés across the Pampas to turn Sucre's left while the rest of the army descended frontally from the hill. The royalists moved onto an exposed slope, and Córdova's division, supported by Miller's cavalry, fired on them."],["Córdova's fire pushes back Villalobos","Córdova's division, firing steadily on the royalist formations, pushed back the scattered men of Villalobos' Second Division, who had come down onto the plain."],["Monet crosses the ravine and is surrounded","Seeing his left in trouble, Monet crossed the ravine without waiting for his cavalry and led his First Division against Córdova. He formed two battalions, but the independentist division surrounded him before the rest could form."],["Monet's division breaks","Monet was wounded and three of his commanders were killed. The scattered royalist divisions dragged the masses of militia with them."],["Ferraz's cavalry charges and is driven off","Ferraz's cavalry charged the squadrons pursuing Monet's broken left. Confusion and crossfire from the infantry caused heavy casualties, and the survivors hurriedly left the field."],["La Mar and Lara stop Valdés' assault","At the other end of the line, La Mar's and Lara's divisions together stopped the assault of Valdés' veterans, who had launched themselves at the isolated building held by some independentist companies."],["Reinforced, the independentists attack again","Defeated at first, the independentists were soon reinforced and went back to the attack, helped in the end by Córdova's victorious division."],["The reserve scatters and the viceroy is taken","Canterac led the reserve across the plain, but its recruits scattered before they met the enemy. By one o'clock the viceroy had been wounded and made prisoner. Valdés retreated to the hill held by his rearguard."]]],
  "Battle of Badr": ["battle-of-badr","5ceea326b3",[["The armies face each other across the valley","The Quraysh were encamped in the south-eastern part of the valley near the road from Mecca. The Muslims had come from the north and held the well nearest the Meccans."],["Al-Aswad challenges for the well","Al-Aswad, a man of Abu Jahl's clan, swore to drink from the Muslims' well or destroy it or die for it. Hamza came out and fought him. Hamza struck his leg and then dealt a second blow that killed him."],["Three Meccans challenge and are answered","Utba, his brother Shayba and his son al-Walid came out of the Meccan ranks. Hamza, Ubayda and Ali went out to meet them. Ali killed al-Walid and Hamza killed Shayba, while Ubayda was seriously wounded."],["Ubayda is carried back to the Muslim lines","Hamza found Ubayda seriously wounded after the fight between Ali and al-Walid. Ali and Hamza then carried him back into the Muslim lines."],["The Meccans charge","A shower of arrows from both sides followed the duels. The Meccans then took the offensive and charged upon the Muslim lines, and the Muslim duellists were carried back into the lines."],["The Muslims counter-attack","Muhammad gave the order for a counter-attack and the Muslim army rushed the Quraysh lines. Both Muslim battalions moved forward against the Meccan body."],["The Meccan lines break","The Meccans broke and ran. The fighting lasted a few hours and was over by early afternoon, after the Meccan army had scattered."]]],
  "Battle of Boyacá": ["battle-of-boyaca","cf99fd8c4a",[["The vanguards meet at Casa de Teja","The Royalist vanguard had stopped near Casa de Teja to rest. Patriot skirmishers met it, and Santander ordered the Cazadores Battalion to attack the Royalists there."],["The Royalist vanguard falls back over the bridge","The Royalist vanguard crossed the bridge over the Teatinos River and took defensive positions there. Santander's full vanguard reached Casa de Teja, and he sent Bejar down the river to cross it and attack the Royalists from the rear."],["The Royalist rearguard is cut off","The Royalist rearguard was still several kilometres behind, and Anzoátegui blocked the way between it and the vanguard. Outnumbered, it retreated to a small hill and formed with its guns in the centre, infantry beside them and cavalry on the wings."],["Flank attack ordered on the Royalist rearguard","Anzoátegui ordered a flank attack on the Royalist rearguard. Barcelona and Bravos de Paez were to attack on the right side and the British Legion and Rifles on the left, and the two groups moved up to the Royalist line."],["Sandes charges the Royalist guns","Arthur Sandes, commander of the Rifles, charged the Royalist artillery. The charge disrupted the arming of the three guns, which had been carried unassembled. Only one was put together, and it fired three shots before its carriage broke."],["Patriot lancers drive back the Royalist dragoons","Bolívar ordered lancers to attack the centre of the Royalist infantry. The 3rd and 5th companies of the Granadan Dragoons charged the patriot cavalry, which turned to face them, and the dragoons retreated because the lancers' lances were longer."],["The Royalist infantry breaks in panic","The Mounted Grenadiers ran towards Samaca. The charge of the two patriot cavalry squadrons caused panic among the infantry, which was formed in a column. It was ordered to charge with the bayonet but did not, and it broke."],["Bejar fords the river behind the Royalist vanguard","A kilometre and a half behind Casa de Teja, Bejar's detachment managed to ford the river and approached the rear of the Royalist vanguard on the far bank."],["Santander storms the bridge","When Bejar's detachment reached the Royalist vanguard, the two engaged. Santander led a bayonet charge across the bridge, and the Royalist vanguard fled, leaving its commander Colonel Juan Tolrá dead on the bridge."]]],
  "Battle of Cannae": ["battle-of-cannae","1198dbe76a",[["The armies draw up facing each other","The Romans faced east with the Aufidus on their right and massed their infantry deep in the centre. Hannibal put the Hispanic and Gallic infantry in the middle, the African infantry at the ends of the infantry line and the cavalry on the flanks."],["Hannibal advances his centre in a crescent","As the armies advanced, Hannibal took the Hispanic and Gallic companies forward from the middle of his line. The flanking companies fell back behind them, so that the line curved out towards the Romans and the Africans were kept in reserve."],["The cavalry clash on both flanks","The battle opened with a fierce cavalry fight on the flanks. On the Roman right the Hispanic and Gallic horse fought hard, and on the other flank the Numidians kept the Roman allied cavalry busy."],["The Roman right wing is driven back","The Carthaginian horse got the upper hand and cut down the Roman cavalry on the right without giving quarter. The Roman right wing retreated, and Hasdrubal did not chase it."],["Hasdrubal rides across and the allied cavalry breaks","Hasdrubal led his victorious horse to the other side of the field to attack the allied cavalry. Assailed from both sides, the allies broke before he reached them, and the Numidians pursued them off the field."],["The centre gives way step by step","The Roman heavy infantry pressed forward and Hannibal held his men together in a controlled retreat. The curve buckled inwards as the Romans advanced and reserve lines pushed into the gaps."],["The Carthaginian cavalry attacks the Roman rear","With the Roman cavalry driven off on both flanks, the Carthaginian horse had time to ride round and attack the Roman centre from behind."],["The Africans turn in against the Roman flanks","Hannibal ordered the African infantry to turn inwards and advance against the Roman flanks. Together with the cavalry behind, this formed a pincer round the Roman infantry."],["The Romans are enclosed and killed","Enclosed in a pocket with no means of escape, the Roman infantry was cut down by the cavalry behind it, the African infantry on both sides and the centre in front."]]],
  "Battle of Caporetto": ["battle-of-caporetto","a950ed3229",[["The attackers gather east of the Italian line","The German and Austro-Hungarian plan sent von Below's army against the Caporetto sector, with Boroević's army attacking the eastern end of the salient and ground near the Adriatic. The Italian Second Army held the line, with the IV and XXVII Corps on either side of the valley."],["Gas near Bovec","In the north, near Bovec, 894 tubes were fired electrically and smothered the Italian trenches in poison gas. The defenders fled, and 500 to 600 of them were killed."],["Mortars and the guns open fire","After a quiet spell, mortars bombarded the Italian wire and trenches at 06:00. At 06:41 2,200 guns opened fire, many aimed at the valley road along which Italian reserves were advancing."],["The infantry break the line in the valley","After two large mines were detonated under strong points on the heights, the infantry attacked and breached the line between the IV and XXVII Corps. Alpine troops went along the ridges on either side to protect the attackers' flanks."],["The central column drives down the valley road","The attackers in the valley marched almost unopposed along the road, and some advanced 25 kilometres on the first day. The Italians beat back the attackers on either side, but the central penetration threw their whole army into disarray."],["Cadorna orders the retreat","Cadorna ordered most of the Italian force to retreat across the Tagliamento, which took four full days. The attackers were close behind."],["The pursuers ambush the retreating Italians","The German and Austro-Hungarian armies were on the heels of the Italians and ambushed the defenders whenever they could."],["The Italians break through the encirclement","The retreating Italian soldiers broke through the Austro-German encirclement and reached the Tagliamento, where the crossing continued."],["A bridgehead over the Tagliamento","After an attack by a battalion of the 4th Bosnian Infantry Regiment, the 55th Infantry Division established a bridgehead across the Tagliamento. The rapid success had stretched German and Austro-Hungarian supply lines to the breaking point."],["The Italians stand on the Piave","The Central Powers could not launch another attack to cut off part of the Italian army against the Adriatic. Cadorna retreated further and by 10 November held a position on the Piave River and Monte Grappa."]]],
  "Battle of Carrhae": ["battle-of-carrhae","ab6c6bea9a",[["The Romans form a hollow square","Cassius advised a line with infantry in the centre and cavalry on the wings. Crassus at first agreed, then changed his mind and redeployed his men into a hollow square, each side formed by twelve cohorts."],["The cataphracts show their armour","The Parthians beat hollow drums, and Surena ordered his cataphracts to cover their armour in cloths and advance. Within sight of the Romans they dropped the cloths together and showed their shining armour."],["Horse archers surround the square","Surena sent his horse archers round the Roman square, beyond the range of the pila. Crassus sent his skirmishers out to drive them off, and the archers' arrows drove the skirmishers back."],["The horse archers shoot into the square","The horse archers then engaged the legionaries. The legionaries' large shields and armour could not cover the whole body, and arrows struck them as the archers circled the square."],["The Romans advance and the archers withdraw","The Romans repeatedly advanced towards the Parthians to fight at close quarters. The horse archers always retreated safely and shot as they withdrew."],["The cataphracts charge the testudo","The legionaries locked their shields in the testudo, which limited their fighting in melee. The cataphracts repeatedly charged the line, and when the Romans loosened their formation the cataphracts drew back and the horse archers shot again."],["Crassus sends Publius against the archers","Crassus hoped his men could hold out until the Parthians ran out of arrows, but camels resupplied the horse archers. He then dispatched his son Publius with Gallic cavalry, archers and eight cohorts to drive them off."],["Publius is drawn away","The horse archers feigned retreat and drew Publius away from the main army. His force suffered heavy casualties from arrow fire as it followed."],["Publius's force is cut off on a hill","Once Publius was well apart from the army, the cataphracts confronted his force and the horse archers cut off its retreat. The Gauls retreated to a hill, where Publius and his officers killed themselves. Only 500 Gauls were taken alive."],["Crassus orders a general advance","Crassus did not know his son's fate but saw that Publius was in danger, and ordered a general advance. He was then shown his son's head on a spear."],["The Parthians attack until nightfall","The horse archers began to surround the Roman infantry and shot at them from all directions. The cataphracts mounted a series of charges that disorganised the Romans, and the onslaught went on until nightfall."],["The retreat towards Carrhae","Crassus ordered a disorganised, ragged retreat to the town of Carrhae. He left behind 4,000 wounded, who were killed by the Parthians the next morning."],["Four cohorts are lost on a hill","Four Roman cohorts lost their way in the dark and were surrounded on a hill by the Parthians. Only 20 Romans survived."]]],
  "Battle of Crécy": ["battle-of-crecy","d31e45783b",[["The English hold the hillside","Edward's army stood in three battles on a slope, with pits dug in front. French scouts came in sight of them from Abbeville, and the French column followed with the Genoese crossbowmen in front."],["The archery duel","The Genoese went forward and shot at the English longbowmen. The longbowmen outranged them and shot more than three times as fast, and the crossbowmen had no pavises, which were still with the French baggage."],["The Genoese flee through their own cavalry","The Italians were quickly defeated and fled. Knights in Alençon's division, hampered by the routed mercenaries, hacked at them as they retreated, and the clash threw the leading battle into disarray."],["Alençon's charge uphill","Alençon's battle charged, disordered by its hurried start, the fleeing Italians, the mud, the slope and the pits. Archers shot the unarmoured horses, and the charge had lost much of its force when it reached the English men-at-arms."],["The first charge is beaten off","The English men-at-arms and spearmen received a charge that had lost much of its force, and the hand-to-hand fighting that followed was fierce. Alençon was among those killed, and the French attack was beaten off."],["Fresh French cavalry repeat the charge","A fresh body of French cavalry moved up to the foot of the hill and charged over ground littered with dead and wounded men and horses. They were too disordered to break into the English formation, and a long mêlée followed."],["Edward sends a detachment from the reserve","With the Prince of Wales hard pressed, Edward sent forward a detachment from his reserve battle to rescue the situation, and the French were repulsed again."],["The charges go on into the night","The French repeated their charges late into the night, each ending in fierce fighting and a French retreat. The English ranks were thinned, but men from the rear stepped forward to fill the gaps."],["Philip leaves the field","Philip was caught up in the fighting, had two horses killed under him and was hit by an arrow in the jaw. He left the field, though it is unclear why, and near midnight most of the French army melted away."]]],
  "Battle of Dien Bien Phu": ["battle-of-dien-bien-phu","296e342ec9",[["The French fortress and the Viet Minh ring","The French held a central headquarters with strong points round it, and Isabelle to the south. The Viet Minh moved divisions and artillery into the hills round the valley and the French were completely surrounded."],["Béatrice is overrun","The Viet Minh opened with a heavy bombardment of Béatrice, the northeastern outpost. The 312th Division then attacked, and by the early hours of 14 March the position had been taken."],["Gabrielle falls","The 308th Division attacked Gabrielle from 20:00 on the night of 14 March after a heavy barrage. A French counterattack failed to relieve it, and the Algerian battalion fell back at 08:00 the next day."],["Anne-Marie is given up","The fall of Béatrice and Gabrielle had demoralised the Tai troops. On the morning of 17 March, under cover of fog, most of them left or defected, and the French and the few Tais who remained withdrew from Anne-Marie."],["Dominique 1 and 2 are captured","At 19:00 the 312th Division captured Dominique 1 and 2, outflanking all positions east of the river. French howitzers firing at zero elevation and anti-aircraft machine guns near the airfield then forced the attackers to retreat."],["Eliane 1 falls and Huguette 7 holds","The 316th Division captured Eliane 1 and half of Eliane 2 by midnight. In the west the 308th Division attacked Huguette 7 and nearly broke through, but a French sergeant took charge of the defenders and sealed the breach."],["The French counterattack in the east","The French retook Eliane 2 just after midnight. On the afternoon of 1 April a counterattack by everybody left who could be trusted to fight retook Dominique 2 and Eliane 1."],["The Viet Minh renew the assault","The Viet Minh assaulted again and the exhausted French, who had no reserves, fell back from both positions late in the afternoon. A column sent north from Isabelle was attacked on the way and fell back to Isabelle."],["Eliane 2 holds and Huguette 7 is retaken","The 316th Division attacked Eliane 2 and a few French tanks arrived from the central garrison and helped push it back. The Viet Minh briefly captured Huguette 7, then were pushed back by a French counterattack at dawn on 1 April."],["Sapping, and Eliane 1 retaken","After heavy losses in the open on 5 April, Giáp turned to entrenchment and sapping. On 10 April the French retook Eliane 1 after it changed hands several times, and a Viet Minh attempt to regain it on 12 April was pushed back."],["Huguette 1 is overrun","Viet Minh trenches almost surrounded Huguette 1 and 6. French sorties to supply Huguette 6 cost heavy casualties, the defenders of Huguette 6 broke out on 18 April, and Huguette 1 was overrun on 22 April."],["Eliane 1, Dominique 3 and Huguette 5 fall","The Viet Minh attacked the exhausted defenders in mass on the night of 1 May and overran Eliane 1, Dominique 3 and Huguette 5. The French beat back the attacks on Eliane 2."],["Eliane 2 is mined and overrun","The Viet Minh attacked Eliane 2 with Katyusha rockets and French artillery broke the first wave. Later that night a mine under Eliane 2 was detonated and within a few hours the defenders were overrun."],["The last attack on the headquarters","Giáp ordered an all-out attack on the remaining French units. By evening enemy troops stood outside the headquarters bunker and the last radio report said the positions had been overrun."]]],
  "Battle of Gaugamela": ["battle-of-gaugamela","d81a913771",[["The two armies stand drawn up","The Persians were already on the field. Darius stood in the centre, Bessus commanded the left wing and Mazaeus the right, with chariots in front. Alexander held the right of the Macedonians and Parmenion the left, with the phalanx between them in a double line."],["The phalanx advances and Alexander rides to the right","Alexander ordered the infantry to march towards the centre of the enemy line, with the wings echeloned back. He himself rode towards the edge of the right flank with the Companions, to draw the Persian cavalry outwards."],["The Persian wings attack","The Scythian cavalry of the Persian left tried to get round Alexander's extreme right, and a long cavalry fight followed there. Darius also sent a large part of his cavalry against Parmenion on the left."],["The chariots charge","Darius launched his chariots at the troops under Alexander. Javelin-throwers stopped many, and the Macedonian ranks opened to let the rest pass harmlessly. Those that got through were then killed."],["Alexander's wedge smashes into the Persian centre","Alexander drew his Companions out of the fight and formed a large wedge with other units. He led it into the weakened Persian centre while the phalanx kept the Persian infantry engaged, and it took out Darius' royal guard and the Greek mercenaries."],["Darius turns and flees","Darius was in danger of being cut off. By the widely held modern view he now broke and ran, with the rest of his army following him."],["Parmenion is hard pressed and Alexander turns back","Parmenion's wing was apparently encircled by the Persian right and sent desperate messages. Alexander could have pursued Darius but turned back to help Parmenion instead."],["A gap opens and raiders ride for the camp","A gap had opened between the left and centre of the phalanx. The Persian and Indian cavalry broke through it and rode on towards the Macedonian camp to loot."],["The reserve phalanx disperses the raiders","The rear reserve phalanx attacked the raiders as they were looting and dispersed them. They had not taken the phalanx or Parmenion in the rear."],["Alexander fights the Persian right and Mazaeus pulls back","Alexander and his Companions met the cavalry of the Persian right in what was the fiercest fighting of the battle. Alexander prevailed, and Mazaeus began to pull his forces back, as Bessus had on the other wing."],["The Persian right falls into disorder","Unlike the Persians on the left with Bessus, those on the right soon fell into disorder as the Thessalians and other cavalry units charged forward at their fleeing enemy."]]],
  "Battle of Gettysburg": ["battle-of-gettysburg","00f6f0c7c0",[["Heth's brigades meet Buford's cavalry west of town","Buford's cavalry held the ridges west of the town to delay the Confederates. Two of Heth's brigades, part of Hill's corps, came east along the Chambersburg Pike and met his dismounted troopers."],["The I Corps arrives as the cavalry falls back","The Confederates pushed the Union cavalry east to McPherson Ridge. The vanguard of the I Corps then arrived and took up the fight beside it."],["Davis and Archer are repelled on McPherson Ridge","North of the pike Davis won a brief success against Cutler's brigade but was repelled with heavy losses near an unfinished railroad cut. South of it Archer's brigade attacked through Herbst Woods and the Iron Brigade captured him."],["Pettigrew and Pender drive the I Corps back","Heth's whole division engaged, with Pettigrew's brigade flanking the Union line, and Pender's division was added to the assault. The I Corps was driven back through the Seminary grounds and the streets of Gettysburg."],["Ewell's corps and the XI Corps come up from the north","Two divisions of Ewell's corps came down on the town from the north while the XI Corps hurried up. Rodes's and Early's divisions then assaulted and outflanked the I and XI Corps north and northwest of the town."],["The Union line falls back to Cemetery Hill","Howard ordered a retreat to Cemetery Hill, and the I Corps was driven back through the Seminary grounds and the streets of Gettysburg."],["The Union line forms a fishhook","Most of the Union infantry reached the field. The XII Corps stood on Culp's Hill, the remnants of the I and XI Corps on Cemetery Hill and the II Corps on Cemetery Ridge. Longstreet's corps came up to attack the Union left."],["Sickles's corps meets Longstreet's attack","Sickles had advanced the III Corps west to the Emmitsburg Road, away from Cemetery Ridge. Longstreet's divisions came forward and found it directly in their path."],["Hood attacks Devil's Den and Little Round Top","Meade sent in the V Corps among other reinforcements. Hood's division moved further east than intended and attacked Devil's Den and Little Round Top."],["McLaws overwhelms the III Corps at the Peach Orchard","McLaws, coming in on Hood's left, drove several attacks into the thinly stretched III Corps in the Wheatfield and overwhelmed it in the Peach Orchard. The III Corps was virtually destroyed as a combat unit."],["The Pennsylvania Reserves beat McLaws back","McLaws's attack reached Plum Run Valley before the Pennsylvania Reserves division of the V Corps, coming down from Little Round Top, beat it back."],["Pickett's Charge crosses the open ground","After a long bombardment, Pickett's division and brigades from Hill's corps stepped off the ridge and advanced to Cemetery Ridge. To the northeast Stuart's cavalry met Gregg's division and Custer's brigade, who stopped it reaching the rear of the Union infantry."],["The charge is repelled at the Angle","The Union line wavered and broke for a time at the Angle, but reinforcements rushed into the breach and the Confederate attack was repelled. Nearly half of the attackers did not return to their own lines."]]],
  "Battle of Grunwald": ["battle-of-grunwald","543ae4d313",[["The two armies form opposing lines","The Polish-Lithuanian army stood with Polish heavy cavalry on its left, Lithuanian light cavalry on its right and mercenaries in the centre. The Order set its heavy cavalry against the Lithuanians and waited to be attacked."],["Vytautas attacks the Order's left flank","The Order had hoped the other side would attack first. Vytautas, supported by the Polish banners, went in against the Order's left flank, and the Lithuanian cavalry fought more than an hour."],["The Lithuanian light cavalry retreats","After more than an hour of heavy fighting the Lithuanian light cavalry began a full retreat from the left flank of the Teutonic forces."],["Heavy fighting breaks out between Poles and the Order","While the Lithuanians were retreating, Kuno von Lichtenstein's force fell on the Polish side. Six of Wallenrode's banners did not pursue the Lithuanians but joined the attack, and the royal banner of Kraków was lost and soon recaptured."],["The king deploys his second line","Władysław II Jagiełło deployed his reserves, the second line of his army, against the Teutonic force that was pressing the Polish side."],["The Grand Master's 16 banners and the last reserves","Von Jungingen led 16 banners, almost a third of the original Teutonic strength, to the Polish side, and the king committed his third line. The fighting reached the Polish command."],["The Lithuanians return and strike from behind","The reorganised Lithuanians came back and attacked von Jungingen from the rear. The Order was by then outnumbered by the mass of Polish knights and the advancing Lithuanian cavalry."],["Von Jungingen is killed","Von Jungingen tried to break through the Lithuanian lines and was killed. By one account a lance through the neck killed him."],["The leaderless Order retreats","Surrounded and leaderless, the Order began to retreat. Part of the routed units made for their camp. The Order had by then been outnumbered by the Polish knights and the Lithuanian cavalry."],["The camp is broken","The knights tried to make a wagon fort of the camp, but the camp followers turned against them and joined the pursuit. The defence was soon broken and the camp ravaged; by one account more knights died there than on the field."]]],
  "Battle of Guandu": ["battle-of-guandu","aaaa678e1c",[["The armies face each other","Yuan Shao's army came to Yangwu, directly north of Guandu, and began building earthen fortifications. Cao Cao's troops held Guandu, where they had prepared fortifications."],["Yuan Shao advances into trench warfare","Yuan Shao's army moved slowly south from Yangwu and fought Cao Cao's men in trench warfare, from behind earthen embankments that both sides made around their positions."],["Siege engines and tunnels at Guandu","Yuan Shao raised siege ramps and high platforms to shoot arrows onto Cao Cao's men, who answered with traction trebuchets that destroyed the platforms. A ditch dug within Cao Cao's lines blocked Yuan Shao's tunnels, and neither side overcame the other."],["Cavalry raid the grain depot at Gushi","Cao Cao sent small cavalry units under Xu Huang and Shi Huan against the depot at Gushi, where Han Meng guarded Yuan Shao's grain. Han Meng was routed and the grain carts were burnt."],["Supplies reach Wuchao and Cao Cao sets out","Chunyu Qiong brought an army of 10,000 with large food reserves to Wuchao. That night Cao Cao and Yue Jin took 5,000 infantry and cavalry from Guandu to attack it, leaving Cao Hong and Xun You in charge of the main camp."],["Wuchao's defences are overrun and its forts burn","Cao Cao's army, disguised as a reinforcement unit from Yuan Shao, attacked Wuchao. Chunyu Qiong's first defences were overrun, and he fell back to his forts, which Cao Cao attacked and set on fire."],["Yuan Shao sends his main army against Guandu","Zhang He urged help for Wuchao, but Guo Tu urged an attack on Guandu. Yuan Shao sent Zhang He and Gao Lan with his main army against Cao Cao's camp, and only a small cavalry unit to Wuchao. Cao Cao held his men at Guandu."],["Wuchao is taken and its supplies burn","The raid on Wuchao inflicted over a thousand casualties. Yue Jin captured Chunyu Qiong, and almost all of Yuan Shao's food supplies there were burnt."],["Zhang He and Gao Lan surrender at Guandu","Zhang He and Gao Lan failed to break through Cao Cao's lines at Guandu. After the news from Wuchao they surrendered to Cao Hong and destroyed their weapons."],["Cao Cao attacks Yuan Shao's army","Cao Cao's forces seized the opportunity and launched a full attack on Yuan Shao's army, whose men were already shaken by the loss of their supplies."],["Yuan Shao flees north across the river","Yuan Shao's army was destroyed and much of its supplies were captured. Yuan Shao fled north across the Yellow River with about 800 cavalry, which was what was left of his army."]]],
  "Battle of Hastings": ["battle-of-hastings","3de509be9e",[["The two armies take their ground","The English formed a shield wall at the top of the slope. William drew up archers in front, spearmen behind them in three divisions, and kept his cavalry in reserve."],["The Norman archers shoot uphill","The battle opened with the Norman archers shooting uphill at the English shield wall. The slope turned their arrows aside or sent them over the top of the hill, and they had little effect."],["The spearmen go forward","William sent the spearmen forward to attack the English. They met a barrage of spears, axes and stones and could not force an opening in the shield wall."],["The cavalry advance in support","The Norman cavalry came up to support the infantry. They too failed to make any headway against the shield wall."],["A general retreat begins","The Normans fell back in a general retreat, blamed on the Breton division on William's left. A rumour that William had been killed added to the confusion."],["Some of the English pursue","Some of the English left the wall to pursue the fleeing invaders. William rode through his forces, showing his face and shouting that he was still alive."],["William counter-attacks","William rode through his forces, showing his face and shouting that he was still alive. He led a counter-attack against the pursuing English, and the men who had left the wall were overwhelmed."],["The cavalry feign flight","After a probable lull William tried a new tactic: cavalry sent against the shield wall, then drawn back to pull the English into further pursuits. It was said to have been used twice."],["The assault on a thinned shield wall","The feigned flights did not break the line but probably thinned out the housecarls, who were replaced by men of the fyrd, and the shield wall held. Archers were used again before and during an assault by cavalry and infantry led by William."],["Harold dies and the English collapse","Harold died late in the battle, though the accounts of how he died contradict each other. His death left the English leaderless and they began to collapse, and the Normans pursued the fleeing troops."],["A last stand at the Malfosse","Except for a rearguard action at the Malfosse, the battle was over. There some Englishmen rallied and seriously wounded Eustace of Boulogne before the Normans defeated them."]]],
  "Battle of Hattin": ["battle-of-hattin","7288904001",[["The Crusaders camp on the dry plateau","The Frankish army could not reach Tiberias by nightfall and turned towards the springs of Kafr Hattin. The Muslims placed themselves between the Franks and the water, so the Franks camped on the arid plateau, closely surrounded."],["Archers shoot into the smoke-blinded camp","Smoke from the fires set by Saladin's forces blinded the Crusaders. Gökböri's mounted archers, resupplied with 400 loads of arrows during the night, shot at the Franks."],["The army breaks camp and is blocked","Amalric formed the battle lines, and the thirsty Crusaders broke camp and changed direction for the springs of Hattin. Saladin's army attacked their ragged approach and blocked the route forward and any retreat."],["Raymond charges twice towards the lake","Count Raymond charged twice to break through to the water at Lake Tiberias. The second charge took him to the lake and he made his way to Tyre."],["The infantry flee to the Horns of Hattin","After Raymond escaped, Guy's position was even more desperate. Most of the Christian infantry fled onto the Horns of Hattin and played no further part in the battle."],["Guy tries to pitch the tents again","Guy attempted to pitch the tents again to block the Muslim cavalry. The knights and mounted serjeants were disorganised but still fought on."],["The Franks charge Saladin's position","The Franks on the hill made a formidable charge on the Muslims facing them and drove them back to Saladin, who was overcome by grief."],["The Muslims rally and drive the Franks up the hill","Saladin rallied his men. The Muslims returned to the fight and climbed the hill, and the Franks withdrew before them."],["The Franks charge a second time","The Franks rallied and charged again as they had the first time, and once more drove the Muslims back to Saladin, who acted as he had on the first occasion."],["The Muslims drive them back and Guy's tent falls","The Muslims turned on the Franks and drove them back to the hill. The Crusaders, surrounded and after three charges, were broken up and defeated, and then Guy's tent fell."]]],
  "Battle of Isandlwana": ["battle-of-isandlwana","f0872cf4f8",[["The Zulu army is found near the camp","Scouts chased some Zulus into a valley and saw most of the main force sitting in total quiet. Durnford had reached the camp with his men and a rocket battery, and Pulleine held the camp with the 24th and two guns."],["The Zulu advance and the British extend their line","Having been discovered, the Zulu leapt to the offensive in the horns and chest of the buffalo. Pulleine sent all six companies of the 24th out into an extended firing line, and Durnford's men moved forward."],["Durnford falls back to a donga","Durnford's men met part of the Zulu centre, retreated to a donga on the British right flank and formed a defensive line there. The rocket battery fell behind, fired three rockets and was overrun."],["Pulleine draws the firing line closer to the camp","After about twenty minutes of firing, Pulleine made his one change to the original dispositions. He brought the companies in the firing line slightly closer to the camp."],["Volleys pin the centre while the left horn swings out","For an hour or so the British volleys and the artillery held the Zulu centre. Meanwhile the left horn moved to outflank and envelop the British right."],["Durnford's men withdraw and G Company is overrun","Durnford's men, who had fought the longest, began to withdraw and their rate of fire fell. Their retreat exposed the flank of G Company, which the Zulu left horn overran relatively quickly."],["The 24th falls back to the camp","Durnford's withdrawal exposed the right flank of the regulars and the Zulu threatened to encircle them, so Pulleine ordered a withdrawal to the camp. The men of the 24th fell back fighting, in order and with discipline."],["The Zulu close in on the camp","The Zulu pressed into the camp and fought hand to hand. Evidence shows large groups of bodies around the camp, and the slaughter was complete in the area around it."]]],
  "Battle of Issus": ["battle-of-issus","191e80e95a",[["The two armies draw up on either side of the river","Alexander placed the Companions on his right and the Thessalian cavalry on the left of the phalanx under Parmenion. Darius set his heavy cavalry by the coast, the Greek mercenaries next, and the Cardaces along the river and into the foothills."],["The Persian cavalry charges across the river","The Persian horse crossed the river and charged Parmenion and the allied cavalry. The Thessalian cavalry faced a larger mass of heavy horse and charged and fell back by turns to buy time."],["The Macedonian centre is thrown back","The centre phalanx advanced across the river and up the fortified bank and suffered badly against the mercenaries. The Macedonians were forced to retreat across the river."],["The right wing breaks into the Persian line","Alexander led the hypaspists on foot against the Cardaces and punched a hole through the Persian line. The Agrianians drove back the skirmishers on his far right, which secured the flank of the Companions."],["Alexander's cavalry attacks Darius, who flees","Alexander mounted a horse at the head of the Companions and led a direct assault on Darius and his bodyguards. They fled from the battlefield."],["The Companions strike the mercenaries from behind","Alexander saw his left flank and centre in trouble and let Darius go. He crashed into the rear of the Greek mercenaries, who broke up and began to retreat."],["The Persians abandon their positions","The Persians saw that the Great King had gone and that the battle was being lost, and they fled in full rout. The Hellenic cavalry pursued them for as long as there was light."]]],
  "Battle of Iwo Jima": ["battle-of-iwo-jima","1f62507a27",[["The Marines land on the southeastern coast","The first wave of Marines landed on the southeastern beaches, the 5th Marine Division in the southern sector and the 4th in the northern. The beach soon became congested with men and materiel on the loose volcanic ash."],["The Japanese open fire on the crowded beach","The Japanese held their fire for just over an hour while the Americans crowded the beach, then opened fire from their fortified positions on the landing force."],["The 28th Marines cross the island; the 25th hit the Quarry","On the left the 28th Marines drove across the island at its narrowest point and cut off the Japanese on Mount Suribachi. On the right the 25th Marines attacked the Quarry in two prongs against heavy fire."],["Mount Suribachi is cut off and the flag goes up","The 28th Marines turned against the mountain. A patrol from E Company, 2nd Battalion, climbed the volcano and raised a flag at the top. Most of the Japanese there stayed in the tunnels and were generally killed."],["The 9th Marines attack at night towards Hill 362","In the north the fighting bogged down against positions the Japanese reoccupied through their tunnels. Erskine sent the 9th Marines forward in the dark, without a barrage, which helped take Hill 362."],["The Japanese counterattack for Hill 362","The local Japanese commander, against Kuribayashi's order, led a banzai charge of about 1,000 men to retake the hill. The Marines counted 784 Japanese dead the next day."],["The 3rd Marine Division reaches the northern coast","On the same day elements of the 3rd Marine Division reached the northern coast and split the Japanese defence in two. The plan stops drawing Inouye's force after its charge."],["The 5th Marine Division faces the northern gorge","The 5th Marine Division still faced the main stronghold in a gorge at the northwestern end of the island. On 21 March the Marines destroyed the command post there with explosives, and on 24 March they sealed the remaining caves at the northern tip."],["The last Japanese counterattack","After the island was declared secure, a 300-man Japanese force attacked near Airfield No. 2. Army pilots, Seabees and Marines fought it for up to 90 minutes. The island was officially declared secure at 09:00 on 26 March."]]],
  "Battle of Jena–Auerstedt": ["battle-of-jena-auerstedt","a2f116ab6a",[["Gudin deploys at Hassenhausen","Gudin's Division was stopped outside Poppel by Prussian cavalry and artillery. Davout ordered it to deploy at Hassenhausen, while Schmettau's division and Blücher's cavalry formed to attack."],["Schmettau and Blücher push Gudin back","Schmettau's troops and Blücher's cavalry attacked together. They pushed Gudin's troops back to the village of Hassenhausen."],["Wartensleben arrives with the Duke of Brunswick","Wartensleben arrived with the Duke of Brunswick, who ordered his infantry to the Prussian left flank and his cavalry to the right."],["The French cavalry take their place","The rest of the French cavalry arrived at 9 am and was placed on Gudin's left, where it waited behind the hill, out of the Prussians' sight."],["Friant's Division arrives on Gudin's right","Friant's Division and the 12-pound artillery arrived at 9:30 am and moved up in squares on Gudin's right, so that the French line now reached out on both sides of the village."],["Friant's squares advance and Hassenhausen is attacked","The advance of the French squares forced Blücher's cavalry back, and Blücher ordered his cavalry to attack. At that moment two of Wartensleben's regiments attacked Hassenhausen."],["The French cavalry strike from behind the hill","Davout hid his chasseurs à cheval behind a hill and released them at a crucial moment, putting Blücher's cavalry to flight. Three Prussian cavalry regiments were routed and the infantry fell back."],["Brunswick orders a full assault on Hassenhausen","With the cavalry routed and the infantry falling back, the Duke of Brunswick ordered a full assault on Hassenhausen. Schmettau's Division and Wartensleben's troops pressed on Gudin's line."],["Orange and Morand arrive","With Brunswick and Schmettau wounded, the Prussian command had broken down. The Prince of Orange arrived and the King split his command between the flanks, while Morand's Division was sent to secure Gudin's left."],["The French counter-attack","Davout could see that the Prussians were wavering. He ordered his infantry to counter-attack, and Gudin's Division went forward against Schmettau's line."],["The Prussian centre breaks and the King orders a withdrawal","Schmettau's centre was broken and forced back over the Lissbach Stream. Blücher's cavalry was blown, Wartensleben was trying to reposition his troops, and the King ordered a withdrawal."]]],
  "Battle of Jutland": ["battle-of-jutland","8cd45d1104",[["The fleets sail towards each other unseen","Beatty's ships ran east while Hipper's battlecruisers ran north, with Scheer's battle line following Hipper. Jellicoe's fleet was still out of sight to the north-west, and the Germans did not know it was at sea."],["The Run to the South","Hipper turned south-east to lead Beatty towards Scheer and opened fire with the British to the south-west of him. The British battlecruisers followed. The German ships scored hits on three of the six British battlecruisers before the British scored their first."],["The 5th Battle Squadron comes into range","Barham, leading the 5th Battle Squadron, caught up with Hipper at 16:08 and opened fire, and by 16:15 all four battleships were engaged. Hipper now faced them astern as well as Beatty's battlecruisers. Hood's squadron had been sent ahead from Jellicoe at about 16:05."],["Beatty turns north and the Germans follow","Beatty sighted the vanguard of Scheer's battle line and turned his battlecruisers through 180° to draw the Germans towards Jellicoe. The 5th Battle Squadron, too far behind to read his flags, passed them on an opposing course, heading for the German main body."],["The 5th Battle Squadron acts as rearguard","Scheer's leading battleships opened fire at 16:48. The 5th Battle Squadron turned north and drew fire from Hipper's battlecruisers and Scheer's leading battleships, while Beatty eased his own ships out of range by 17:10."],["Hood meets the German scouting ships","Hood's battlecruisers came up and relieved the scout cruiser Chester, which had been pounded by Boedicker's four light cruisers. They disabled the light cruiser Wiesbaden, and Boedicker's other ships turned towards Hipper and Scheer."],["Jellicoe deploys to the east","Hipper turned back towards Scheer at about 18:00, and Boedicker's ships fell back on him. Jellicoe, needing to turn his six columns into a single battle line, ordered deployment to the east at 18:15; it would take twenty minutes."],["Windy Corner: Defence is lost","Beatty's battlecruisers crossed the front of the British battleships to join Hood. Arbuthnot's flagship Defence charged across his bows and ran into the guns of Hipper's and Scheer's oncoming ships, which blew her up with all hands."],["Invincible blows up and Lützow is crippled","Hipper came within range of Hood's squadron and Beatty's ships, and the German flagship Lützow was hit many times. At 18:30 Invincible appeared as a clear target and was sunk in 90 seconds by Lützow and Derfflinger; Hood was killed."],["Scheer's T is crossed and he turns away","The leading German battleships came out of the mist facing the whole British line, which crossed their T. Scheer ordered a turn together through 180°, and the German fleet disengaged under smoke and mist."],["Scheer doubles back to the east","Jellicoe did not chase directly but headed south, keeping the High Seas Fleet west of him. Scheer, judging it not yet dark enough to escape, doubled back to the east, which took his ships again towards Jellicoe's deployed line."],["The T is crossed again and the battlecruisers charge","By 19:15 Jellicoe had crossed Scheer's T again. Scheer turned west a second time and sent his torpedo boats and the four remaining battlecruisers of Hipper's group at the British to cover the turn, under heavy fire."],["The last exchanges after sunset","Just after sunset the surviving British battlecruisers caught up with the German battlecruisers, which were briefly relieved by the older pre-dreadnoughts. These were the last major exchanges between capital ships in the battle and in the war."],["Scheer slips round the British rear in the night","Jellicoe headed south with a screen of cruisers and destroyers behind him to guard Scheer's expected escape route. Scheer instead crossed the British wake and made for Horns Reef, passing through the screen."]]],
  "Battle of Kadesh": ["battle-of-kadesh","1ea7fd6bed",[["The Hittites wait behind Kadesh","Muwatalli had placed his troops behind Old Kadesh. Ramesses had been told that the Hittite army was far off, so he was with the Amun division and his bodyguard while the Re division was still coming up to the camp."],["Hittite chariots cross the river and scatter the Re division","The Hittite chariots crossed the river and charged the middle of the Re division as it marched towards Ramesses. Caught in the open, the division scattered in all directions."],["The chariots attack the camp","The Hittite chariotry rounded to the north and attacked the Egyptian camp through the Amun shield wall, and panic spread among the Amun division. Some of the Re division fled north to the camp, pursued by Hittite chariots."],["Ramesses charges into the Hittite ranks","The momentum of the Hittite attack waned as chariots were impeded by obstacles in the camp. Ramesses defeated the first attackers and personally led several charges with his guard, some Amun chariots and survivors of the Re division."],["The Egyptian counter-attack drives them to the river","The Hittites, believing their enemies routed, stopped to plunder the camp. An Egyptian counter-attack then drove them back towards the Orontes and away from the camp, and in the pursuit lighter Egyptian chariots overtook Hittite chariots."],["Muwatalli sends another thousand chariots","As the retreat reached the river, Muwatalli, who still held a large reserve of chariots and infantry and the walls of Kadesh, ordered another thousand chariots to counter-attack. High nobles close to the king led them."],["The Ne'arin surprise the Hittites at the camp","As the Hittite force approached the same Egyptian camp again, the Ne'arin from Amurru arrived without warning and surprised them."],["The Ptah division arrives from the south","Finally the Ptah division arrived from the south. It came up behind the Hittite force that was pressing on the camp and threatened its rear."],["The Hittites are pinned against the Orontes","After six unsuccessful charges the Hittite forces were almost surrounded and the survivors were pinned against the Orontes. They abandoned their chariots and tried to swim the river, and many drowned. The Hittites held on to Kadesh."]]],
  "Battle of Karbala": ["battle-of-karbala","c94ea51c68",[["Both sides take up positions","After the morning prayer both sides formed up. Husayn put Zuhayr ibn al-Qayn on his right and Habib ibn Muzahir on his left, with the tents tied together and the ditch behind them. The Kufan wings stood opposite, with 500 horsemen on the route to the river."],["Volleys of arrows and duels","Zuhayr ibn al-Qayn tried to dissuade the Kufan soldiers from killing Husayn, but failed. The Kufans fired several volleys of arrows, and in the duels that followed several of Husayn's companions were slain."],["The Kufan right wing attacks and is repulsed","The right wing of the Kufans, under Amr ibn al-Hajjaj, attacked Husayn's force and was repulsed. Hand-to-hand fighting then paused and further volleys of arrows were exchanged."],["Shimr attacks and is repulsed","Shimr, who commanded the left wing of the Umayyad army, launched an attack. There were losses on both sides, and he was repulsed."],["Cavalry attacks and Ibn Sa'd brings in reinforcements","Cavalry attacks followed, and Husayn's cavalry resisted fiercely. Ibn Sa'd brought in armoured cavalry and five hundred archers."],["Husayn's cavalrymen dismount","Arrows wounded the horses of Husayn's cavalrymen, who dismounted and fought on foot. The Kufan archers kept shooting at both of Husayn's flanks."],["The tents are burned","Because the Kufans could approach only from the front, Ibn Sa'd ordered the tents burned, except the one Husayn and his family used. The flames hindered the Umayyad advance for a while."],["Husayn's companions are encircled","After noon prayers Husayn's companions were encircled, and almost all of them were killed. The Kufan wings closed on the two flanks from north and south."],["Husayn's relatives join the fighting","Husayn's relatives, who had not fought until then, joined the battle. His son Ali al-Akbar was killed, then his half-brothers, including Abbas, and sons of Aqil ibn Abi Talib, Ja'far ibn Abi Talib and Hasan ibn Ali."],["Husayn goes towards the river","The Umayyad soldiers hesitated to attack Husayn directly, but he was struck in the mouth by an arrow as he went to the river to drink. Later he was surrounded and struck on the head by Malik ibn Nusayr."],["Shimr advances on Husayn","Few people were left on Husayn's side. Shimr advanced towards him with a group of foot soldiers, and a young boy from Husayn's camp tried to defend him from a sword stroke."],["Husayn is killed","Shimr urged the Kufan soldiers on, and they rushed at Husayn and wounded him on his hand and shoulder. He fell face-down and was then stabbed and decapitated by Sinan ibn Anas."]]],
  "Battle of Kohima": ["battle-of-kohima","5501dba045",[["The garrison is penned in on Garrison Hill","The Japanese took GPT Ridge on the first day and pressed the garrison from the south and from the north, around the tennis court. The 161st Brigade, cut off at Jotsoma, could only support it with artillery."],["The Japanese cut the garrison in two","The Japanese took the area of the Deputy Commissioner's bungalow and Kuki Picquet, cutting the garrison in two. They did not go on to attack Garrison Hill, being exhausted by hunger and the fighting."],["The relief force breaks through","The 161st Brigade, with air, artillery and armour support, attacked towards Kohima from Jotsoma. After a day of heavy fighting its leading troops and tanks broke through and began to relieve the garrison."],["The 6th Brigade takes over Garrison Hill","The 6th Brigade replaced the original garrison, and Colonel Richards handed over command at 06:00 on 20 April. The Japanese went on trying to capture Garrison Hill for several more nights."],["The Japanese defend; two brigades try to outflank them","The Japanese held Kohima Ridge with the Left Force and Naga Village with the Centre Force. The 5th Brigade took a foothold in Naga Village and lost it to a counter-attack, while the 4th Brigade marched round Mount Pulebadze and took part of GPT Ridge by surprise."],["Jail Hill and Kuki Picquet are captured","Both outflanking moves failed, and the 2nd Division attacked along Kohima Ridge itself. After a barrage of smoke shells blinded the Japanese machine-gunners, Jail Hill and Kuki Picquet were captured on 11 May."],["The 7th Indian Division turns on Naga Village","The 7th Indian Division arrived in stages from the Arakan, and with the 161st Brigade under its command it concentrated on recapturing Naga Village from the north."],["A tank clears the tennis court","After several failed attempts to outflank or storm the position, a track was bulldozed to the summit above it. A Lee tank was dragged up and crashed down onto the tennis court, destroying the Japanese trenches and bunkers, and the ridge was cleared."],["The attacks on Naga Village and Aradura Spur fail","The Japanese kept defending Naga Village and Aradura Spur. Attacks on 16 May and on Naga Hill on 24/25 May gained no ground, and an attack on both ends of Aradura Spur on 28/29 May was repulsed."],["Satō abandons Naga Village","Satō left Naga Village and the other positions north of the road on 31 May, against the orders of Mutaguchi to hold on. This let XXXIII Corps outflank Miyazaki's position on Aradura Spur."],["Miyazaki is driven off the road","Miyazaki's detachment fought rearguard actions and demolished bridges along the road to Imphal, but was eventually driven off the road and forced to retreat eastwards."],["The two relief forces meet at Milestone 109","The 2nd Division followed the retreat down the main road. On 22 June its leading troops met the 5th Indian Division, advancing north from Imphal, at Milestone 109, which ended the siege of Imphal."]]],
  "Battle of Kosovo": ["battle-of-kosovo","acf93f79d0",[["The armies draw up on Kosovo Field","Lazar held the Serbian centre, Branković the right and Vuković the left, with heavy cavalry in front. Murad stood behind his Janissaries in the centre, Bayezid held the Ottoman right, Yakub the left, and archers stood on both wings."],["Ottoman archers shoot at the Serbian cavalry","The fighting began with Ottoman archers shooting at the Serbian cavalry, who then made ready to attack. The archers had been posted on the two wings of the Ottoman line."],["The Serbian cavalry breaks the Ottoman left","After forming a wedge, the Serbian cavalry charged and broke through the Ottoman left wing under Yakub. It was less successful against the centre and the right wing."],["Ottoman light troops counterattack","When the knights' charge was spent, light Ottoman cavalry and light infantry counterattacked, and the Serbian heavy armour became a disadvantage."],["The Serbian centre pushes back the Ottomans","In the centre the Serbian troops pushed the Ottomans back. Bayezid's wing barely held off the Bosnians under Vuković, who inflicted heavy losses on the Ottomans."],["Bayezid's counterattack pushes the Serbs back","The Ottomans, in a ferocious counterattack led by Bayezid, pushed the Serbian forces back. The Janissaries came forward again in the centre."],["The Serbian infantry is routed","Later in the day the Ottomans prevailed and routed the Serbian infantry, which broke and fell back from the centre of the field."],["The Bosnians drift towards the centre","Both flanks still held. Vuković's Bosnian troops drifted toward the centre to make up for the heavy losses of the Serbian infantry."],["Branković withdraws","Branković saw that there was no hope of victory and withdrew from the field with as many of his men as he could save."],["The remaining forces yield the field","Some time after Branković's retreat the remaining Bosnian and Serbian forces gave up the field, believing that victory was no longer possible."]]],
  "Battle of Kursk": ["battle-of-kursk","e605527755",[["The two armies take up their ground","The Germans planned two pincers on the Kursk salient, the 9th Army from the north and the 4th Panzer Army with Army Detachment Kempf from the south, while the 2nd Army held the west. The Central and Voronezh Fronts stood in fortified belts, with the Steppe Front in reserve."],["The 9th Army attacks in the north","The 9th Army opened its attack on the Central Front at 05:30 after a preliminary bombardment. It broke into the first belt of the Soviet defences and reached the small town of Ponyri in the second belt on the first day."],["The southern pincer attacks","In the south the 4th Panzer Army and Army Detachment Kempf attacked the Voronezh Front after a bombardment. By the end of the day a wedge had been created in the first belt of the Soviet defences."],["The Central Front counterattacks and holds","The Soviet 16th Tank Corps counterattacked and was forced back by Tiger tanks. The Germans then attacked at Olkhovatka and Ponyri, but the Central Front held both places."],["The northern attack is halted at Ponyri","Model put the weight of the 9th Army on Ponyri and Olkhovatka, and control of Ponyri changed hands several times. By 10 July the Soviets had completely halted the German advance in the north."],["The 5th Guards Tank Army moves to Prokhorovka","The 5th Guards Tank Army, from the Steppe Front, began advancing to Prokhorovka to reinforce the Voronezh Front."],["The Voronezh Front counterattacks in pieces","Vatutin ordered a counterattack on the II SS Panzer Corps, but poor coordination made it a series of piecemeal attacks. It still stalled the II SS Panzer Corps for the day."],["The II SS Panzer Corps turns to Prokhorovka","Hoth turned the II SS Panzer Corps of the 4th Panzer Army away from Oboyan to attack north-east towards Prokhorovka. This move had been part of the plan since the start of the offensive."],["Army Detachment Kempf breaks through","In a surprise night attack the 6th Panzer Division seized a bridge across the Donets. Army Detachment Kempf then pushed troops across for an advance on Prokhorovka from the south."],["The tank battle at Prokhorovka","The 5th Guards Tank Army attacked down the slopes before Prokhorovka against the II SS Panzer Corps, which was attacking on its own plan. Neither side accomplished its objectives that day."],["Operation Kutuzov opens against the Orel salient","The Bryansk and Western Fronts began Operation Kutuzov against the Orel salient. The thrusts endangered German supply routes and threatened the 9th Army with encirclement, so that it went over fully to the defensive."],["The II SS Panzer Corps ends its offensive","The southern offensive went on with Operation Roland from 14 July. On 17 July the II SS Panzer Corps was ordered to end its offensive operations and begin to withdraw."],["Operation Rumyantsev drives south to Belgorod","The Voronezh and Steppe Fronts attacked the northern wing of Army Group South, aiming to destroy the 4th Panzer Army and Army Detachment Kempf. They drove through the German positions and by 5 August had liberated Belgorod."]]],
  "Battle of Königgrätz": ["battle-of-koniggratz","38551f82f7",[["The armies take position either side of the river","The Prussians took their position west of the Bystřice river. The Austrian infantry stood partly fortified, with artillery behind it and cavalry in the rear, and the Saxons on the Austrian left."],["Austrian guns pin down the Prussian right","The Austrian artillery opened fire on the Prussian right under Herwarth von Bittenfeld. The Saxons fell back in good order and fired on the advancing Prussians from higher ground. Around 10 am the advance guard of seven battalions was pulled back to the river."],["The 7th Division advances into the Swiepwald","The Prussian centre under Fransecky led the advance into the Swiepwald, where two Austrian corps met it. The division had to clear the forest and cover the Prussian left until the Second Army arrived."],["The First Army crosses and Sadová is taken","King Wilhelm I ordered the First Army across the river to support Fransecky. Sadová was captured, but a fierce battle followed in a nearby forest."],["Austrian artillery halts the Prussian attack","The Austrian artillery fired into the smoke of the Prussian advance. The advancing Prussian 8th and 4th Divisions were cut down as soon as they came out of it, and the attack was halted."],["Pöckh's charge drives the 7th Division back","The Austrian centre began a manoeuvre to flank the 7th Division. Colonel Pöckh was sent to drive the Prussians back, and with a fierce infantry charge forced the division back to the outskirts of the forest."],["Flanking fire destroys Pöckh's battalion","Fire from the 8th Division raked Pöckh's battalion, ended it as a fighting force and killed its commander. The 8th Division stiffened the Prussian centre against the Austrian thrusts."],["The Second Army reaches the field","Crown Prince Frederick William arrived with the main bulk of the Second Army and hit the Austrian right flank as it retired from the Swiepwald. The 1st Guard came with it."],["The 1st Guard reaches the Austrian artillery","Hiller von Gaertringen's 1st Guard reached the Austrian artillery, which had been trying to form a line again. It destroyed the one cavalry battery that stayed to fight and forced the other guns to flee."],["The Second Army breaks through and takes Chlum","The Second Army completely broke through the Austrian lines and took Chlum behind the centre, as the Austrian centre was rolled up."],["The Army of the Elbe breaks the Austrian left","The Army of the Elbe, which had merely held position since the morning, attacked and broke through the Austrian left flank. It seized Probluz and went on to destroy the flank."],["Austrian cavalry cover the retreat","Benedek ordered a general retreat and a series of cavalry countercharges to back up his artillery and cover it. The cavalry kept the bridges over the Elbe open, at a cost of 2,000 men and almost as many horses."]]],
  "Battle of Lake Trasimene": ["battle-of-lake-trasimene","9c40e8d9ae",[["The Carthaginians lie hidden behind the hills","After dark Hannibal moved his army behind the hills and placed it on the reverse slopes: cavalry in the west, Gauls next, and the African and Iberian infantry in the east near the camp. The Romans, who could see the camp, set off along the lakeshore."],["The head of the column meets the easternmost Carthaginians","The Romans marched east in a thick morning mist, by the ancient accounts, with no cavalry scouts sent ahead. The leading men made contact with the most easterly Carthaginians and the signal to advance was given."],["The Carthaginians attack from the flank and rear","The whole Carthaginian force came down from the hills against the column's flank and rear. The Romans could not form even a rudimentary fighting line, and some fled while others gathered in groups to face the enemy on all sides."],["Some Roman groups fight hard, most of all in the centre","Some of the clustered Roman groups put up a stiff fight, especially in the centre, where the attacking Gauls suffered heavy casualties. The fighting went on for three hours."],["The trapped Romans collapse and some go into the lake","After three hours the trapped part of the Roman army collapsed. Men who tried to swim the lake drowned, and others waded out until the water reached their necks, where Carthaginian cavalrymen swam their horses out to cut at them."],["The 6,000 at the front push out through the defile","The trap did not close on the 6,000 Romans at the head of the column. They pushed out through the defile against little opposition and marched on."],["The 6,000 are surrounded and surrender","Later in the day pursuing Carthaginians surrounded the 6,000 Romans. They surrendered to a force under Maharbal on the promise of being disarmed and freed."]]],
  "Battle of Leipzig": ["battle-of-leipzig","e7e47f5098",[["The French round Leipzig, the Coalition armies closing","Napoleon drew his army round Leipzig, with Ney and Marmont on the north, MacDonald on the east, Poniatowski and Augereau on the south-east and Bertrand's corps at Lindenau. The Coalition armies stood round them."],["The Coalition attacks from all sides","The Allies attacked from every side and completely encircled the French army. After over nine hours of fighting the French were slowly forced back towards Leipzig."],["The Allied assaults on Probstheida are beaten back","Barclay's columns advanced on Probstheida, a village southeast of Leipzig held behind high garden walls. The Prussian and then the Russian assaults were driven back by French artillery and the Imperial Guard."],["The Army of the North takes Paunsdorf and Schönefeld","Langeron's Russians and the Army of the North crossed the Parthe on the north-east and fought for Paunsdorf and Schönefeld. The Russians drove the French out of Schönefeld, and Ney ordered a withdrawal from Paunsdorf."],["Bertrand drives the Austrians from Lindenau","On the western side the French IV Corps drove the Austrian III Corps away from Lindenau. This broke the encirclement and cleared the way for the French retreat."],["The French withdraw in the night","The French army was ordered to withdraw silently through Leipzig and over its single bridge across the Elster. A rearguard was left to hold the city for a day or a little longer, while the Allies did not realise that the army was leaving."],["The Allies storm Leipzig","The Allies learned of the evacuation at 7 am and attacked from the north, south and east. Oudinot's rearguard held them up in street fighting among barricades and houses."],["The bridge is blown and the rearguard is cut off","A corporal fired the fuses of the only bridge over the Elster while it was crowded with French troops and Oudinot's rearguard was still in Leipzig. The panic became a rout in which thousands died and 30,000 were captured. The rest of the army had crossed."]]],
  "Battle of Lepanto": ["battle-of-lepanto","4afa286821",[["The two fleets draw up in line","The Holy League fleet formed four divisions in a north-south line, its left towards the coast. The Ottoman fleet faced it with a right, a centre and a left under Uluç Ali offshore in the south."],["The galleasses open fire","The wind shifted to favour the Christians shortly before contact. Galleasses in front of their line fired at close quarters at the foremost Turkish galleys and confused their battle array."],["Barbarigo and Sirocco meet by the shore","First contact was between Barbarigo and Sirocco near the northern shore. Barbarigo tried to keep close to the coast, but Sirocco knew the depth of the water and put galleys between his line and the shore. The ships closed into a hand-to-hand melee."],["Barbarigo is killed and Bazán's reserve arrives","Barbarigo was killed by an arrow to the eye. The timely arrival of Bazán with the reserve fleet balanced the situation on the left."],["Sirocco is killed and the freed slaves join in","Sirocco was eventually killed and beheaded. Christian galley slaves freed from the Turkish ships were given arms and joined the fighting, which turned the battle on this wing in the Christian favour."],["The centres clash around the flagships","The centres met so hard that Ali Pasha's galley drove into the Real as far as the fourth rowing bench. Spanish Tercio infantry and Turkish janissaries fought hand to hand around the two flagships."],["Colonna's counter-attack clears the Real","When the Real was nearly taken, Colonna came alongside with the bow of his galley and mounted a counter-attack. With his help the Turks were pushed off the Real."],["The Turkish flagship is taken","The Turkish flagship was boarded and swept, and its whole crew was killed, Ali Pasha among them. After two hours of fighting the Turks were beaten on the left and in the centre."],["Doria sails south and opens a gap","On the Christian right Doria went on sailing south instead of taking his assigned position, and opened a wide gap with the centre. His captains took his signals for treachery."],["Uluç Ali turns on the centre","With Doria too far away to interfere, Uluç Ali swung round and fell on Colonna's southern flank. He attacked some fifteen galleys round the flagship of the Knights of Malta."],["Bazán's reserve arrives and Uluç Ali retreats","The arrival of the reserve under Bazán kept Uluç Ali from breaking into the Christian centre. He was forced to retreat and escaped the battle with the captured flag of the Knights of Malta."]]],
  "Battle of Leuctra": ["battle-of-leuctra","4eb60d502c",[["The armies draw up","The Spartans stood with their own troops on the right and their allies on the left. Epaminondas set his Thebans in a very deep column on the left, opposite the Spartan right, with the other Boeotians further right and back."],["The cavalry meet and the Spartan horse is beaten","The battle opened with a cavalry engagement between the lines. The Theban cavalry, the more experienced of the two, quickly defeated the Spartan cavalry."],["The Spartan cavalry flees and the Thebans charge","The Spartan cavalry fled back through its own infantry and disordered the Spartan line just as the Thebans charged. The other Boeotians held back, by one account withdrawing gradually as they were attacked."],["The Spartan right holds while the Thebans press it","The Spartan line held long enough for the mortally wounded Cleombrotus to be carried off the field still alive. The massed Thebans, with the Sacred Band in front, pressed against the Spartan right."],["The Spartan right falls back","The Spartan right wing then fell back under the pressure of the Theban mass, which kept pushing against it with the Sacred Band in front."],["The Spartan right wing is turned and routed","The Spartan right wing was pushed back, then turned, and in the end it was routed and fled. Many of the Spartan hippeis, the royal bodyguard, had been killed in the fighting."],["The Spartan left wing gives way","The men on the left wing of the Spartan army saw that the right wing was being pushed back and gave way, so both wings of the Spartan army had given way."]]],
  "Battle of Madagascar": ["battle-of-madagascar","74050366a9",[["The landings west of Diego-Suarez","The first wave of the 29th Brigade landed at Ambararata Bay and the 17th Brigade at Courrier Bay. The Vichy troops were concentrated around Diego-Suarez, and the defences of Antisarane lay ahead."],["Both brigades advance inland","The 17th Brigade took the town of Diego-Suarez after crossing mangrove swamp and bush. The 29th Brigade, with the tanks, advanced 21 miles towards Antisarane against light resistance."],["A frontal assault on the line fails","The 29th Brigade and its tanks attacked the defences head on. Ten of the twelve tanks were destroyed by the end of the day, and the British had not known how strong the line was."],["The South Lancashires swing behind the line","The South Lancashires worked round the Vichy defences, but the swamps and bad terrain broke the unit into groups. It swung behind the Vichy line and caused chaos, and fire was poured on the defences from behind."],["The South Lancashires take the radio station and withdraw","From behind the line the South Lancashires captured the radio station and a barracks, and 200 prisoners were taken in all. They then had to withdraw when the radio set failed and contact with the main force was lost."],["Marines land in the rear and the line is broken","HMS Anthony ran past the Antisarane harbour defences and landed fifty Royal Marines from Ramillies in the Vichy rear area. At the same time the 17th Brigade broke through the defences."],["Antisarane surrenders","With the Vichy defence broken, Antisarane surrendered that evening, though substantial Vichy forces withdrew to the south."]]],
  "Battle of Magnesia": ["battle-of-magnesia","67edd4abec",[["The armies line up","The two armies lined up facing each other without fighting. The Romans stood with their left towards the rivers, and Antiochus took the Roman challenge on the third day after the last Roman advance."],["Eumenes drives off the chariots","Eumenes sent forward archers, slingers and spearmen against the scythed chariots. The chariots suffered heavy casualties and fled in panic, throwing the camel archers and cataphracts behind them into confusion."],["The Seleucid left is broken","Eumenes charged with his cavalry before the cataphracts could reorganise. The Roman and Pergamene cavalry broke through the Seleucid left flank, and the cataphracts fled to the Seleucid camp."],["The infantry beside the phalanx give way","The Galatians, Cappadocians and mercenary infantry were attacked at once by the Roman centre and right. They retreated, which left the left flank of the phalanx exposed."],["Antiochus attacks and the Roman infantry retreats","Antiochus led the attack with the cataphracts and agema cavalry against the Latin infantry, while the argyraspides engaged the Roman legionaries. The Roman infantry broke ranks and retreated to their camp."],["The Romans are reinforced and rallied at their camp","The Thracians and Macedonians reinforced the Roman infantry at their camp, and the tribune Marcus Aemilius Lepidus rallied them. Antiochus's cavalry was unsuitable for taking the camp and became bogged down in the fighting."],["The phalanx holds, then begins to retreat","The phalanx held its ground against the Roman infantry but was not mobile enough to dislodge the archers and slingers who bombarded it with projectiles. It began a slow organised retreat."],["The elephants panic and the phalanx breaks","The war elephants between the units of the phalanx panicked at the projectiles, and the phalanx broke formation. The phalangites discarded their weapons and abandoned the battlefield."],["Antiochus withdraws","Antiochus's cavalry came back to reinforce the centre after his army had already dispersed. He gathered the surviving troops and retreated to Sardes."]]],
  "Battle of Manzikert": ["battle-of-manzikert","21c7ad5401",[["The Byzantines form up and march out","The Byzantine army gathered into battle formation and marched on the Seljuk positions, with Bryennios on the left, Theodore Alyates on the right and the emperor in the centre. Andronikos Doukas held the reserve in the rear, and the Seljuks stood in a crescent about four kilometres away."],["Horse archers draw the crescent round them","Seljuk horse archers shot at the Byzantines as they drew closer. The centre of the crescent kept falling back while the wings moved round to surround the advancing troops."],["The Byzantines take the camp, but the wings fray","The Byzantine centre pressed on despite the arrows and captured Alp Arslan's camp by the end of the afternoon. The wings, where the arrows did most damage, almost broke up as units tried to force a pitched battle, and the Seljuk cavalry simply disengaged when challenged."],["The withdrawal is ordered and misunderstood","With the Seljuks avoiding battle, Romanos ordered a withdrawal. The right wing misunderstood the order, and Doukas ignored it and marched his reserve straight back to the camp outside Manzikert instead of covering the retreat."],["The Seljuks attack and the right wing breaks","With the Byzantines confused, the Seljuks attacked. The right wing was almost at once routed, its men thinking they had been betrayed."],["The left wing is routed","The left wing under Bryennios held out a little longer, but it was also soon routed. The Seljuk upper wing was drawn against it."],["The centre is encircled and the emperor taken","The Seljuks encircled the remnants of the Byzantine centre, including the emperor and the Varangian Guard. Romanos was wounded and taken prisoner."]]],
  "Battle of Marathon": ["battle-of-marathon","3ad0af937e",[["The two armies form their lines","The two tribes in the Greek centre were drawn up four ranks deep and the tribes on their flanks eight deep. One explanation is that this made the Athenian line as long as the Persian line."],["The Greeks advance and the lines meet","On a signal from Miltiades the Athenians advanced and probably broke into a run at the limit of the archers' effectiveness. They went through the Persian arrows, protected for the most part by their armour, and met the enemy line."],["The Greek centre is pushed back","The fighting lasted a long time. In the centre the Persians and Sacae prevailed and broke through in pursuit inland, while on each wing the Athenians and Plataeans prevailed."],["The Persian wings are routed","The Athenian and Plataean wings quickly routed the Persian levies on the flanks, which were of poorer quality than the troops in the centre."],["The Greek wings turn inwards on the centre","The Athenian wings then turned inwards to surround the Persian centre, which had been more successful against the thin Greek centre."],["The Persian centre breaks and flees to the ships","The Persian centre broke in panic towards its ships, pursued by the Greeks. Some, unaware of the local terrain, ran towards the swamps, where unknown numbers drowned."]]],
  "Battle of Marengo": ["battle-of-marengo","c7f7ff949e",[["The two armies take their ground","The Austrians crossed the Bormida in three bodies. Victor held Marengo and the Fontanone with Kellermann's cavalry on his left, Lannes stood to the north-east, and Monnier and the Guard waited east of Castel Ceriolo."],["The Austrian assaults at the Fontanone fail","Melas sent his divisions at Marengo across the stream one after another. Forced into a funnel by the ground, the first attack failed under fire from two sides, and the second was beaten off by 11 am."],["Ott takes Castel Ceriolo and presses the French right","Ott's leading troops took Castel Ceriolo and began to press the French right flank. Monnier's division and the Guard were brought across to extend and shore up that flank."],["The Austrians bridge the Fontanone and take Stortiglione","A small bridge was thrown over the Fontanone under cover of reserve artillery, and Lattermann's grenadiers crossed to attack the French defending Marengo village. O'Reilly took Stortiglione by 2:00 pm."],["Victor withdraws to the vines","Victor could no longer hold his positions and fell back south-east to the main belt of vines, with Lannes mirroring the move."],["Ott defeats Monnier","Ott, aided by Frimont, defeated Monnier's division and forced two thirds of it to retreat to the north-east, away from the main French army."],["The Consular Guard is destroyed","Bonaparte sent his main Guard battalion and its artillery to flank the Austrian column and delay it. After a firefight of fifteen minutes the Guard was surprised and destroyed by Frimont's cavalry."],["The French fall back towards San Giuliano","The French fell back about 3 km and tried to regroup at San Giuliano. The Austrian centre formed into a pursuit column to chase them off the field."],["Desaix's force arrives and meets the column","Boudet's division was deployed in front of San Giuliano and advanced in line of brigades against the head of the Austrian column, defeating its leading brigade. The column came on along the road."],["Lattermann's grenadiers renew the attack","The Austrian staff brought up Lattermann's grenadier brigade in line and renewed the attack, with a dragoon regiment in support."],["Desaix is sent forward and the cavalry is ordered in","Faced with a crisis, Napoleon sent Desaix forward again and ordered the cavalry charge that Desaix had asked for."],["Kellermann's charge breaks the grenadiers","Lattermann's formation was charged on its left flank by Kellermann's heavy cavalry, about 400 men, and disintegrated."],["The supporting dragoons are routed","Murat and Kellermann at once attacked the supporting Liechtenstein Dragoons, which were too slow to respond, and routed them. The fleeing horsemen crashed into Pilatti's troopers and carried them away."],["The Austrian main body breaks and the French advance","As the terrified cavalry stampeded past, the exhausted Austrian infantry of the main body lost heart and rushed to the rear. The whole French infantry line advanced westward."]]],
  "Battle of Marignano": ["battle-of-marignano","5af01841aa",[["The French form up and the Swiss approach","The French, who had not expected battle, formed in three divisions. The vanguard held two shallow rises with crossbowmen, a Landsknecht square and arquebusiers, the king's force stood two kilometres behind, and the Swiss came on in three divisions."],["The Swiss forlorn hope charges the guns","A forlorn hope detached from the Swiss vanguard and charged the grand battery with lowered pikes. It drove back the Landsknecht defenders and captured a few of the guns, the speed of its advance making the French fire ineffective."],["Bourbon's cavalry strikes the flank","Cavalry from the French right counter-attacked the forlorn hope's flank and drove it back to the shelter of the Swiss vanguard."],["The pursuing French horse fall back","The French horse that chased the forlorn hope were themselves forced to retreat a full kilometre after taking heavy losses."],["Night fighting by moonlight","Smoke and darkness hid the fighting. French cavalry charges, often led by the king himself with Bayard at his side, threw back temporary Swiss gains time and again, and the outcome hung in the balance."],["The fighting stops in the darkest hours","In the darkest hours the fighting stopped and both armies drew apart and reorganised. During the night some Swiss soldiers, and even whole units, slipped away from the field."],["The Swiss charge the guns again at dawn","The Swiss reformed their largest phalanx and charged the reassembled battery once more. Massed cannon fire slowed it, and though the Landsknechts were again driven back, the guns at point blank range stopped it pushing farther."],["Bayard's cavalry charge forces the Swiss back","Another French cavalry charge, this time led by Bayard, forced the attacking Swiss to give ground after the guns had checked their advance at point blank range."],["The Swiss turn on the French left","Baffled by the artillery, the Swiss turned their assault on Alençon's left-wing division. They made some headway, and the attack was then thrown back."],["The Venetians arrive","Allied Venetian forces under d'Alviano arrived in the mid-morning and turned the tide against the Swiss, whose attacks had been repulsed everywhere."],["The Swiss withdraw in a single square","With their ranks in bloody shambles, the Swiss gave ground grudgingly and withdrew by forming a single large open square, keeping strict discipline in their retreat."]]],
  "Battle of Midway": ["battle-of-midway","fec76a06d2",[["The fleets take their places","Nimitz sent Fletcher's force to the Midway area before the Japanese arrived, and the two American task forces waited northeast of the atoll. Nagumo's carriers approached from the west, with Yamamoto's ships several hundred miles behind them."],["The Japanese strike Midway","Nagumo launched his first attack on Midway at 04:30, with 36 dive bombers and 36 torpedo bombers escorted by 36 Zeros. Marine fighters from the island intercepted it, and the base was bombed from 06:34, though most of its defences stayed intact."],["Midway's bombers attack the carriers","American bombers that had taken off from Midway before the Japanese attack made several attacks on the Japanese carrier force. The Japanese repelled them, losing only three Zero fighters."],["The American carriers launch their strikes","Enterprise and Hornet began launching a few minutes after 07:00, steaming away from the Japanese to launch into the wind, and Yorktown followed at 08:00. Spruance sent his aircraft off piecemeal rather than wait for them to assemble."],["American torpedo squadrons are shot down","Torpedo Squadron 8 sighted the Japanese carriers and attacked at 09:20 without fighter escort, and all 15 of its aircraft were shot down. Torpedo Squadron 6 lost 9 of its 14. All their torpedoes missed or failed to explode."],["Yorktown's torpedo squadron draws the fighters off","Yorktown's torpedo squadron VT-3 attacked from the southeast, and 10 of its 12 aircraft were shot down without a hit. Its appearance drew most of the Japanese fighter patrol to that side of the fleet, leaving it out of position."],["Dive bombers set Kaga ablaze","Both of Enterprise's dive-bomber squadrons dived at Kaga by mistake, and she took three to five direct hits. One bomb killed her captain and most of her senior officers, and she was ablaze from stem to stern within six minutes."],["Akagi and Sōryū are hit","Lieutenant Best and his two wingmen dived on Akagi, which took a single bomb that exploded among the armed and fuelled aircraft. At the same time Yorktown's VB-3 hit Sōryū at least three times. All three carriers were eventually abandoned and scuttled."],["Hiryū's first wave hits Yorktown","Hiryū, the one carrier still operating, sent 18 dive bombers and 6 Zeros after the retreating American aircraft. They found Yorktown and hit her with three bombs, and Fletcher moved his command staff to the heavy cruiser Astoria."],["Hiryū's second wave cripples Yorktown","About an hour later ten torpedo bombers and six Zeros arrived. Yorktown's repairs had been so effective that the Japanese pilots took her for a different carrier. Two torpedoes crippled her, and she lost all power and listed 23 degrees to port."],["Enterprise's bombers cripple Hiryū","A Yorktown scout found Hiryū, and Enterprise launched 24 dive bombers. Four bombs, possibly five, hit her and left her ablaze. She stayed afloat for several hours and sank after being spotted the next morning."],["Spruance withdraws to the east","Spruance, fearing a possible night encounter with Japanese surface forces, changed course and withdrew to the east. He turned back west towards the Japanese at midnight."],["Yamamoto sends his ships east","Yamamoto at first decided to continue the engagement and sent his remaining surface forces searching eastward for the American carriers. At the same time he detached a cruiser raiding force to bombard Midway."],["Yamamoto orders a general withdrawal","The Japanese surface forces failed to make contact with the Americans, and Yamamoto ordered a general withdrawal to the west. His main body and the cruiser force turned back."]]],
  "Battle of Mikatagahara": ["battle-of-mikatagahara","7294086627",[["The armies take their ground on the plain","Ieyasu drew up his forces in a line on the plain of Mikatagahara. Shingen arrayed his men in the fish-scale formation in four lines, with the vanguard first and Baba Nobuharu in the fourth."],["The Takeda cavalry overruns the Tokugawa right","As snow began to fall the Tokugawa arquebusiers opened fire. Naitō Masatoyo's vanguard cavalry attacked all the same and overran Honda Tadakatsu's segment on the Tokugawa right."],["The Oda reinforcements are routed","Takeda horsemen exploited the opening and quickly assaulted the Oda reinforcements, who were overrun and routed. Their officer Hirate Hirohide was killed, and Sakuma Nobumori and Takigawa Kazumasu fled the battle."],["The Takeda horse charge the Tokugawa rear","Having exploited the opening and assaulted the Oda reinforcements, the Takeda horsemen went on to charge the Tokugawa rear, which put them behind the Tokugawa centre."],["The attack on the Tokugawa left is thrown back","A similar attack on the Tokugawa left was thwarted. The Tokugawa units there refused to yield ground and shrugged off the advance of the Takeda right, which prevented an encirclement of the centre."],["Shingen rests his vanguard and brings up fresh horse","Shingen withdrew his vanguard to rest. He brought forward a new set of horsemen from the main body of the army, under Takeda Katsuyori, Obata Masamori and Saegusa Moritomo."],["A two-pronged charge and the main body break the line","The fresh horsemen led a two-pronged cavalry charge into the weakening Tokugawa line, closely followed by the main body. Their combined weight drove the already battered Tokugawa army into a disorderly retreat."],["Ieyasu flees and his units are overrun","Convinced by his retainers that his life was too important, Ieyasu fled the field. Several of his retainers died as their units were encircled and overrun by the Takeda forces."],["The Takeda vanguard stops short of Hamamatsu","Ieyasu returned to Hamamatsu with five men and ordered the gates left open and braziers lit. The Takeda vanguard under Baba Nobuharu and Yamagata Masakage took this for a trap and stopped to make camp for the night short of the town."],["A night raid throws the Takeda vanguard into confusion","A band of about one hundred Tokugawa foot soldiers and 16 matchlock gunners under Ōkubo Tadayo and Amano Yasukage attacked the Takeda camp and threw the vanguard of the Takeda army into confusion."],["Shingen decides to withdraw","Uncertain of the Tokugawa strength, and worried that reinforcements from Oda Nobunaga or Uesugi Kenshin were on their way, Shingen decided to withdraw his forces to his own territories."]]],
  "Battle of Mohács": ["battle-of-mohacs","df1cd0bf17",[["The two armies take up their positions","The Hungarians stood in two lines on a waterlogged plain, mercenary infantry and artillery in the centre of the first with heavy cavalry on both flanks, and levy infantry and reserve cavalry behind. The Ottomans faced them with Ibrahim Pasha in the centre."],["The Hungarian cavalry charges the Ottoman front","In the early afternoon Archbishop Pál Tomori led a charge that drove back the Ottoman front line and caused disorder among the irregulars. The Ottoman centre gave ground."],["The Ottoman centre falls back in a crescent","Ibrahim Pasha's centre fell back in a controlled manoeuvre and formed a crescent. This exposed the flanks of the advancing Hungarian cavalry."],["King Louis and the reserves move in support","King Louis and the reserves moved forward in support of the Hungarian cavalry, which had advanced past the Ottoman centre."],["The Ottoman wings counterattack and the guns open fire","The Ottomans struck with cavalry and light infantry from both wings, while Janissaries and artillery fired from prepared positions. The Hungarian army had advanced too far and too fast."],["The Hungarian army is encircled and its lines collapse","Surrounded and under concentrated musket and cannon fire, the Hungarian army collapsed within two hours. Over 14,000 Hungarian troops died by one estimate."],["King Louis flees and drowns near the Csele","King Louis II tried to flee from the collapsing army but drowned in the marshy ground near the Csele stream, weighed down by his armour."]]],
  "Battle of Mycale": ["battle-of-mycale","1bcae42366",[["The Greeks land and form two wings","The Greeks landed out of sight of the Persian camp and formed two wings. The left stood from the shore to the foothills of the mountain, the right on the hills. The Persians had a palisaded camp with the fleet beached behind it."],["The Greek left meets the Persians alone","The Persians came out of their camp and set up their shield wall. The Greek left began fighting while the right was still crossing the hills."],["The Greek right comes up under arrow fire","The Greek right, under heavy arrow fire, decided to fight at close quarters. The Persians kept their shield wall unbroken and held their position."],["The right breaks the shield wall","The contingents of the right encouraged each other, saying the victory should belong to them and not to the Spartans, and broke through the wall by shoving. Tigranes and Mardontes died in the fighting."],["The Persians flee to their camp","The Persians fought back at first, then broke their lines and escaped to their camp, pursued by the Greek right."],["The right follows them into the camp","The soldiers of the right wing followed the Persians into the camp. The ethnic Persian troops grouped together and fought the Greeks who entered."],["The left outflanks the camp","The Greek left arrived, outflanked the camp and fell on the rear of the Persians who remained, which completed the rout."],["The Milesians turn on the Persians","The Milesians guarding the passes first misdirected the fleeing Persians, who ended up back among the Greek troops, and then began killing them."],["The fleet and fortifications are burned","The Greeks burned the Persian fleet after a heavily contested fight with the Persian marines, once the loot had been taken from the ships and placed on the beach. The fortifications were burnt too."]]],
  "Battle of Myriokephalon": ["battle-of-myriokephalon","1b55c3c131",[["The Byzantine column enters the pass","The Byzantine divisions entered the pass in a long column, vanguard first and the rear division last. The Seljuks held the heights above it, perhaps not yet fully deployed."],["The leading divisions clear the pass","The vanguard and main division went through with few casualties. Their infantry climbed the slopes and forced the Seljuks back to higher ground, while the divisions behind did not take this precaution."],["The Turks close the trap on the right wing","With the first two divisions out of the pass and the rear about to enter, the Turks attacked from the heights. The right wing lost cohesion and broke, and its commander Baldwin was killed."],["The baggage and siege trains are shot down","The Turks then concentrated their attacks on the baggage and siege trains. They shot down the draught animals and choked the road."],["The left wing is struck and Kantakouzenos falls","The left wing also suffered significant casualties. One of its leaders, John Kantakouzenos, was killed while fighting alone against a band of Seljuk soldiers."],["The rear is attacked and a dust-storm falls","The remaining Byzantine troops panicked at the carnage ahead and as the Turks began to attack their rear. A blinding dust-storm fell, and Manuel sat down to await his fate."],["Manuel forms up and leaves the pass","Roused by his officers, Manuel restored discipline and formed a defensive order. His troops pushed past the wrecked baggage and out of the pass to join the van and main divisions, who had built a fortified camp."],["The rear division reaches the camp","The rear division under Andronikos Kontostephanos arrived at the camp somewhat later than the emperor, having suffered few casualties."],["The camp repulses attacks through the night","Seljuk mounted archers attacked the camp again and again through the night, and the Byzantines repulsed them each time."],["The Turks circle the camp and two counterattacks follow","The Turks circled the camp firing arrows, and Manuel ordered two counterattacks, but there was no renewal of a general action."]]],
  "Battle of Nagashino": ["battle-of-nagashino","b8dc666e6d",[["The allies camp at Shitaragahara","Nobunaga halted at Shitaragahara without going to the relief of Nagashino Castle and camped west of it, Nobutada on Mount Shinmido and Ieyasu on Mount Takamatsu facing the river. The Takeda were still around the castle."],["Takeda marches east of Shitaragahara","Katsuyori led his main force towards Shitaragahara and left 3,000 men at the siege. The Oda and Tokugawa forces advanced, and the two sides faced each other across the Rengo River."],["Sakai sets out by night","Nobunaga took a suggestion from Sakai Tadatsugu and ordered a surprise attack on Mount Tobigasu. Sakai set out in the middle of the night with a flying column of about 4,000 men."],["Sakai attacks the fort from behind","Sakai's column fell on the Takeda soldiers defending the fort from behind Mount Tobigasu. The soldiers in the fort were defeated, and the enemy now stood behind Katsuyori's main army."],["The column joins the castle and drives off the besiegers","The detachment entered the castle in cooperation with its own soldiers and drove the Takeda forces off. Katsuyori's main army was left with its retreat cut off and the enemy behind it."],["The Takeda charge the allied camps","With nowhere to go, the Takeda main force attacked the Tokugawa and Oda camps in front of it, one after the other."],["Nobunaga takes command and sends up the matchlocks","Nobunaga came to Ieyasu's camp on Mount Takamatsu to see how the fighting stood and took command of the whole army there. He then sent a matchlock corps led by five bugyō to the front line."],["Guns and fences stop the charges","The Takeda charged repeatedly, and each time the matchlock guns and the horse defence fences blocked them and they fell back."],["The Takeda forces begin their rout","By midday it was increasingly clear which side had won, and the Takeda forces began their rout, their main force falling back from the allied camps towards the east."],["Nobunaga falls on the broken ranks","With many of their troops killed, the Takeda army retreated. Nobunaga pounced on them when their ranks gave way and inflicted even more casualties; the fighting lasted until around 2 pm."]]],
  "Battle of Navarino": ["battle-of-navarino","1d876e4865",[["The Allied squadrons enter the bay","The Ottoman and Egyptian fleet lay anchored in a horseshoe of three lines. The Allied ships came through the southern entrance in two lines, British and French to starboard and the Russians to port, slightly behind."],["The Allies anchor in the crescent","The three British ships of the line anchored facing the Ottoman centre. The French took the side facing the Ottoman left wing, and the Russians the right end of the crescent. Allied captains had orders to fire only if attacked."],["Dartmouth's cutter is fired on","Fellowes of Dartmouth saw an Ottoman crew preparing a fireship and sent a boat to tell them to stop. The Ottomans fired on the boat and lit the fireship, then fired on the cutter sent to tow it clear."],["The firing spreads along the line","Fellowes opened musket fire to cover his men, and the French flagship Sirène fired to support Dartmouth. An Ottoman corvette attacked Sirène, and the shooting spread until there was a general engagement."],["A fireship jams under Scipion's bowsprit","The French Scipion came under intense attack from Egyptian frigates, the shore batteries and a fireship. The fireship jammed under her bowsprit and set her fore sails alight; Trident towed it clear."],["The French silence the fort's battery","Sirène, with the support of Trident and Scipion, bombarded the fort of Navarino and eventually silenced its shore battery."],["Breslaw breaks away from the French","Seeing that Rigny did not need further support, the captain of Breslaw decided on his own initiative to leave the French formation and head for the centre of the bay."],["The centre fights at anchor","Ships fired at very close range, with little room to manoeuvre. Asia concentrated her fire on Fahti Bahri, and Breslaw reached the junction of the British and Russian lines to reinforce Albion and Azov, both hard pressed."],["The Ottoman front line is despatched","All three Ottoman ships of the line and most of the large frigates of the first line had been despatched. The small British and French vessels stopped the fireships, and only the first attack on Scipion got home."],["The smaller vessels are destroyed","The Allied ships of the line, all still operational, fired on the second and third lines. Codrington tried twice to order a ceasefire, but his signals were hidden by smoke or ignored. Of 78 vessels, just eight stayed seaworthy."]]],
  "Battle of Nicopolis": ["battle-of-nicopolis","e694770966",[["The armies take position","The French knights rode out with their backs to Nicopolis. The Hungarians, Hospitallers and Germans stayed behind them, while the Turks stood on the slope to the south with their reserve of sipahis out of sight on the plateau."],["The French charge the Turkish front line","The French knights charged and crushed the untrained conscripts at the front of the Turkish army. The conscripts broke and the knights pressed on towards the trained infantry behind them."],["The knights fight through the stakes","The knights came under heavy fire from archers and were held up by rows of sharpened stakes. Many were unhorsed or dismounted to pull the stakes up, and the Turkish infantry fled behind the sipahis."],["The knights press up the slope on foot","The younger knights overruled Coucy and Vienne, who wanted a pause to reform, and insisted on pursuit. The French went on up the hill, more than half of them on foot, while sipahis appear to have swept down along the flanks."],["The knights reach the plateau and meet the sipahis","Struggling in heavy armour, the French reached the plateau at the top of the slope, where they had expected to find fleeing Turks. They found instead a fresh corps of sipahis that Bayezid had kept in reserve."],["The sipahis counter-attack on the plateau","The sipahis surged forward, sounding trumpets, banging kettle drums and shouting the Tekbir. Some French knights fled back down the slope, and the rest fought on."],["Nevers is taken and the French yield","The Turks threatened to overwhelm Nevers, and his bodyguard threw themselves to the ground to plead for his life. The Turks took Nevers prisoner, and seeing this, the rest of the French yielded."],["The Transylvanians and Wallachians leave the field","The Hungarians and other nationalities were in confused combat on the plain. At the sight of a stampede of riderless horses, the Transylvanians and Wallachians decided the day was lost and abandoned the field."],["Sigismund resists the envelopment","Sigismund, the Master of Rhodes and the Germans fought to prevent the sipahis from enveloping them, and both sides suffered heavy losses."],["The Serbian knights join the Turks","The Turks received a reinforcement of 1,500 Serbian knights under Stefan Lazarević, which proved critical to the outcome of the fight."],["Sigismund's force is overwhelmed","Sigismund's force was overwhelmed. Sigismund and the Master escaped on a fisherman's boat, and the rest of his army, after a deal was made, surrendered."]]],
  "Battle of Okinawa": ["battle-of-okinawa","d8d0574fd2",[["The Kerama Islands are taken","Soldiers of the 77th Infantry Division landed in the Kerama Islands, 15 miles west of Okinawa. Task Force 58 lay to the east of the island, the Thirty-Second Army held the south and Udo Force the north."],["The main landing at the Hagushi beaches","The XXIV Corps and the III Amphibious Corps landed on the Hagushi beaches on the west coast. Off the Minatoga beaches in the south-east the 2nd Marine Division made a demonstration to deceive the Japanese."],["The Marines close the north and the 77th takes Ie Shima","The 6th Marine Division sealed off the Motobu Peninsula, where Udo Force was cornered around Mount Yaedake, and cleared it on 18 April. The 77th Division assaulted Ie Shima on 16 April."],["The advance south meets Kakazu Ridge","The 96th and 7th Infantry Divisions wheeled south and met Japanese soldiers in fortified caves at Kakazu Ridge, part of Shuri's outer defences. The advance went on, with heavy casualties on both sides."],["Ushijima attacks across the front","Ushijima, influenced by General Chō, took the offensive and the Thirty-Second Army attacked American positions across the whole front on the evening of 12 April. The assaults of the following nights were repulsed."],["The offensive of 19 April meets the Japanese defences","General Hodge launched a new offensive with a barrage of 324 guns, joined by ships and by 650 aircraft. The Japanese waited on reverse slopes, and a tank assault round Kakazu Ridge failed, so there was no breakthrough."],["The Japanese counter-offensive of 4 May fails","Ushijima attempted amphibious assaults on the coasts behind the American lines, and his artillery moved into the open to support it. American counter-battery fire destroyed dozens of guns, and the attack failed."],["Conical Hill and Sugar Loaf Hill are taken","The 96th Infantry Division took Conical Hill, the eastern anchor of the main Japanese defences, while the 1st and 6th Marine Divisions fought for Sugar Loaf Hill on the opposite coast. Their capture exposed the Japanese round Shuri on both sides."],["Shuri is left and the army withdraws south","The 6th Marine Division occupied the ruins of Naha, and Marines of the 1st Marine Division took Shuri Castle on 29 May, which the Thirty-Second Army had left. The army moved nearly 30,000 men to the Kiyan Peninsula."],["The Oroku Peninsula is taken","Elements of the 6th Marine Division launched an amphibious assault on the Oroku Peninsula. The 4,000 Japanese sailors, including Admiral Ōta, killed themselves in the tunnels of their underground headquarters on 13 June."],["The last pocket in the south","By 17 June the remnants of Ushijima’s Thirty-Second Army were pushed into a small pocket in the far south of the island, to the southeast of Itoman."]]],
  "Battle of Oriskany": ["battle-of-oriskany","ea767bdafb",[["The column enters the ravine","Herkimer's column marched towards Fort Stanwix and went down into the ravine and over the stream. The Haudenosaunee lay hidden on both sides of it, and the Royal Yorkers waited behind a nearby rise."],["The warriors open fire too early","The Haudenosaunee fired before the plan called for it, and the Patriots were taken by surprise. Colonel Ebenezer Cox was shot from his horse in the first volley, and Herkimer's leg was shattered."],["The rear of the column flees","The trap had been sprung too early and part of the column had not yet entered the ravine. Most of these men panicked and fled, and some of the warriors pursued them, leaving dead and wounded for several miles."],["The militia rally on the crest","About half of Herkimer's men were still fighting 30 minutes in. They rallied and fought out of the ravine to the crest on its west side, and Herkimer regrouped them on higher ground during a one-hour thunderstorm."],["The Royal Yorkers advance in turned coats","When the fighting resumed, the Royal Yorkers advanced with their coats turned inside out, to pass as a relief party from the fort. The ruse failed when a Patriot captain recognised a Loyalist neighbour, and close combat followed."],["Willett's sortie raids the camps","Herkimer's messengers reached the fort at around 11 am. Lieutenant Colonel Marinus Willett led 250 men out to raid the nearly deserted camps to the south, driving off the few people left in them."],["The warriors leave to protect their camps","A guard ran to the field to warn the warriors that their camps were being raided. They broke off with the Seneca signal to retire and headed for the camps to protect their women and possessions."],["The Germans and Loyalists withdraw","With the warriors gone, the smaller number of German and Loyalist combatants was forced to withdraw as well, and the fighting at the ravine ended."]]],
  "Battle of Plassey": ["battle-of-plassey","8e4669b76d",[["The two armies take their ground","The Nawab's army formed an arc with the French guns at the larger tank in front. Clive lined up the Europeans in the centre with a sepoy division on each side, and posted four guns at the kilns to answer the French."],["The guns open and Clive withdraws to the grove","The French guns fired the first shot and the rest of the Nawab's artillery followed. The British lost 10 Europeans and 20 sepoys by 8:30, and Clive left the advanced guns at the kilns and drew the army back into the grove."],["Rain, and Mir Madan Khan's charge is driven back","A rainstorm drenched the Nawab's gunpowder, while the British covered theirs. Mir Madan Khan led his cavalry in a charge, but British grapeshot mortally wounded him and drove his men back."],["The Nawab's army turns back to its entrenchments","The Nawab's army stopped its cannonade and began turning back north to its entrenchments. This left St. Frais and his artillery without support."],["Kilpatrick advances on his own","Seeing the Nawab’s forces retire, Major Kilpatrick, left in charge while Clive rested in the hunting lodge, took two companies and two field pieces and advanced towards St. Frais’ position."],["Clive leads the army against the French position","Clive hurried to the detachment and had Kilpatrick bring up the rest of the army from the grove. He then led the army against St. Frais, whose artillery retreated to the redoubt of the entrenchment."],["Mir Jafar's division turns towards the grove and is halted","The Nawab's left arm, which had lingered behind the rest, turned and marched towards the grove. Clive took it for a threat to his baggage and sent three platoons and a field piece, whose fire halted it."],["The British cannonade the camp from the larger tank","British field pieces cannonaded the Nawab’s camp from the mound of the larger tank, and many of his troops and guns came out of the entrenchment. Clive moved half his troops and guns to the smaller tank and half to rising ground."],["The British take the hill and the redoubt","Clive ordered a three-pronged attack on the redoubt and the hill east of it. Major Coote’s grenadiers took the hill at 16:30 after the enemy fled without firing a shot, and the redoubt fell after St. Frais was forced to retreat."],["The British hold the entrenchment and the camp","By 17:00 the British occupied the entrenchment and the camp left by a dispersing army. The Nawab’s divisions broke up and left the field."]]],
  "Battle of Plataea": ["battle-of-plataea","5043bcff76",[["The two lines face each other across the Asopos","The Persians lined up north of the river with their left wing facing the Spartans and Tegeans. Next to them stood the Medes and their neighbours, then the Boeotians and other Greek allies of Persia facing the Athenians."],["The night retreat goes wrong","The Greek centre moved off late at night and went too far, ending at the Temple of Hera. The Spartans and Tegeans had not begun to move by daybreak, and a lochos under Amompharetus was left behind. The Athenians were blocked by the scattered centre."],["The Spartans reach the Temple of Demeter under attack","The Spartans and Tegeans reached the Temple of Demeter, and the lochos under Amompharetus began to withdraw from the ridge to join them. The first units of the Persian cavalry started attacking them."],["Mardonius hurries after the Greeks","Mardonius learned of the retreat and rushed to Pausanias's position with the Persian left wing. When the Persian infantry arrived, the cavalry drew back to rest its mounts. The Athenians were engaged by the Boeotians."],["The Tegeans and Spartans charge","The Tegeans ran at the Persian line as arrows fell, and once Pausanias had favourable omens the Spartans charged too. Artabazos brought up 40,000 men in a tight formation, slowly. Amompharetus's men had joined the Spartans."],["Mardonius is killed and the Persian line breaks","Aeimnestus killed Mardonius with a stone. His guards broke and retreated and the rest of the army followed. Artabazos led his men away to Phocis, avoiding the camp."],["The Athenians outlast the Thebans","The Athenians still had to fight the Thebans who stood against them. Eventually the Thebans retreated. Meanwhile the Persian cavalry did not let the Greeks pursue and outflank the retreating Persian army."],["The Greeks chase the Persians to their camp","The Greeks chased the Persians to their camp. The Spartans arrived first and waited for the Athenians, who knew how to scale the walls."],["The Tegeans breach the camp walls","The Tegeans were the first to breach the walls. The Persians could not get into formation and took heavy losses."]]],
  "Battle of Poitiers (1356)": ["battle-of-poitiers-1356","3bbf97170a",[["The two armies take their ground","The Anglo-Gascons held a wooded hill, the Prince's division behind the other two as a reserve. The French drew up in four divisions, the first about 500 yards from the English."],["The French cavalry charge the two wings","Audrehem's horsemen rode at Warwick's division and Clermont's at Salisbury's. On the left the marsh kept them from the archers. On the right they reached the gap in the hedge and a fierce melee began."],["The French cavalry and the first division are driven back","The French cavalry took heavy casualties and withdrew; Audrehem was captured and Clermont killed. The longbowmen then forced the first division back as well, and its leader, Brienne, the constable of France, was killed."],["The second division attacks at the hedge","With no pursuit of the survivors, the English held their positions and reformed. The second French division, 4,000 strong, forced its way through the hedge and fought hand to hand for two hours, and the Prince committed almost all of his reserves."],["The Dauphin's division withdraws","Bourbon was killed and the Dauphin's standard-bearer captured, and the Dauphin's advisers took him and two brothers from the front line. The exhausted rest of the division withdrew as well, and the disengagement was orderly."],["Orléans marches away with half of the third division","As the Dauphin's division recoiled there was confusion in the French ranks. Orléans marched away with half of the third division, and the 1,600 who stayed launched a feeble attack that was repelled easily."],["Warwick's men pursue the French","After the failed attack a number, possibly a large number, of men from Warwick's division left their positions and pursued the French, one motive being the ransom of prisoners."],["The King's division advances with the Oriflamme","Survivors of the earlier attacks rallied to the King, and his division marched across the one-mile gap towards the exhausted Anglo-Gascons, all on foot. The Oriflamme was unfurled, the signal that no prisoners were to be taken."],["The Captal de Buch rides round the flank","The Anglo-Gascon commanders thought they would be defeated if they stood to face a fourth attack. They sent a small mounted group under the Captal de Buch on a circuitous march round the French flank to strike the rear."],["The Prince orders a general advance","Concerned that his army would break, the Black Prince ordered a general advance, and the Anglo-Gascons moved forward out of their defensive positions. The French men-at-arms charged home into them."],["Warwick's men return and steady the line","The Anglo-Gascon line was starting to break when men of Warwick's division returned from their pursuit and reinforced it. This heartened the Anglo-Gascons and discouraged the French."],["The Captal's force arrives behind the French","The Captal de Buch's 160 men arrived undetected in the French rear. His 100 archers dismounted and fired into it, and his 60 mounted men-at-arms charged into the back of the French line."],["The French break and the King surrenders","Some Frenchmen began to run, others copied them and the division fell apart. Those still fighting round the King were forced into a loop of the River Miosson and surrounded, and John and his youngest son surrendered."]]],
  "Battle of Poltava": ["battle-of-poltava","7f93fffac0",[["The Swedes form south of the redoubts","The Swedish columns formed in the night 600 metres south of the redoubts, with the Russian cavalry behind the redoubts and the camp beyond. The infantry was in place early, but the cavalry arrived late."],["Roos overruns two redoubts and stalls at the third","The Swedes in Roos' column quickly overran the first two redoubts and killed every Russian soldier inside them. By 4:30 a.m. their attempts to take the third redoubt had stalled."],["The Swedish wings pass the redoubts","Lewenhaupt's ten battalions on the right bypassed the first four redoubts. Hamilton's cavalry on the left passed the redoubts and charged the Russian cavalry, forcing it to retreat."],["Lewenhaupt is ordered back to the west","Rehnskiöld ordered his cavalry to stop the pursuit and Lewenhaupt to withdraw to the west. There the wings waited for Roos' battalions for two hours."],["Roos takes his men into the Yakovetski woods","Roos and six battalions, a third of the Swedish infantry, had become isolated at the third redoubt. After severe casualties he led the remaining 1,500 of his original 2,600 men into the Yakovetski woods to the east."],["The Russians attack Roos and drive him back","The Russians reoccupied the first two redoubts and launched a two-pronged attack by ten regiments. Roos was forced to retreat towards Poltava."],["Roos takes refuge in a fort and surrenders","Roos could not reach the Swedish siege works and took refuge in an abandoned fort by 9:00 a.m. He was forced to surrender at 9:30 a.m."],["Peter leads the Russian infantry out of the camp","Peter led his infantry out of the fortified camp, with dragoons under Bauer on his right and cavalry under Menshikov on his left. West of the camp they faced the Swedish infantry, with Creutz's cavalry in the rear."],["The Swedes advance and the Russian first line gives way","Rehnskiöld ordered Lewenhaupt and the Swedish line forward. The Swedes fired a volley, charged with muskets and pikemen, and the Russian first line retreated towards its second line."],["The Swedish left flank breaks","The Russian line was longer, and the Swedish infantry on the left flank lagged behind the right and finally threw down their weapons and fled. A gap opened in the Swedish line and the Russians filled it."],["Creutz is checked and Menshikov takes the Swedes in the rear","Barely able to gather his squadrons, Creutz tried to advance on the right flank, but the Russian battalions formed hollow squares. Menshikov's cavalry outflanked the Swedes and attacked them from the rear."],["The Swedish assault disintegrates","Small groups of Swedes broke through and escaped to the south through the Budyschenski woods, while many of the rest were overwhelmed, ridden down or captured. Charles ordered a retreat to the woods with what forces he could gather."]]],
  "Battle of Puebla": ["battle-of-puebla","90ce50675b",[["The French form up below the forts","Lorencez drew up an attack column of zouaves and guns, with a reserve behind it and a convoy guarded on the flank. The forts of Loreto and Guadalupe were held by Negrete's division, and Mexican cavalry stood on the French right."],["The French halt and shell the forts","The zouaves climbed towards Guadalupe and, when opposite it, halted and planted their artillery. The guns fired on both forts, and Zaragoza gave orders for them to be reinforced."],["The French meet two Mexican battalions halfway up","The zouaves climbed with little opposition and had gone halfway when two Mexican battalions met them, exchanged shots with the sharpshooters and returned to their position. Berriozábal was ordered to reinforce the two hills."],["The column turns between the forts and is fired on","The ascending column turned diagonally to the right, as if to go between the two forts, and both forts fired on the French troops. Mexican infantry under cover of the maguey field kept firing as well."],["The French retreat and a second column comes up","The zouaves retreated and the Mexicans pursued them. The pursuit stopped when another French column came to support the retreating troops."],["The second attack, from the north and the east","The two French columns pushed on together towards the Guadalupe and Resurrection chapel, joined and split into three. The attack was more vigorous. Diaz came up with part of his brigade and two guns."],["The columns are repulsed","The columns that went up from the north were routed. Diaz, with part of his brigade and two guns, checked and drove away the French columns marching against the Mexican positions."],["The third column is defeated on the east side","The third French column reached the east side just as the other two were being repulsed, and it was also defeated there."],["The Mexican cavalry charge and the French withdraw","The Mexican cavalry charged the remaining French and prevented them from reorganising for further assaults. The two armies faced each other until seven in the evening, when the French returned to their camp."]]],
  "Battle of Pydna": ["battle-of-pydna","87ff79b285",[["The armies stand drawn up","The Romans put their two legions in the middle with allied infantry on the flanks and cavalry on the wings. The Macedonian phalanx held the centre, with the Guard on its left, light troops on both flanks and Perseus with the cavalry on the right."],["The two centres engage","The Macedonians advanced on the Romans a short distance from the Roman camp. The Roman allies tried to beat down the pikes or hack off their points, but with little success."],["The allies retreat and the phalanx loses its line","Unable to get under the thick bristle of pikes, the Romans used a planned retreat over the rough ground. As the phalanx pushed on into the foothills the ground became uneven and its line lost cohesion."],["Paullus sends the legions into the gaps","Paullus ordered the legions into the gaps and they attacked the phalangites on their exposed flanks. At close quarters the longer Roman sword and heavier shield prevailed over the lighter Macedonian arms."],["The Roman right routs the Macedonian left","The Roman right succeeded in routing the Macedonian left, and it then joined the legions, who were already fighting the phalangites on their exposed flanks."],["Perseus flees with the right-wing cavalry","Seeing the tide of battle turn, Perseus fled with the cavalry on the Macedonian right, leaving the infantry still fighting on the field."],["The Guard fights to the death","The 3,000-strong Guard fought to the death. Nearly 11,000 Macedonians were captured, and the pursuit lasted until nightfall, though the battle itself lasted about an hour."]]],
  "Battle of Raphia": ["battle-of-raphia","4e48858234",[["The two armies drawn up","After five days of skirmishing both kings arrayed their troops. Each put the phalanx in the centre, the light troops and mercenaries beside it, the elephants in front of them and the cavalry on the wings."],["The elephants charge and the Ptolemaic ones panic","The elephants on both wings moved to charge. The smaller Ptolemaic African elephants retreated in panic before the larger Indian ones and ran through the infantry behind them, which fell into disorder."],["Antiochus charges the Ptolemaic horse","Antiochus led his cavalry to the right, rode past the left wing of the Ptolemaic elephants and charged the enemy horse."],["The phalanxes engage, and the Ptolemaic right wins","The two phalanxes closed in a stiff and chaotic fight. Ptolemy rode to the centre to urge on his phalanx, which had Egyptians beside the Macedonians. On the far right the Ptolemaic cavalry routed its opponents."],["Antiochus routs the Ptolemaic horse and pursues","Antiochus routed the Ptolemaic horse facing him and pursued the fleeing enemy in a mass, believing that he had won the day."],["The Ptolemaic phalanx drives the Seleucids back","The Ptolemaic phalanx drove the Seleucid phalanx back. Antiochus tried to ride back, but by then his troops were routed and could not be regrouped, and the battle was over."]]],
  "Battle of Sagrajas": ["battle-of-sagrajas","0d78dedbfe",[["The armies face each other across the river","The two armies had camped facing each other with only the Wadi Bira between them. The Muslims put heavy cavalry in front and kept the Almoravid reserve hidden behind a high mound; Alfonso drew up two divisions and a vanguard."],["The Castilian first division is thrown back","Alfonso sent his first division against Al-Mu'tamid, but it met the Almoravid cavalry before reaching him. The Almoravid archers helped drive it back to its second line, though the Almoravids lost men."],["The Andalusian front line gives ground","The Castilian assault and its better weapons forced Al-Mu'tamid's front line back. Some Andalusian leaders fled to Badajoz, but Al-Mu'tamid and the knights of Seville held their ground, though surrounded."],["Alfonso attacks the Almoravids with Al-Mu'tamid","Alfonso then turned on the Almoravid horsemen supporting Al-Mu'tamid, the contingent of Dawud ibn Aisha. The pressure on Dawud and his knights grew."],["Sir's battalion breaks into the Castilian army","Dawud ibn Aisha sent word to Yusuf ibn Tashfin, who dispatched a battalion under Sir ibn Abi Bakr. It broke into the heart of the Castilian army and linked up with Al-Mu'tamid's forces."],["Alfonso reaches the tents and the vanguard gives way","Alfonso kept up the pressure until he reached the Almoravid tents and breached the trench round them. Al-Mu'tamid's Andalusian forces were driven back, and the Castilians went after them."],["The reserve strikes the Castilian camp","While the Castilians chased Al-Mu'tamid, Yusuf ibn Tashfin brought up his reserve and attacked their camp. The Castilian horses panicked at the camels, which he set ablaze, and the camp's remaining men fled towards Alfonso."],["Alfonso halts his pursuit and falls back","Alfonso learned of the chaos in his camp and gave up the chase of Al-Mu'tamid, withdrawing to save his stronghold."],["Al-Mu'tamid orders an attack and Sir charges","Al-Mu'tamid ordered his companions to attack as Alfonso fell back, and Sir ibn Abi Bakr and his men charged. The soldiers who had fled to Badajoz came back into the fight."],["The Takrur cavalry charge","Yusuf ibn Tashfin ordered 4,000 black cavalry from Takrur to charge. Their charge destroyed the Christian cavalry."],["Alfonso retreats to a hill and escapes at night","As the sun began to set, Alfonso retreated with a small contingent to a nearby hill and later escaped in the dark."]]],
  "Battle of Salamis": ["battle-of-salamis","fb30e65715",[["The Persians block the southern exit","The Allied fleet lay off Salamis, the Athenians on the left and the Spartans probably on the right. The Persian fleet was sent out to block the exit from the straits, and about 400 troops stood on Psyttaleia."],["The Persians pivot into the straits","The Persians seem to have swung their fleet round off Cape Vavari, from a line across the exit to a line running north to south. The Phoenicians were on the right by Mount Aigaleo and the Ionians on the left."],["The Allies back their ships away","As the Persians came on, cramped and disordered in the narrow water, the Allies seemed to back away as if in fear. Plutarch gave the reason as a better position and time to wait for the early morning wind."],["One ship shoots forward to ram","While the Allies were backing water, a single ship shot forward to ram the nearest Persian vessel. The Athenians claimed it as the ship of Ameinias of Pallene, and the Aeginetans as one of theirs."],["The whole Greek line follows","The whole Greek line followed the first ship and made straight for the Persian battle line, which had become disorganised and cramped in the narrow water."],["The Phoenicians are pushed back and the centre is split","The first Persian line fouled the second and third behind it. The leaderless Phoenician squadrons were pushed back against the coast, many running aground, and in the centre a wedge of Greek ships split the Persian fleet in two."],["The Persian fleet begins to retreat","The Persian fleet began to retreat towards Phalerum, the centre and the Ionians turning back towards the exit from the straits."],["The Aeginetans ambush the retreating ships","According to Herodotus the Aeginetans ambushed the Persians as they tried to leave the straits. The remaining Persian ships limped back to the harbour of Phalerum and the shelter of the Persian army."],["Aristides destroys the garrison on Psyttaleia","After the Persian fleet had limped back, the Athenian general Aristides took a detachment across to Psyttaleia to slaughter the garrison that Xerxes had left there."]]],
  "Battle of San Jacinto": ["battle-of-san-jacinto","679af8c988",[["The armies face each other across the grass","Houston drew up the Texian line with the second regiment on the left, Burleson in the centre, the artillery and Millard's companies on the right and the cavalry on the extreme right. The Mexican troops lay behind breastworks in their camp."],["The cavalry go wide and the guns move up","The Texian cavalry was sent to the Mexican far left, and the artillery advanced through the tall grass to within 200 yards of the breastworks."],["The Twin Sisters open the battle","The Texian guns, within 200 yards of the Mexican breastworks, fired the Twin Sisters at 4:30 and began the battle."],["The Texians swarm over the breastworks","After a single volley the Texians broke ranks and swarmed over the breastworks to fight hand to hand. The Mexican soldiers were taken by surprise, and Santa Anna and his officers shouted conflicting orders."],["The Texian infantry take the woodland and the breastwork","The Texian infantry advanced without halt until they held the woodland and the Mexican breastwork. The right wing of Burleson's force and the left wing of Millard's took the breastwork."],["The Mexican troops abandon the camp and flee","Within 18 minutes the Mexican soldiers abandoned their campsite and fled. Santa Anna escaped towards Vince's Bridge."],["Pursuit to Peggy Lake","Many Mexican soldiers retreated through the marsh to Peggy Lake, where Texian riflemen stationed themselves on the banks and shot at anything that moved. The killing lasted for hours."]]],
  "Battle of Sedan (1870)": ["battle-of-sedan-1870","6c210b2d04",[["The French held a ring round Sedan","MacMahon placed the 7th Corps on the north-west crest, the 1st Corps facing east and the 12th Corps in Bazeilles, with the 5th Corps in reserve in the centre. The Germans closed a ring round the north-west, west and east."],["The Bavarians storm Bazeilles","A Bavarian brigade crossed the Meuse on pontoon bridges in the morning mist and rushed Bazeilles. The French fought from stone houses while Bavarian guns shelled the buildings."],["The 8th Division arrives and Bazeilles is stormed again","The Prussian 8th Infantry Division arrived at 08:00. Von der Tann had not been able to bring artillery to bear from long range, so he committed his last brigade to storm the town, supported by guns across the Meuse."],["Wimpffen attacks the Saxons at La Moncelle","Ducrot ordered a retreat but Wimpffen overruled him and threw his forces against the Saxons at La Moncelle. The French rallied briefly, drove back the artillery around the village and pressed the Saxons."],["The French counter-attack collapses","With Bazeilles taken and fresh waves of Prussian troops arriving, the French counter-attack began to collapse. The 8th Infantry Division was sent to reinforce the fighting at La Moncelle."],["The 22nd Division turns Douay's left flank","Douay's left flank was dug in above Floing and its fire kept the Germans out of the village. By 13:00 the French artillery there had been defeated and the German 22nd Division turned the flank, defeating French counterattacks."],["French cavalry charge at Floing","Ducrot sent Margueritte's cavalry to open an escape route with three attacks on Floing, where the XI Corps stood. Margueritte was mortally wounded in the first charge and German infantry fire defeated the other two."],["German infantry reach the Calvaire and the French break","By 14:00 German infantry stood on the Calvaire and attacked from all sides. The 7th Corps broke, seeking refuge in the Bois de la Garenne or Sedan, and the 1st Corps was routed by Saxon and Guards artillery."],["The Guards take the Bois de la Garenne","The Bois de la Garenne was under German artillery fire from several sides. When the Prussian Guards infantry captured the wood at 14:30, the French troops inside it surrendered."],["A last attack after the white flag","Napoleon III hoisted a white flag on the walls of Sedan. Wimpffen refused to surrender and launched one more attack, which the Germans defeated."]]],
  "Battle of Shanghai": ["battle-of-shanghai","3d1148ff05",[["The first Chinese attack is halted","The 88th Division attacked the Japanese positions in downtown Shanghai in the late afternoon. Direct fire from the entrenched Japanese and bombardment by the Third Fleet in the Huangpu halted it."],["Operation Iron Fist","The 88th and 87th Divisions shelled the Japanese positions and then charged with infantry. Poor coordination between infantry and artillery and the shelling from the ships kept them from a major breakthrough, and by 18 August the attack was called off."],["The 36th Division attacks towards the docks","The newly arrived 36th Division was sent in on 19 August, half of it towards the Hueishan docks on the northern side of the Huangpu, to cut the Japanese forces in two. It fought through the streets under fire from the buildings and from the Third Fleet."],["Chinese tanks reach Broadway but the wharf holds","The Japanese were pushed back towards the docks, and by 21 August Chinese tanks had reached Broadway, the last street before the Huangpu. The Japanese held the high walls of the wharf and the Chinese could not finish the breakthrough."],["Japanese troops land on the coast","The Japanese 3rd and 11th Divisions landed on the north-east coast under naval bombardment, and the Chinese brought troops from the city to meet them. The front line was lengthened from the city to the coast."],["Baoshan falls","Chinese troops held the walled town of Baoshan against several Japanese assaults. The Japanese surrounded it, a tank assault with air support broke through the gate, and the town fell on 6 September."],["The fight for Luodian","The Chinese concentrated around Luodian and the Japanese came inland against them with naval gunfire, tanks and aircraft. The Chinese were bled almost dry and gave up the town by the end of September."],["Liujiahang and Gujiazhai fall","The Japanese 3rd Division attacked the Liujiahang and Gujiazhai positions on the right wing of the Chinese group army, while the 11th Division pursued it. After days of attacks and counterattacks the Chinese retreated."],["The Japanese reach the Wusong Creek","The Chinese fell back to the south bank of the Wusong Creek, and the Japanese attacked across it. The line was breached after two days at one bridge, but the Japanese met further defences behind it."],["The Guangxi counteroffensive","The Guangxi Army joined the line and counterattacked to retake the banks of the creek. It was poorly coordinated and bogged down, and the Japanese answered with artillery, tanks and poison gas."],["Dachang falls and the Chinese leave the city","The Japanese pushed on to Dachang and took it. The Chinese then withdrew from downtown Shanghai, which they had held for almost three months, to a line behind the Suzhou Creek."],["The Sihang Warehouse is held","A battalion of the 88th Division, some 411 men under Xie Jinyuan, made a stand in the Sihang Warehouse on the Suzhou Creek. Its thick walls and the nearness of the International Settlement nullified Japanese firepower, and several attacks were beaten back."],["The Japanese cross the Suzhou Creek","On 30 October the Japanese crossed the Suzhou Creek, and the Chinese troops were in danger of encirclement. The Japanese 3rd Division began landing on the east side of the creek on 31 October."],["The 10th Army lands at Jinshanwei","On 5 November the Japanese 10th Army, made up of units diverted from the Battle of Taiyuan, landed at Jinshanwei on Hangzhou Bay, a position held by only two companies of the 63rd Division."]]],
  "Battle of Stalingrad": ["battle-of-stalingrad","a32fcb9dec",[["The 6th Army reaches Stalingrad","The 6th Army reached the outskirts of Stalingrad in pursuit of the Soviet 62nd and 64th Armies, which fell back into the city. The 4th Panzer Army had turned northwards to help take the city from the south. Romanian armies guarded the German flanks."],["The attack on the city itself begins","The German attack on the city overran the small hill where the 62nd Army headquarters stood. The railway station was captured and the Germans advanced far enough to threaten the Volga landing stage."],["The Germans hold the south, the Soviets the centre and north","The Germans made slow but steady progress through the city and by 27 September held its southern part. The Soviets held the centre and the north, and with them the ferries to their supplies on the east bank of the Volga."],["The factory assault splits the 62nd Army","After an intense bombardment the German assault groups attacked towards the Stalingrad Tractor Factory. The forward group reached the Volga, splitting the 62nd Army into two."],["The Germans push the defenders to the river","The Germans advanced into the city at great cost and pushed the Soviet defenders back into narrow zones along the west bank of the Volga."],["Operation Uranus overruns the Romanian 3rd Army","Three Soviet armies struck the northern flank of the 6th Army. The Romanian 3rd Army, with thin lines and outnumbered and poorly equipped, was overrun."],["A second attack comes from the south","A second Soviet offensive of two armies was launched south of Stalingrad against the Romanian 4th Army Corps. The Romanian infantry were overrun by large numbers of tanks."],["The ring closes at Kalach","The two Soviet forces raced west and met at Kalach, sealing the ring around Stalingrad. The 6th Army and the 4th Panzer Army were left inside it."],["The relief force advances from Kotelnikovo","Operation Winter Storm was left with the LVII Panzer Corps around Kotelnikovo, supported by the rest of the 4th Panzer Army. By 18 December it had pushed to within 48 km of the 6th Army. The trapped army made no attempt to break out or link up."],["The relief attempt is abandoned","The attempt to relieve Stalingrad was given up. Manstein's forces, including the LVII Panzer Corps, switched to the defensive."],["Operation Koltso begins","Operation Koltso opened on 10 January 1943 with a very large bombardment of the German positions. The Germans retreated from the suburbs of Stalingrad to the city."],["The German forces are split into two pockets","The German forces inside Stalingrad were split into two pockets, one north and one south of Mamayev Kurgan. The northern pocket was cut off from telephone communication with Paulus in the southern pocket."],["The southern and central pockets collapse","The southern pocket collapsed on 31 January, and the central pocket under Heitz surrendered the same day. The northern pocket under Strecker held out for two more days."],["The northern pocket surrenders","On 2 February General Strecker learned that one of his officers had gone to the Soviets to negotiate terms, and the northern pocket surrendered. The 6th Army, having exhausted its ammunition and food, had capitulated."]]],
  "Battle of Tarawa": ["battle-of-tarawa","731b3fd496",[["The warships bombard Betio","Warships and aircraft shelled the island for about three hours before the landing. The Marines waited in the lagoon to land on the north beaches, while the defenders sat in their pillboxes and firing pits."],["The landing is pinned down at the sea wall","The tide was too low for the Higgins boats, and only the tracked LVTs crossed the reef. They could not clear the sea wall, so the first waves were pinned against it. Defenders from the south beaches were shifted to the north."],["By nightfall the line is half-way across","The arrival of tanks started the line moving on Red 3 and the end of Red 2. By nightfall it was about half-way across the island, only a short distance from the main runway."],["Ryan's Orphans clear the western end","Major Ryan gathered remnants of his company with Marines and sailors from other waves and two Sherman tanks, and took them to a more lightly defended part of Green Beach. With naval gunfire they cleared the western end of the island."],["The Marines cross the airstrip","Machine gun posts set up in the night had cut the Marines of Red 2 and Red 3 off from each other. By noon heavy machine guns put them out of action, and by early afternoon the Marines had crossed the airstrip to the south side."],["Marines land on Bairiki","Word came that some defenders were crossing the sandbars to Bairiki. Portions of the 6th Marines landed there, with tanks and pack artillery, to seal off the retreat path. They found a single pillbox, which aircraft set on fire."],["The 1st Battalion, 6th Marines attacks along the south coast","The 1st Battalion, 6th Marines, formed up after landing on Green Beach, attacked at 12:30 and pressed the Japanese east along the south coast. By late afternoon it had reached the eastern end of the airfield and joined the line from Red 3."],["The Japanese counterattack is broken up","That night the Japanese formed up for a counterattack and sent small units to infiltrate the American lines. Concentrated artillery fire broke up the assembling force, and the full assault never took place."],["The banzai charge at night","A large banzai charge was made at 03:00 and met with some success, killing 45 Americans and wounding 128. With support from the destroyers Schroeder and Sigsbee, the Marines killed 325 Japanese attackers."],["The Marines attack both pockets","The 3rd Battalion, 6th Marines relieved the first battalion and attacked the eastern end. The 3rd Battalion, 2nd and the 1st Battalion, 8th closed on the pocket between Red 1 and Red 2 from west and east."],["The island is declared secure","By noon the pocket between Red 1 and Red 2 had been reduced. The 3rd Battalion, 6th Marines reached the eastern tip by 13:00, and the island was declared secure at 13:30."]]],
  "Battle of Thapsus": ["battle-of-thapsus","32cf8a8f61",[["The armies draw up facing each other","Scipio set his legions in three lines in the centre, with cavalry and light infantry on the flanks and elephants in front of them. Caesar also used three lines, with archers and cavalry on the flanks and half of the fifth legion in reserve."],["Caesar's lines advance","A trumpeter of the seventh legion sounded the attack. Caesar saw his right surge forward and ordered a general advance."],["The archers panic the elephants on one flank","Caesar's archers on the right flank attacked the elephants facing them. The animals panicked, turned and trampled their own men."],["The elephants on the other flank charge","The elephants on the other flank charged against Caesar's left. The five cohorts of the fifth legion, placed behind that flank, met the charge."],["The fifth legion turns the elephants back","The legionaries stabbed their pila at the elephants' eyes and weak spots and sounded their trumpets. The beasts turned and ran towards their own lines."],["The elephants crash into their own right flank","The elephants that Caesar's fifth legion had turned back ran towards their own lines and crashed into Scipio's right flank."],["Scipio's left breaks","After the elephants were lost, Scipio began to lose ground. His left broke first, on the wing that faced Caesar's right."],["The rest of Scipio's army follows","The rest of Scipio's army followed his left in breaking. The legions of the centre and the right flank gave way before Caesar's advancing lines."],["The garrison of Thapsus sallies out","During the battle the garrison of Thapsus sallied out and attacked Caesar's siege works. The two legions he had left to continue the siege forced it back."],["The siege legions march south","Having forced the garrison back, the two legions marched south to reinforce the troops fortified opposite Afranius and Juba's camp."],["Afranius' camp is overrun","Together with the troops fortified there, the legions attacked and overran Afranius' camp. They then prepared to attack the Numidians."],["Juba's allied troops leave the site","Before the legions could attack the Numidians, Juba's allied troops abandoned the site, and with that the battle was decided."]]],
  "Battle of Thermopylae": ["battle-of-thermopylae","63a1a30330",[["The Greeks hold the middle gate","Leonidas camped at the narrowest part of the pass, where the Phocians had built a wall, and posted 1,000 Phocians on the heights above. The Persian army came up across the Malian Gulf."],["Xerxes's archers shoot first","On the first day Xerxes ordered 5,000 archers to shoot a barrage of arrows. They shot from at least 100 yards away, and the Greeks' wooden shields and bronze helmets deflected the arrows."],["The Medes attack the Greek line","Xerxes sent 10,000 Medes and Cissians forward, and the Persians launched a frontal assault in waves of about 10,000 men. The Greeks fought in front of the Phocian wall, at the narrowest part of the pass."],["The Immortals fare no better","Xerxes threw the Immortals into a second assault the same day, and they made no headway. On the second day the Persian infantry again attacked the pass without success."],["Xerxes withdraws to his camp","Xerxes stopped the assault and withdrew to his camp, as the Persians had had no more success than on the first day."],["Ephialtes shows the mountain path","A Trachinian named Ephialtes told Xerxes of the mountain path around Thermopylae. Hydarnes set out that evening with the Immortals to encircle the Greeks."],["The Phocians see the Persian column","The Phocians guarding the path became aware of the outflanking Persian column. They retreated to a nearby hill to make their stand, assuming the Persians had come to attack them."],["The Persians bypass the Phocians","Not wishing to be delayed, the Persians merely shot a volley of arrows at the Phocians. They then went on past them with the encirclement of the main Greek force."],["Leonidas lets his allies leave","Learning that his army had been encircled, Leonidas told his allies that they could leave if they wanted to. Many did, and around 2,000 soldiers stayed behind."],["The Greeks sally from the wall","A Persian force of 10,000 light infantry and cavalry charged the front of the Greek formation. The Spartans and Thespians marched out from the wall to meet it in the wider part of the pass. Leonidas was killed."],["The Greeks withdraw to the hill","As the Immortals approached, the Greeks withdrew from the wall and took a stand on a hill behind it. The Spartans and Thespians moved there together."],["The Thebans surrender","The Thebans moved away from their companions and advanced towards the Persians with their hands upraised. A few were killed before their surrender was accepted."],["The last stand on the hill","Xerxes had part of the wall torn down and the hill surrounded, and the Persians shot arrows until every last Greek was dead."]]],
  "Battle of Trafalgar": ["battle-of-trafalgar","62a13dd6e9",[["The fleets close in a ragged line and two columns","The French and Spanish were in a ragged curved line headed north, with the coast behind them. The British came from the west in two columns: Nelson's to the north, Collingwood's to the south."],["Nelson feints at the van and the columns converge","Nelson led his column in a feint toward the van and then turned toward the real point of attack, while Collingwood altered course so that the two columns converged. The foremost British ships came under heavy fire for almost an hour before their guns could bear."],["Royal Sovereign breaks the line","Royal Sovereign outran the rest of the British fleet and came under fire from four allied ships. She then broke the line just astern of the Spanish flagship Santa Ana."],["Victory cuts between Bucentaure and Redoutable","Nelson's flagship Victory cut the line between Villeneuve's flagship Bucentaure and Redoutable. Her broadside through Bucentaure's stern knocked the French flagship out of action, and a general mêlée followed."],["Victory locks masts with Redoutable","Victory engaged the 74-gun Redoutable and locked masts with her. Redoutable's crew, with a strong infantry corps, gathered to board Victory, and Nelson was shot from her mizzentop."],["Temeraire fires on the French crew","As the French prepared to board Victory, Temeraire, second ship of the windward column, came up on the starboard bow of Redoutable. She fired a carronade into the exposed French crew and caused many casualties."],["Redoutable surrenders","Captain Lucas of Redoutable, severely wounded and with 99 fit men left out of 643, surrendered. Bucentaure was isolated by Victory and Temeraire and then engaged by three more British ships."],["The allied centre and rear are overwhelmed","More British ships entered the battle and the allied centre and rear were gradually overwhelmed. The British ships of both columns stayed engaged with them."],["Dumanoir breaks off with four ships","Dumanoir tried to help the collapsing centre. After failing to fight his way through, he led four French ships away from the fighting. The British took 20 allied vessels and lost none."]]],
  "Battle of Tsushima": ["battle-of-tsushima","9d2a5d0fb1",[["The fleets sight each other","The Russians steamed north-north-east in columns, the main group on the right, the left column and the Nikolai I group to the west and the auxiliaries behind. The Japanese main group came down from the north-east heading south-south-west."],["The Japanese turn in sequence and open fire","Mikasa turned about and the ships behind her followed in sequence to head north-north-east. The Russian main group had shifted left to cover the left column, and the Russians opened fire on the Japanese as they turned."],["The Japanese cross ahead of the Russian column","Mikasa and her line turned north-east and then east to cross the T, and the Russian left column turned north-east and then east-south-east in response. Oslyabya was set on fire, and Knyaz Suvorov caught fire and fell away from the line."],["Aleksandr III turns the Russian main group north","With the Japanese 1st division completely overtaking the Russian battleships heading east-south-east, Aleksandr III turned to the north, with Borodino and Oryol following."],["The Japanese 1st division reverses; the 2nd attacks","The 1st division made an immediate left U-turn on the spot to head west-north-west, in reverse order. The 2nd division continued south-east and then south-west, attacking the secondary Russian warships."],["Aleksandr III turns south-east","Aleksandr III, apparently giving up fleeing north, turned south-east, with Borodino and Oryol following. The Russian main group thus reversed its northward course and stood on the opposite heading to the Japanese line."],["The 1st division loses sight of the main group","Nisshin and the five ships made another immediate left U-turn, and the 1st division headed north-east in normal order, with Mikasa first and Nisshin last. The 1st division lost sight of the Russian main group in the smoke and mist."],["The 2nd division finds the main group","The Japanese 2nd division found the Russian main group close to where the 3rd Squadron, the 5th and 6th divisions, was attacking the Russian auxiliaries protected by cruisers."],["The Japanese main group catches the Russian main group","After losing sight of the Russian main group in smoke and mist, the 1st division caught up with it heading north-west and fired on the leading Aleksandr III. The 5th division, part of the 3rd Squadron, had been attacking the Russian auxiliaries."],["Torpedo attacks in the night","The 1st division left the battleground and the 2nd and 4th were ordered to gather in the north, leaving the destroyers and torpedo boats to attack in the dark. The attacks forced the Russians to turn west, and they were dispersed in small groups."],["The Russian remnant is sighted and surrounded","The remaining Russian ships were sighted heading north-east. Tōgō's battleships moved to surround Nebogatov's remaining squadron south of the island of Takeshima, the 1st division approaching from the north."],["Nebogatov surrenders","The Japanese battleships opened main battery fire at 12,000 metres. Nebogatov signalled surrender at 10:34, and Tōgō accepted it at 10:45 once the Russian ships had stopped."]]],
  "Battle of Uhud": ["battle-of-uhud","e689b22e1f",[["The two armies face each other","The Muslims stood on the lower slopes of Uhud with the mountain behind them and 50 archers on a rocky hill to the west. The Meccans formed facing them, with the main body in the middle, a wing on each side and their camp behind."],["Abu Amir leads the first Meccan charge","The Meccans opened with an initial charge led by Abu Amir, a Medinan exile, which advanced across the ground in front of the Muslim line."],["The charge is thrown back with stones","The Muslims met Abu Amir and his men with a shower of stones. They were forced to retreat to the camps behind the Meccan lines."],["The Meccans are pushed back","The Muslims advanced and drove the Meccan army back. Meccan cavalry tried several times to overrun the Muslim left flank, and the archers on the hill turned them away."],["The archers leave the hill","The Muslims pierced the Meccan lines and victory seemed certain. Most of the archers disobeyed Muhammad's order to stay and ran down to join the advance, leaving the flank open."],["Khalid's cavalry attacks the archers on the hill","The archers who had gone down made for the Meccan camp. Khalid ibn al-Walid's cavalry exploited the open flank and attacked the minority of archers who had refused to leave the hill."],["The Muslim flank and rear are overrun","From the hill the Meccans were able to overrun the Muslim flank and rear. Confusion followed and numerous Muslims were killed."],["The Muslims are broken and regroup higher up","Confusion followed and many Muslims were killed, Hamza among them. After hand-to-hand fighting many fled and regrouped higher up the slopes of Uhud, with the Meccan cavalry coming after them."],["Umar's counter-charge clears the slopes","Umar ibn al-Khattab rallied troops at the base of the mountain and led a counter-charge on the Prophet's instruction. It drove Khalid's forces off the slopes, and the Meccan cavalry could not climb them after the Muslims, so the fighting ceased."],["A small group is cut off on the way to Medina","A small group of Muslims was cut off and tried to make its way back to Medina, though many of them were killed."]]],
  "Battle of Verdun": ["battle-of-verdun","e6f696f4d7",[["The German guns open on the east bank","After delays from fog, rain and wind, a ten-hour bombardment began on the right bank, and the German corps attacked at 4:00 p.m. French XXX Corps held the north and north-east of the salient."],["The Germans take Bois des Caures","German troops advanced about 5 km and took the wood after two French battalions had held it for two days. The French XXX Corps was forced out of its second line of defence."],["A German party enters Fort Douaumont","Infantry reached their objectives in under twenty minutes, and two parties made for the fort. One of about 100 men found the ditch bunkers unmanned and took prisoner the small maintenance crew inside."],["The attack widens to the west bank","Falkenhayn lent two divisions from the reserve, and the German VI and X Reserve Corps attacked towards Mort-Homme and Côte 304 to silence the French guns across the river. Those guns went on firing into the Germans on the east bank."],["The French attack Fort Douaumont","After a bombardment begun on 17 May, the French attacked the fort. The 129th Infantry Regiment reached it in a few minutes and got in through the west and south sides, and by nightfall about half of the fort was recaptured."],["The French in the fort are cut off","The 34th Division was sent to reinforce the French in the fort, but the attempt failed. German reserves cut off the French troops inside and forced them to surrender, and 1,000 French prisoners were taken."],["The top of Fort Vaux is occupied","The German attack shifted back to the right bank, south of Fort Douaumont. After a final assault by about 10,000 men on 1 June, the top of Fort Vaux was occupied on 2 June."],["The garrison of Fort Vaux surrenders","Fighting went on underground until the garrison ran out of water. The 574 survivors surrendered on 7 June, and when news reached Verdun the Line of Panic was occupied."],["Gas shells fall on the French guns","German artillery fired over 116,000 Diphosgene gas shells at French artillery positions. They caused over 1,600 casualties and silenced many of the French guns."],["The German salient towards Fort Souville","At 5:00 a.m. the Germans attacked on a front of 5 km and drove a salient of 3 by 2 km into the French defences. They came within 5 km of the Verdun citadel before the French recaptured Chapelle Sainte-Fine and the advance stopped."],["The attack on Fort Souville","Three German divisions attacked, but the infantry bunched on the path to the fort and came under French artillery fire and under sixty machine-gunners who had come out of the fort. Thirty men reached the top on 12 July."],["The French retake Fort Douaumont","After a six-day bombardment French divisions attacked on 20 October behind a creeping barrage. The Germans had partly evacuated Fort Douaumont, and French marines and colonial infantry recaptured it on 24 October."],["The Germans evacuate Fort Vaux","An attempt on Fort Vaux failed, but the heaviest French artillery bombarded it for a week. On 2 November the Germans evacuated the fort, and by 5 November the French were back at the front line of 24 February."]]],
  "Battle of Vienna (1683)": ["battle-of-vienna-1683","c34da7606c",[["The Ottoman army attacks before the allies are deployed","The Ottoman army attacked early, seeking to disrupt the allied deployment. The imperial troops on the left and in the centre met the attack while the Polish troops were still coming into place."],["The imperial army counterattacks in the north","The Germans were the first to counterattack. After heavy fighting and several Ottoman counterattacks, the imperial army took the fortified villages of Nussdorf and Heiligenstadt."],["The Ottomans counterattack and assault the city","By midday the imperial army had done significant damage and come close to a breakthrough. Mustafa Pasha counterattacked with most of his forces but held back elite Janissary and Sipahi units for an assault on the city."],["Polish infantry advances on the Ottoman right","Polish infantry advanced against the Ottoman right flank. Parts of the Ottoman army kept attacking the city, which let the Poles move forward, and by 4:00 pm they held Gersthof."],["The imperial left presses on to the Türkenschanze","The Germans resumed the offensive on the left at 3:30 pm. They met fierce resistance at first, then advanced by 5:00 pm and took Unterdöbling and Oberdöbling, closing on the central Ottoman position."],["Hussars probe the Ottoman line","The Polish cavalry came out of the forest, and a detachment of 120 hussars made a probing charge that showed the Ottomans could be attacked, at the cost of many casualties. The vizier left the Türkenschanze for the main camp."],["The cavalry charges down the hills","The king ordered the cavalry to attack in four contingents, three Polish and one from the Holy Roman Empire. The horsemen charged down the hills towards the Ottoman lines."],["The Ottoman lines break","The charge quickly broke the battle lines of the Ottomans, who were already exhausted and demoralised, and they began to retreat from the battlefield. The hussars of the probing charge left the plan."],["The cavalry rides for the camps","The cavalry headed directly towards the Ottoman camps and the headquarters of Kara Mustafa, while the remaining Viennese garrison sallied out of its defences to join in the assault."]]],
  "Battle of Warsaw (1920)": ["battle-of-warsaw-1920","2fc59db9e7",[["The Red Army's final assault on Warsaw","The Soviet 16th Army began the attack at Radzymin, east of Warsaw, in a frontal assault on the Praga bridgehead, while the 3rd and 15th Armies approached Modlin."],["Radzymin falls and the 5th Army line is broken","Radzymin fell to the Red Army, and the lines of the Polish 5th Army were broken. The 5th Army had to fight three Soviet armies at once, the 3rd, 4th and 15th."],["The 203rd Uhlans raid a Soviet command post","The 203rd Uhlan Regiment broke through the Red Army lines and attacked a Soviet command post. The raid destroyed a radio station of the Soviet 4th Army, which had one station left."],["The 4th Army marches on without orders","The 4th Army was cut off from its headquarters. It continued marching towards Toruń and Płock, unaware of Tukhachevsky's order to turn south."],["Budyonny's army marches on Lwów, not Warsaw","The Soviet High Command ordered the 1st Cavalry Army to advance on Warsaw from the south. It marched on Lwów instead and missed the battle, leaving only a weak Mazyr Group on the southern flank."],["The Polish 5th Army counterattacks in the north","The 5th Army crossed the Wkra and fought the Soviet 3rd and 15th Armies at Nasielsk. The Soviet advance towards Warsaw and Modlin was halted at the end of 15 August."],["The 5th Army pushes the Soviets back","Sikorski's 5th Army pushed exhausted Soviet units away from Warsaw, with tanks, armoured cars and two armoured trains in support. It advanced about 30 km a day and disrupted the Soviet enveloping move in the north."],["The Assault Group breaks through the Mazyr Group","Piłsudski's Assault Group marched north from the Wieprz and met almost no opposition. It routed the Mazyr Group and cut the supply lines of the Soviet 16th Army."],["The Soviet retreat order comes too late","Tukhachevsky ordered the Red Army to retreat and regroup, but his orders arrived too late or not at all. The 3rd Cavalry Corps continued to advance towards Pomerania, with its lines endangered by the Polish 5th Army."],["The 1st Legions Division sets out from Lubartów","The Polish 1st Legions Infantry Division began a forced march from Lubartów towards Białystok to cut the enemy's retreat. It was on the move for up to 21 hours a day."],["The Soviet 16th Army is cut off near Białystok","The 1st Legions Division covered 262 km in six days and engaged the enemy twice. Its rapid advance let it intercept the 16th Army and cut it off near Białystok, and most of its troops surrendered."],["The northern Soviet armies join the general rout","The 15th Army, which had tried to cover the retreat of the 4th Army, was defeated twice and joined the rout. By 21 August all organised Soviet resistance had ceased."]]],
  "Battle of Waterloo": ["battle-of-waterloo","be2cbfc8bb",[["The armies face each other across the Brussels road","Wellington drew up his infantry just behind the crest of the ridge, with Hougoumont held ahead of his right and his heavy cavalry out of sight behind the ridge. The French formed on the slopes of another ridge to the south, symmetrically about the road."],["Hougoumont is attacked and the grande batterie fires","Reille's corps pressed in on Hougoumont, which the Guards light companies and others held, and the fighting there lasted all afternoon. The 80 guns of the grande batterie fired on Wellington's centre."],["I Corps advances against Wellington's left","D'Erlon advanced some 14,000 men against Wellington's left wing, where about 6,000 stood in the first line. His divisions reached the sunken Ohain road and pushed back Bylandt's brigade."],["The British heavy cavalry charge and I Corps breaks","Uxbridge ordered his two heavy brigades, formed unseen behind the ridge, to charge. They crossed the crest and went downhill, and I Corps was driven back across the valley in rout."],["Some of the cavalry ride on to the French guns","The British horsemen lost their cohesion and found themselves before the main French lines, on blown horses. Some attacked the guns of the grande batterie and put many of them out of action."],["The French cavalry counter-charge","Napoleon ordered a counter-attack by cuirassiers and lancers. They caught the scattered British cavalry in the valley and drove the Union Brigade from it, with very heavy losses."],["The French cavalry attack the centre","Ney tried to break Wellington's centre with cavalry alone, sending in his reserve cavalry between Hougoumont and La Haye Sainte. Wellington's infantry formed squares against them."],["La Haye Sainte falls to the French","Rallied elements of I Corps, led by the 13th Légère, renewed the attack on La Haye Sainte and took it once the German garrison's ammunition ran out. The French then brought skirmishers and guns up towards the centre."],["The Prussians arrive and Lobau is sent to stop them","Bülow's IV Corps came up on the French right with Plancenoit as its objective. Napoleon sent Lobau's corps to stop it."],["The Young Guard fights for Plancenoit","Napoleon sent the Young Guard to reinforce Lobau, who was hard pressed. It secured Plancenoit and was driven out, and two battalions of the Middle or Old Guard then recaptured the village."],["Zieten's I Corps attacks the French right","Zieten's Prussian I Corps came up in strength north of La Haie and attacked the French troops before Papelotte. By 19:30 the French position was bent into a rough horseshoe."],["The Imperial Guard attacks and I Corps breaks","Napoleon committed the Imperial Guard against Wellington's centre, led by Ney. As it went forward the rest of d'Erlon's corps broke and fled."],["The Guard retreats and Wellington advances","The Guard was repulsed and the last of it retreated. Wellington signalled a general advance and his army went forward against the retreating French."]]],
  "Battle of Yamen": ["battle-of-yamen","e73e4a713b",[["The chained Song fleet and the four Yuan parts","The Song ships lay chained in a long string in the bay, with the emperor's boat in the middle. Zhang Hongfan split his ships into four parts: three for the Song's east, north and south sides, and one under his own command a short way off."],["The north flank is repulsed","The Yuan north squadron engaged the Song fleet first and was driven back. The Song ships stayed chained where they lay, and the rest of the Yuan force held its places."],["Zhang Hongfan attacks from the front","The Yuan played festive music, which led the Song to think they were feasting and to lower their guard. At noon Zhang Hongfan attacked from the front, with more soldiers hidden under large pieces of cloth."],["The horn sounds and arrows fall on the Song ships","Once Zhang Hongfan's boats neared the Song fleet, the Yuan sounded the horn of battle and the soldiers under the cloth were seen. Waves of arrows hit the Song ships, which lost seven ships at once."],["Close fighting on the chained ships","The ill and weakened Song soldiers were no match for the Yuan troops in close combat, and the chaos made command impossible. The chained Song ships could not support the middle or retreat."],["Zhang Shijie tries to break out","The chained Song ships could neither support the middle nor retreat, and command broke down. Zhang Shijie cut about a dozen ships from the formation in an attempt to save the emperor."],["The Yuan reach the emperor's boat","The Yuan advanced quickly to the centre and to Zhao Bing, killing everyone in their way. Lu Xiufu took the boy emperor and jumped into the sea, and both drowned. Zhang Shijie escaped the battle."]]],
  "Battle of Zama": ["battle-of-zama","ef906f35d2",[["The armies draw up facing each other","The Romans put their heavy infantry in the centre in three ranks, with Laelius's cavalry on the left and Masinissa's Numidians on the right. Hannibal set elephants before two lines of infantry, a third line well behind them, and cavalry on each flank."],["The Numidian horsemen skirmish on the flank","The first clashes came on the Carthaginian left and the Roman right, between the Numidian cavalry on the Carthaginian side and those siding with the Romans. Each force sent detachments to hurl javelins at the other and then withdraw."],["The elephants charge with two lines behind them","Hannibal ordered his 80 elephants to charge the Roman infantry, with the whole of his first two infantry lines moving forward in support. His third line stayed where it stood."],["The velites and the bugles turn the elephants","The velites moved forward, threw javelins at the elephants and fell back, and the Roman heavy infantry sounded their bugles. Many elephants bolted, most into the gaps the Romans had left between their maniples."],["Masinissa routs the Numidian cavalry","Elephants that fled trampled through the Numidian cavalry on the Carthaginian side and disordered it. Masinissa ordered a charge, which routed those horsemen, and his force pursued them."],["Laelius routs the African cavalry","Elephants that fled disorganised the Carthaginian cavalry on the other wing. Laelius ordered his cavalry to charge, and the Carthaginian cavalry was swept from the field with the Roman cavalry close behind."],["The first lines of infantry clash","With elephants and cavalry gone from the field, all three Roman ranks and the first two Carthaginian ranks advanced and met in close, hand-to-hand fighting. The Carthaginian third rank, the veterans from Italy, remained in place."],["The Carthaginian front line breaks","The Romans' superior weapons and organisation told, though the hastati took further heavy losses, and the Carthaginian front rank broke and fled. The second rank would not let them through, so they escaped round its flanks."],["The second line pushes the Romans back","The hastati then attacked the Carthaginian second rank. Its African spearmen fought fanatically, and the Romans were pushed back in disorder."],["The Romans commit the principes","The Romans were forced to commit their second line, the principes, to the fight. Even they struggled to hold the line at first, but their reinforcement was enough to break the Carthaginian second line."],["The second line flees and the hastati pursue","The Carthaginian second line broke and fled, pursued impetuously by the hastati. The Carthaginian third line did not move."],["Both sides re-form into single long lines","The Romans recalled the hastati and formed one line, with the principes and triarii at the sides. The Carthaginians rallied survivors of their first two lines to lengthen the veterans' line, so it outflanked the Romans."],["The two single lines charge each other","Having re-formed, the two lines charged each other with, in Polybius's words, the greatest fire and fury. The fight continued for some time and neither side gained the advantage."],["The Roman cavalry returns and takes the line in the rear","Masinissa's and Laelius's cavalry returned to the battlefield at more or less the same time. The Carthaginian infantry, fiercely engaged to their front, could not prevent a charge into their rear, and their line collapsed."]]],
  "Battle of al-Qadisiyyah": ["battle-of-al-qadisiyyah","45e325eec3",[["Both armies form up in four divisions","The Sasanian army crossed the canal before dawn and drew up in four divisions with the river at its back. The Rashidun army stood in four corps about 500 metres away, with Muslim cavalry at the rear of each."],["Elephants and archers press the Muslim right","The Persian left wing attacked the Muslim right wing with heavy arrow fire, and the elephants led the charge. Jareer's cavalry was stopped by the Sasanian heavy cavalry, and the Muslim infantry began to fall back."],["The Muslim right counterattacks","Reinforcing infantry from the right centre and a cavalry regiment under Al-Ash'ath joined the right wing. The infantry attacked from the front and the cavalry from the flank, and the Sasanian left wing retreated."],["The Persian right and its elephants drive back the left","Rustam then sent his right centre and right wing forward after heavy arrow fire, with the elephants in the lead. The Muslim cavalry on the left, already in panic at the elephants, was driven back."],["The elephants are turned back","Asim's archers shot the men on the elephants and his infantry cut the saddle girths. The elephants were withdrawn, the Persian right centre and right wing retreated, and the Muslims counterattacked."],["Sa'd orders a second counterattack","By afternoon the Persian attacks on the Muslim left had been beaten back. Sa'd ordered another counterattack and the Muslim cavalry charged from the flanks. Rustam went into the fray himself and the attacks were repulsed."],["The elephants are driven off the field","On the third day the Muslims blinded the elephants and severed their trunks until they were driven off. The frightened elephants rushed through the Sasanian ranks towards the river, and by noon none were left on the field."],["Qa'qa leads the left centre forward","The elephants had been driven from the field on 18 November. On the fourth day the Muslim left centre surged forward and the other corps followed. By noon Qa'qa and his men had pierced the Sasanian centre but could not break the army."],["The Sasanian right wing counterattacks","The Sasanian right wing counterattacked and regained its lost position. The Muslim left wing retreated to its original position, and the left centre, denied its support, also fell back to where it had stood."],["The Sasanian front collapses","After a last stand the Sasanian front gave way. Part of the army retreated in order and the rest in panic towards the river, while Jalinus held the bridgehead and brought the bulk across. The Muslim cavalry pursued."]]],
  "Battle of the Bulge": ["battle-of-the-bulge","fb657bee7f",[["Three German armies face the Ardennes front","The Sixth Panzer Army stood in the north, the Fifth Panzer Army in the middle and the Seventh Army in the south. The U.S. 99th and 2nd Divisions, the 106th Division and VIII Corps held the line opposite them."],["The assault opens and Peiper's group leads the north","The Germans opened with a 90-minute artillery barrage and attacked all along the front. Kampfgruppe Peiper, charged with the main effort, left the Sixth Panzer Army's line late."],["Two regiments of the 106th Division are surrounded","The Fifth Panzer Army surrounded two largely intact regiments of the 106th Division in a pincer movement and forced their surrender."],["The northern shoulder holds","The 99th and 2nd Divisions repelled an advance by volksgrenadier and armoured units of the Sixth Panzer Army. This forced the German spearhead onto less favourable routes further south and slowed its advance."],["Peiper turns south and attacks Stavelot","Peiper turned south to detour round Hünningen and arrived in front of Stavelot. He attacked it on 18 December, but the Americans evacuated a large fuel depot before he could take the town."],["The defenders fall back to Elsenborn Ridge","The 12th SS Panzer Division renewed its advance into Krinkelt, and the defenders were ordered to break contact and abandon the twin villages. They reestablished their positions on Elsenborn Ridge."],["St. Vith slows the Fifth Panzer Army","The defenders of St. Vith, led by the 7th Armored Division, resisted the German attacks and slowed the advance. The 2nd Panzer Division spearheaded the Fifth Panzer Army."],["St. Vith is evacuated","At Montgomery's orders the town of St. Vith was evacuated on 21 December. The American troops fell back to entrenched positions in the area, which presented an obstacle to the German advance."],["The defenders retreat west of the Salm","By 23 December the Germans had shattered the flanks of the defenders and their position became untenable. The American troops were ordered to retreat west of the Salm River."],["The 101st Airborne reaches Bastogne","The struggle for the villages and strong points slowed the German attack enough for the 101st Airborne Division to reach Bastogne by truck on the morning of 19 December."],["Bastogne is cut off","The panzer columns swung past on either side and cut Bastogne off on 20 December, but did not secure the crossroads. By 21 December the Germans had surrounded the town."],["The 2nd Panzer Division reaches Foy-Notre-Dame","On 22 and 23 December German forces reached the woods of Foy-Notre-Dame, a few kilometres ahead of Dinant. On 24 December parts of the 2nd Panzer Division were in sight of the Meuse, the furthest German advance west."],["The 2nd Armored Division attacks the spearheads","The U.S. 2nd Armored Division attacked in an attempt to cut off the spearheads of the 2nd Panzer Division at the Meuse. As a result parts of the 2nd Panzer Division were cut off."],["The 4th Armored Division breaks through to Bastogne","On 26 December the spearhead of the 4th Armored Division, supplemented by the 26th Infantry Division, broke through and opened a corridor to Bastogne. The siege had lasted barely a week."]]],
  "Battle of the Catalaunian Plains": ["battle-of-the-catalaunian-plains","8dd80da2a4",[["The two armies deploy along the ridge","Both sides spent the day deploying their coalition armies. Attila's Huns stood in the centre with their vassals on the wings, while the Romans took the left, the Alans the centre and the Visigoths the right."],["Attila's forces take the right of the ridge","Attila's forces reached the ridge first and held its far right side, before the Visigoths could take that position. The Amali Goths stood forward on the ridge there."],["The Romans take the left and repulse the Gepids","The Romans then arrived on the left side of the ridge and drove back the Gepids as they came up, so that the Romans held the left side of the ridge."],["The Alans and Thorismund secure the centre","The Alans and the Visigoths under Thorismund fought their way up the ridge and took its centre, which they held against Attila."],["The Visigoths try to take the right of the ridge","Theodoric led his Visigoths against the Amali Goths on the right. He was killed in the assault, and the rest of his army did not know of his death."],["Thorismund drives back the Hunnic centre","Thorismund found Attila's place in the Hunnic line and attacked the Hunnic centre, nearly killing Attila. The Hunnic centre was forced to retreat."],["Attila withdraws into his camp","The Hunnic centre fell back, and Attila was forced to seek refuge in his own camp, which he had fortified with wagons."],["Thorismund enters the camp by mistake at night","When night fell, Thorismund, returning towards friendly lines, entered Attila's encampment by mistake and was wounded in the fighting there."]]],
  "Battle of the Granicus": ["battle-of-the-granicus","0690f681b6",[["Both armies drawn up on either side of the river","The Persian cavalry stood along the east bank with the Greek mercenaries behind it. Alexander took the right wing of the Macedonian line and Parmenion the left, and the Persians moved cavalry to their own left when they saw Alexander opposite it."],["Amyntas attacks the Persian left wing","After a moment of silence Alexander ordered Amyntas to attack the Persian left wing, with the squadron of Socrates at the front. The Persians answered the charge with volleys of javelins."],["Amyntas's force is pushed back","Outnumbered, and facing Persians on higher ground at the top of the bank, the vanguard suffered losses and retreated towards Alexander. Alexander now attacked with the remaining Companion cavalry of the right wing."],["Alexander crosses and fights on the far bank","Alexander and his men engaged the Persians and their leaders in a hard-fought fight. Other Macedonian cavalry established a foothold on the bank and reinforced his force."],["The Persian left is forced back where Alexander fights","The Macedonian cavalry and the light infantry interspersed with them forced back the Persian cavalry. This began on the Persian left flank, where Alexander was fighting."],["The Persian centre breaks and both wings flee","Then the centre was broken and the Persian cavalry on both wings fled, leaving the Greek mercenaries at their first position."],["The Greek mercenaries are attacked from all sides","Alexander did not pursue the fleeing cavalry far. The mercenaries, mostly inactive at their original position, were surprised and attacked by the Foot Companions from the front and the cavalry from all other directions. They were soon defeated and only a few escaped."]]],
  "Battle of the Hydaspes": ["battle-of-the-hydaspes","69f42e22dc",[["The armies draw up","The Indians stood with cavalry on both flanks, chariots in front and infantry in the centre with elephants before it. Alexander judged the centre the strongest part and kept his phalanx back while his cavalry prepared to go for the flanks."],["The Macedonian cavalry strikes the Indian flanks","The Dahae horse archers harassed the Indian right-wing cavalry, and the Companions, with Alexander leading, charged the Indian cavalry on the left wing, which was outnumbered."],["The Indian right-wing cavalry is caught from behind","The rest of the Indian cavalry rode over to help the hard-pressed left wing. Coenus's squadrons followed and struck them from the rear, so the Indian horsemen had to face two attacks at once."],["The Indian cavalry is routed","The Indian horsemen tried to form a double phalanx, but the manoeuvre added to their confusion. They were routed and fled towards the safety of their elephants."],["The elephants advance and meet the phalanx","The war elephants advanced and were met by the Macedonian phalanx. They caused heavy losses among the Macedonian foot, trampling and disorganising their dense lines, but the infantry kept up its resistance."],["The Indian cavalry sallies again and is repulsed","While the elephants fought the phalanx, the Indian horsemen attempted another sally. Alexander's cavalry squadrons, which had all massed together, repulsed them once again."],["The elephants are driven back into their own lines","The elephants were repulsed and fled back to their own lines. Many of their mahouts had been struck down, and the maddened animals trampled many of their own infantry and cavalry."],["The phalanx advances and the cavalry charges from behind","The Macedonian foot locked their shields and advanced on the confused enemy mass while the cavalry charged from the rear. The whole Indian army was put to rout."],["Craterus crosses and pursues the fleeing Indians","Meanwhile Craterus and his force in the base camp had succeeded in crossing the river. They arrived at the right moment and conducted a thorough pursuit of the fleeing Indians."]]],
  "Battle of the Little Bighorn": ["battle-of-the-little-bighorn","0e9af08133",[["Reno crosses the river and charges the village","Reno's three troops forded the Little Bighorn at the mouth of Reno Creek on Custer's orders and rode towards the village. Custer's battalion followed the ridges to the east, while Benteen and the pack train were still behind."],["Reno dismounts and the warriors ride out","Reno stopped a few hundred yards short of the village, fearing a trap, and put his men into a skirmish line. The mounted warriors streamed out and rode against the centre and exposed left end of the line."],["The warriors turn Reno's left and he falls back to the trees","Over 500 warriors massed behind a small hill on Reno's left and attacked his left and rear. Reno withdrew into the timber along the river bend, where the warriors pinned his men down."],["Reno's men break for the bluffs","Reno led his men out of the timber in a disorderly rout along the river. They crossed under attack and climbed the bluffs on the far side, leaving wounded behind in the timber."],["Benteen and the pack train join Reno on the bluffs","Benteen's three troops reached Reno's shaken men while they were still climbing from the valley, and McDougall's troop and the pack train came later. The command dug rifle pits and held an all-round defence."],["Weir's troops advance to Weir Peaks","Captain Weir and Company D moved out to contact Custer, and troops D, M and K advanced a mile north to Weir Peaks and dismounted. From there Weir could see the size of the camps and warriors moving about in the distance."],["Weir's troops are attacked and fall back to Reno Hill","The three troops on Weir Peaks were attacked by warriors, who increasingly came from the finished fight with Custer. On Reno's order they withdrew, and all seven companies fell back to Reno Hill."],["Yates' companies appear at the mouth of Medicine Tail Coulee","With Reno driven from the village, Custer moved north and a wing of his battalion under Yates went down to the river at the Minneconjou Ford. Hundreds of warriors left the fight with Reno to protect the village."],["Custer's battalion is overwhelmed on Custer Hill","Custer's companies moved to Custer Hill, where the warriors, led by Crazy Horse by the Lakota account, charged from the northeast. Custer's remaining troops, E, F and half of C, were soon killed."]]],
  "Battle of the Nile": ["battle-of-the-nile","b7c0dbe873",[["The French line at anchor, the British closing","The French thirteen ships of the line lay at anchor along the edge of the shoals, with the van near Aboukir Island and Orient at the centre. The British fleet came up in the evening, with Swiftsure and Alexander out of sight to the west."],["Goliath and Zealous cross the head of the line","Foley saw a gap between Guerrier and the shoal and took Goliath through it, and Zealous followed. Orion, Theseus and Audacious also passed inside the line, which was ready to fight only on its seaward side."],["The seaward ships anchor and the centre is engaged","Vanguard, Minotaur and Defence anchored on the seaward side of the French van at 18:40, which was now caught on both sides. Bellerophon and Majestic passed the melee and went on to the unengaged French centre."],["Culloden runs onto the shoal","Culloden, the last ship of the British line, sailed too close to Aboukir Island in the growing darkness and stuck fast on the shoal. Her boats, the brig Mutine and Leander could not move her."],["Bellerophon drifts out and the last ships join","Bellerophon, battered by Orient, cut her anchor cables at 20:20 and drifted away from the fight. Swiftsure and Alexander came up in the dark and opened fire on Orient and Franklin."],["The French van surrenders","Guerrier, Conquérant and Spartiate struck their colours, and Aquilon was dismasted and its officers surrendered at 21:25. Peuple Souverain had already cut its cable and drifted south, and surrendered in the night."],["Fire breaks out on Orient","A fire was seen on Orient's lower decks and Swiftsure fired into the blaze, which spread through the stern. The nearest British ships edged away, and Tonnant, Heureux and Mercure cut their cables and drifted south."],["Orient explodes","At 22:00 the fire reached the magazines and Orient was destroyed by a massive explosion. Flaming wreckage fell over the nearest ships, and for ten minutes afterwards there was no firing."],["Franklin fights on and surrenders","Franklin restarted the engagement by firing on Swiftsure. Isolated and battered, she was dismasted, and the wounded Blanquet surrendered to the combined fire of Swiftsure and Defence."],["Tonnant fights on alone and drifts south","By midnight only Tonnant remained engaged, fighting Majestic until about 03:00, when she was a dismasted hulk. She then drifted southwards away from the action towards Villeneuve's division."],["Firing begins again at dawn","As the sun rose, firing broke out again between the French southern division and the battered Alexander and Majestic."],["Heureux and Mercure surrender on the shoal","Heureux and Mercure had cut their cables to escape the exploding Orient and had drifted onto the shoal. Alexander and three other British ships attacked them, and both surrendered within minutes."],["Tonnant is driven ashore and the French rear stands out","Villeneuve brought most of the surviving French ships to the mouth of the bay at 11:00. The dismasted Tonnant could not make the speed and was driven ashore by her crew, while the others stood out to sea."]]],
  "Battle of the Plains of Abraham": ["battle-of-the-plains-of-abraham","eee495810e",[["Wolfe's line forms on the plateau","Wolfe's army formed a line across the Plains, its right on the bluff above the St Lawrence and its left on a bluff and wood above the St Charles. Canadian militia and native sharpshooters engaged the left flank from the trees and scrub."],["The British left takes the houses and mill","On the left wing Townshend's regiments exchanged fire with the militia in the scrub and captured a small collection of houses and a gristmill to anchor the line. The defenders burned several houses as they left, and the smoke masked the British left."],["Montcalm orders a general advance","The French regulars moved against the British line. The British lines held their fire as the French approached."],["The French fire two volleys","The British held their fire until the French came within about 30 yards, and for two or three minutes both armies waited. The French then fired two disorganised volleys."],["The British fire twice and the French break","The British fired at close range, marched forward a few paces and fired a second general volley, which shattered the attackers and sent them into retreat."],["The French retreat and the Highlanders pursue","British troops fell into a disorganised pursuit of the retreating French. The 78th Fraser Highlanders followed with their swords and met heavy fire from the militia that remained in the trees."],["The Highlanders attack the militia on the flank","Once gathered together, the Highlanders made a separate attack on a large body of Canadians posted on the flank in a small village and a bush of woods. They suffered great loss before driving them back under cover of their cannon."],["Bougainville's column arrives from the rear","Townshend took charge and saw Bougainville's column approaching from the British rear. He formed two battalions from the confused troops and turned them to meet it."],["Bougainville retreats and the French cross the St Charles","Bougainville did not attack and retreated. The rest of Montcalm's army slipped back across the St Charles, the militia holding the bridge."]]],
  "Battle of the Pyramids": ["battle-of-the-pyramids","7b7808a9a9",[["The two armies take their places","The French formed five divisional squares in echelon, Desaix on the right and Bon on the left beside the Nile. Murad Bey fixed his right at fortified Embabeh and his left at Biktil, with the Mamluk cavalry between them."],["The French advance and occupy Biktil","The French moved south in echelon, each division holding its square, and Desaix sent a detachment to occupy the village of Biktil on Murad Bey’s left, where he had placed additional guns."],["Mamluk cavalry charge the squares","Murad meant to hold the French on his fortified flanks and strike their centre with cavalry, and repeated charges were directed against the squares."],["The mass assault of about 15:30","Murad ordered his defterdar, Ayyub Bey, to lead a mass assault. The divisions of Desaix, Reynier and Dugua held firm and repelled the horsemen with musketry and artillery."],["The Mamluks attack Desaix’s detachment","After the assault failed, some of the Mamluks then attempted to attack Desaix’s detached force near Biktil, but without success."],["Bon’s columns storm Embabeh","Near the river Bon’s division formed attack columns and stormed Embabeh. The garrison broke and many fled into the Nile, where hundreds drowned."],["Murad escapes towards Upper Egypt","Murad’s losses were far heavier than the French, with thousands killed or wounded, Ayyub Bey among them. Murad, wounded in the cheek, got away with several thousand cavalry to Upper Egypt."]]],
  "Battle of the Somme": ["battle-of-the-somme","371ada62ce",[["The corps stand along the front","From north to south the British VII, VIII, X, III, XV and XIII Corps and the French Sixth Army faced three corps of the German 2nd Army. The Reserve Army's cavalry waited west of Albert."],["The first day: success in the south, disaster in the north","The French, XIII Corps and XV Corps took their objectives, and a German retreat began south of the Somme. North of the Albert–Bapaume road the attacks failed, or won ground that was mostly lost again."],["The Fourth Army attacks the second position","Four divisions attacked at 3:25 a.m. after a five-minute bombardment on a front of 6,000 yards. Most of the objective was captured, but the attack was not followed up."],["The South Africans hold Delville Wood","The fighting at Delville Wood was an operation to secure the British right flank while the centre advanced. The South African 1st Infantry Brigade held the wood from 15 to 20 July and lost 2,536 men."],["The Reserve Army takes Pozières","The 1st Australian Division of the Reserve Army captured Pozières, the only British success of a general attack on 22 and 23 July. German bombardments and counter-attacks continued until 7 August."],["The Fourth Army captures Guillemont","Guillemont lay on the right flank of the British sector, near the boundary with the French Sixth Army, and the Fourth Army captured it on the first day of the attack."],["Ginchy falls and the French attack","The 16th Division captured the German-held village of Ginchy, which overlooked Combles. The French Sixth Army then made its biggest attack of the battle on 12 September."],["The third general offensive and the attack on Combles","The British attacked an intermediate line and the German third line, while a French attack aimed to encircle Combles. The front line moved forward by 2,500 to 3,500 yards, but no breakthrough came."],["Morval and Thiepval Ridge","The Fourth Army attacked Morval, Gueudecourt and Lesboeufs, held by the German 1st Army, and took them with Combles, while the French made slower progress."],["The Reserve Army attacks Thiepval Ridge","The Reserve Army under Hubert Gough began its first large offensive a day after the attack at Morval. The ridge was well fortified and the defenders fought hard, and co-ordination declined after the first day."],["Le Sars falls and the Reserve Army attacks Regina Trench","Le Sars was captured on 7 October, after which the Fourth Army was reduced to limited operations. The Reserve Army attacked to complete the capture of Regina Trench and Stuff Trench against frequent German counter-attacks."],["The Fifth Army attacks into the Ancre valley","The Fifth Army, as the Reserve Army was now named, attacked into the Ancre valley. Beaumont Hamel and Beaucourt were captured, though the attack on Serre failed. It was the last big British operation of the year."]]],
  "Battle of the Teutoburg Forest": ["battle-of-the-teutoburg-forest","8f19d7cdf2",[["The column strings out through the forest","On the morning of 8 September the Roman army decamped and went on through the area’s thick forest, which slowed movement and strung the army out until the line of march ran 15 to 20 kilometres. The Angrivarii lay hidden at Kalkriese."],["The Bructeri strike the strung-out column","The Bructeri attacked the Roman army along its length and rained javelins on it. It was a brief skirmish, meant to tire the Romans and do the most damage to their supplies."],["The Bructeri withdraw","The Bructeri withdrew quickly, likely taking with them the Cheruscii spies who had been embedded in the Roman army. The skirmish had been brief."],["A downpour halts the Romans and they camp","A torrential downpour began and the Romans, unable to advance, made camp on Varus’s order. A sturdy camp was erected and a war council called. Losses were light, but the baggage trains and scout cavalry were shown to be vulnerable."],["A night march into the second trap","The Romans marched at night to escape and went into another trap set at the foot of Kalkriese Hill. There the open strip they could march along was squeezed to a gap of about 100 metres between the woods and the swampland at the edge of the Great Bog."],["The Romans try to storm the wall","The road was blocked by a trench and an earthen wall built along its side, which let the Germanic alliance attack from cover. The Romans made a desperate attempt to storm the wall and failed."],["Vala rides off with the cavalry","The highest-ranking officer next to Varus, Numonius Vala, abandoned the troops by riding off with the cavalry after the failed attempt on the wall."],["Germanic cavalry overtake Vala","Vala’s retreat was in vain. The Germanic cavalry overtook him and he was killed shortly afterwards, according to Velleius Paterculus."],["The Germanic warriors storm the field","The Germanic warriors stormed the field and cut down the disintegrating Roman forces. Varus killed himself. By later accounts some officers were sacrificed or ransomed and some common soldiers enslaved."]]],
  "Battle of the Trebia": ["battle-of-the-trebia","2e030438e5",[["The Numidians provoke the Romans","Hannibal sent his Numidian cavalry across the river to force back the Roman pickets and provoke a fight. Mago had already taken 1,000 cavalry and 1,000 infantry in the night to an old watercourse, where they hid."],["The Romans ford the Trebia after the Numidians","Sempronius sent out his cavalry, then the velites and then the whole army. The Numidians drew back slowly, and the Romans followed them through the icy, chest-high water of the Trebia."],["The armies form up on the west bank","The Romans organised themselves in battle formation west of the Trebia, facing the Carthaginian army, which had formed up symmetrically. The light infantry of both sides stood between the lines. Mago's force lay hidden in a watercourse to the south."],["The Carthaginian skirmishers drive back the velites","The velites had used many javelins against the cavalry and were unfed, tired and cold, while the Carthaginian slingers outranged them. The Carthaginians got the better of the skirmishing and drove the velites back through the gaps in their heavy infantry."],["The skirmishers harass the Roman cavalry","The Carthaginian light infantry moved towards the flanks of their army and harassed the Roman cavalry with their missiles. The Roman horses were already tired from chasing the Numidians."],["The Roman cavalry flees and the infantry lines close","On each wing 5,000 Carthaginian horse charged the 2,000 Roman horse, which fled back over the Trebia. Most of the Carthaginian cavalry pursued them while the heavy infantry lines met in the centre. The skirmishers fell back behind their cavalry."],["Carthaginian skirmishers go round the exposed flank","The Carthaginian light infantry moved forward and round the Roman flank, which the flight of the cavalry had left open. The velites turned to face them and many allied infantry on the flanks turned too, which slowed their push on the front."],["Mago's force strikes the Roman left rear","Mago's men came down the watercourse unnoticed and charged the velites, who were already holding off the Carthaginian light infantry. The velites held their formation."],["The Carthaginian cavalry returns to the Roman rear","Increasing numbers of Carthaginian cavalry broke off the pursuit, came back and attacked the Roman rear. Under this strain the allied infantry on the flanks and the velites began to break up."],["The Roman centre breaks through the Gauls","The Roman infantry in the centre routed the 8,000 Gauls facing them and broke through the middle of the Carthaginian army. Behind them the rest of the Roman army had dissolved."],["Ten thousand Romans reach Placentia","Sempronius ordered the infantry away from the field. Keeping its formation, 10,000 of them recrossed the Trebia and reached Placentia. The Carthaginians cut down the partly surrounded rest of the Roman army."]]],
  "Battle of the Yarmuk": ["battle-of-the-yarmuk","f813f020a2",[["The two armies stand facing each other","The Byzantines faced east and the Muslims west, on fronts of about 13 and 12 kilometres. Each side had four sections, and each Byzantine army kept its cavalry in the rear."],["A third of the Byzantine infantry attacks","After duels between champions, Vahan ordered a limited attack with a third of his infantry to test the Muslim line. The assault lacked determination, and neither side gained ground."],["The Byzantine left wing drives back the Muslim right","The Byzantine centre did not press hard, meaning to pin the Muslim centre in place. Qanatir, with mainly Slavs on the Byzantine left, attacked in force, and the Muslim infantry on the right wing had to retreat."],["Khalid's guard strikes the Byzantine left wing","Khalid sent the mobile guard against the southern flank of the Byzantine left wing while the Muslim right wing attacked from the front. The Byzantine left wing gave up the ground it had won and Amr regained his position."],["Gregory overruns the Muslim left wing","The Byzantines were more numerous on the south, where the Muslim left wing under Yazid had no help from the mobile guard. Its positions were overrun and its soldiers fell back towards their camps."],["Khalid's guard relieves the Muslim left","Khalid ordered the mobile guard to relieve the battered left wing, and it attacked Gregory's flank. Under attack from the front and the flank, the Byzantines fell back, but more slowly because they had to keep their formation."],["Qanatir presses the Muslim right again","Qanatir led his Slavs, with help from Armenians and Jabalah's Christian Arabs, against the Muslim right wing and right centre, which fell back. Khalid then ordered Abu Ubayda and Yazid to attack on the left to stall the Byzantine front."],["Khalid's guard flanks the Byzantine left centre","Khalid divided the mobile guard into two and attacked the flanks of the Byzantine left centre, while the Muslim right centre attacked from the front. The left centre fell back, and the exposed left wing fell back with it."],["The Day of Lost Eyes","Byzantine horse-archers took the field and shot at the troops of Abu Ubayda and Yazid so heavily that they could not press the Byzantine lines. The Muslim armies fell back, except one regiment on the left of Abu Ubayda's corps."],["Ikrimah covers the retreat","Ikrimah covered the retreat with his 400 cavalry by attacking the Byzantine front. All of his men were seriously injured or dead that day, and he himself was mortally wounded."],["Dhirar secures the bridge in the night","Khalid had grouped all his cavalry into one force of about 8,000, with the mobile guard as its core. He sent Dhirar with 500 cavalry round the Byzantine northern flank to take the bridge at Ayn Dhakar, the best crossing of the western ravine."],["The cavalry rides round the left wing and it collapses","Khalid galloped his cavalry round the Byzantine left wing and attacked its rear while the Muslim right wing pressed from the front. The left wing fell back and collapsed onto the left centre, disordering it, and the Muslim right centre attacked that centre's front."],["The Byzantine cavalry is routed","Vahan ordered his cavalry to group, but too slowly. Khalid wheeled his cavalry back and fell on the squadrons from the front and the flank while they were still forming, and they were routed and dispersed to the north."],["The Byzantine centre breaks and the army is trapped","Khalid's cavalry fell on the Byzantine left centre from behind and broke it, and a general retreat began. The Byzantines fell back west towards the bridge, which Dhirar's men already held, while Khalid took his cavalry north to block that way out."]]],
  "Battle on the Ice": ["battle-on-the-ice","97c486918b",[["The two armies stand on the lake","Alexander and the men of Novgorod drew up their forces by the lake. The crusaders stood opposite, ready to ride at them. The wings were part of Alexander's army."],["The crusaders charge across the lake","The knights and crusaders charged across the lake and reached the enemy. The chronicle of Novgorod says they drove themselves like a wedge through the Rus' army."],["The militia holds them up","The infantry of the Novgorodian militia held up the charge and its momentum slowed. For a little more than two hours the two sides fought at close quarters on the frozen surface."],["Alexander sends in the wings","After a little more than two hours Alexander ordered the left and right wings of his army, cavalry among them, into the battle. The crusaders were by then exhausted from the struggle on the slippery ice."],["The crusaders fall back in disarray","The crusaders started to retreat in disarray, deeper onto the ice, with the fresh Novgorod cavalry now facing them."],["The retreat becomes a panic","The appearance of the fresh Novgorod cavalry made the crusaders retreat in panic. The Novgorod First Chronicle says the fighting went on during the pursuit, seven versts short of the Subol shore."]]],
  "Battles of Khalkhin Gol": ["battles-of-khalkhin-gol","df9abe2208",[["The Soviet attack opens with guns and aircraft","The Soviet and Mongolian force stood west of the Khalkhin Gol with armour on both wings. The Japanese 23rd Infantry Division held the east bank. Artillery and aircraft opened the attack."],["The Soviet centre crosses and pins the Japanese","Three infantry divisions and a tank brigade crossed the river and attacked the east bank, supported by the guns. The Japanese were held in place while the armour on the wings prepared to move."],["The armour sweeps round both flanks","Soviet armoured units swept around the flanks of the Japanese and came at them from the rear. Each wing moved round its own side of the Japanese line while the centre held them in front."],["The wings meet at Nomonhan","The two Soviet wings joined near Nomonhan, which closed a ring behind the Japanese 23rd Infantry Division and trapped it."],["A Japanese counterattack fails to relieve the division","A Japanese force came up from outside the ring to relieve the trapped 23rd Division. The counterattack failed, and the division stayed inside the encirclement."],["The 23rd Division fails to break out","The 23rd Division tried to break out of the encirclement the next day. It pushed east against the Soviet wings and failed to get through."],["The encircled force is hit again and broken up","The surrounded Japanese would not surrender and were hit again with artillery and air attacks. By 31 August the Japanese on the Mongolian side of the border were destroyed."]]],
  "Battles of Lexington and Concord": ["battles-of-lexington-and-concord","d6aaa70b56",[["The militia stand on Lexington Common","About 80 Lexington militiamen formed up on the Common in plain sight, but not across the road to Concord. Pitcairn's advance guard entered Lexington, with Smith's remaining companies further down the road."],["The Regulars charge and the militia disperse","After shots were fired, of which the accounts disagree on the first, the Regulars charged with bayonets. Eight Lexington men were killed and ten wounded, and the militia fell back."],["The Concord militia withdraw to a hill north of the bridge","The militia of Acton, Concord and Lincoln marched out to meet the Regulars, then turned back, as the Regulars numbered about 700 and they about 250. Barrett led them across the Old North Bridge to a hill about a mile north."],["The Regulars search Concord","Smith reformed the column and marched to Concord, where he divided it to search for supplies. Four companies under Captain Parsons went beyond the North Bridge, while Laurie's companies guarded it. Barrett's militia watched from a hill to the north."],["The militia advance and the British fall back to the bridge","Seeing only a few light infantry companies below, Barrett marched his men down to a flat hilltop about 300 yards from the Old North Bridge. The two British companies holding the position near the road retreated to the bridge."],["The fight at the North Bridge","Barrett's militia advanced on the bridge and exchanged fire with Laurie's companies, who broke and fled to the grenadiers coming from the town. This isolated the four companies at the farm."],["The rear guard fires at Meriam's Corner","Parsons' companies came back to the town and the Regulars left Concord after noon. At Meriam's Corner the rear guard wheeled and fired a volley at militia who had closed to musket range, and the militia returned fire with deadly effect."],["The Regulars charge Brooks Hill","Beyond Meriam's Corner the leading Regulars charged up Brooks Hill, where nearly 500 militiamen had gathered. The colonists did not withdraw and inflicted heavy casualties on the attackers, and Smith drew his men back from the hill."],["Crossfire at the Bloody Angle","At a sharp bend where the road curved left, militia caught the British in a crossfire from both sides, and again at a second bend further on. The British lost thirty killed or wounded and escaped by breaking into a trot."],["Parker's militia ambush the column near Lexington","Near the boundary between Lincoln and Lexington, Parker's men, some of them bandaged, ambushed the column from a hill overlooking the road. Smith was wounded in the thigh and Pitcairn sent light infantry up the hill to clear the militia."],["Percy's brigade arrives and its guns open fire","A brigade of about 1,000 men with artillery under Earl Percy arrived at about 2:30 pm. Percy ordered his guns to fire at extreme range, which dispersed the pursuing militia, and Smith's men collapsed with exhaustion when they reached his lines."],["Fighting through Menotomy at Jason Russell's farm","The column left Lexington with flankers on both sides and a rear guard rotated every mile or so. Fighting grew more intense in Menotomy, where hundreds of militiamen had gathered at Jason Russell's farm."],["Percy turns onto the Charlestown road","At Watson's Corner Percy used his two guns and flankers on fresh militia. He then led his troops down a narrow track onto the road to Charlestown. The militia, about 4,000, were unprepared for it, and the circle of fire was broken."]]],
  "Fall of Constantinople": ["fall-of-constantinople","af32181ec7",[["The armies take their positions","The Ottoman army camped outside the land walls. The European regulars lay along the walls, the troops from Anatolia south of the Lycus, and the guns and Janissaries near the Mesoteichion. Constantine held the Mesoteichion and Giustiniani the Charisian Gate to the north."],["The guns open fire and the chain holds the fleet out","Mehmed's cannons fired on the walls for weeks, but the Byzantines repaired most of the damage after each shot. The Ottoman fleet made probing attacks but could not enter the Golden Horn because of the chain."],["Ships are hauled over the hill into the Horn","Mehmed had a road of greased logs laid across Galata and dragged his ships over the hill into the Golden Horn, bypassing the chain. This threatened the supply ships from Pera and demoralised the defenders."],["The fire-ship attack on the hauled ships fails","The defenders tried to destroy the Ottoman ships in the Golden Horn with fire ships. The Ottomans forced the Christians to retreat with many casualties."],["The Ottoman first waves attack at Blachernae","The Christian troops of the Ottoman Empire attacked first, followed by waves of irregular azaps and Anatolian Turkmen. They focused on a damaged section of the Blachernae walls in the north-west part of the city."],["The Turkmen breach the wall and are pushed back","The Turkmen mercenaries breached this older, weaker section of the walls and entered the city, but the defenders pushed them back just as quickly."],["The Janissaries attack and Giustiniani is wounded","The last wave, the elite Janissaries, attacked the city walls. Giustiniani, the Genoese general in charge of the defenders on land, was grievously wounded, and his evacuation from the ramparts caused a panic in the ranks of the defenders."],["The Genoese retreat and Constantine holds","Giustiniani's Genoese troops retreated into the city and towards the harbour. Constantine and his men, left to their own devices, continued to hold their ground against the Janissaries."],["The defenders are overwhelmed and the Venetians fall back","Constantine's men could not prevent the Ottomans from entering the city, and the defenders were overwhelmed at several points along the wall. Janissaries pressed forward, many Greek soldiers ran back home, and the Venetians retreated to their ships."],["The Venetians leave and the Ottoman navy holds the Horn","The Venetian captain had the gate of the Golden Horn broken open, and the Venetians left in ships filled with soldiers and refugees. A few Genoese ships and the Emperor's ships followed them. The Ottoman navy took control of the Golden Horn by midday."]]],
  "Fall of Singapore": ["fall-of-singapore","9a9d1b8fdf",[["The island divided into three sectors","Two Australian brigades held the west, where the invasion was most likely, III Indian Corps held the north-east and the Southern Area forces the south-east. The Japanese stood across the Johor Strait."],["The 5th and 18th Divisions land in the north-west","The first wave crossed the strait shortly before 20:30. The main weight, about 13,000 men, fell on the 22nd Australian Brigade, which numbered just 3,000."],["The 22nd Brigade falls back towards the centre","The Australian battalions that had been engaged regrouped and moved back east from the coast, and the Japanese divisions followed inland. The Australians were pushed back further in the early morning."],["Indian battalions are sent to the Australians","Percival, who expected further landings in the north-east, sent no help until the morning of 9 February. Two half-strength battalions of the 12th Indian Brigade then reached Bennett around noon."],["The Imperial Guards attack the 27th Brigade","The Guards crossed on the 27th Brigade's front, near the causeway, and some reached the shore. Mortars, machine-guns and burning oil on the water caused them severe casualties, and Yamashita ordered them to press on."],["The 27th Brigade withdraws from the causeway","Maxwell could not contact the 22nd Brigade and feared encirclement. His brigade withdrew east of the Bukit Timah Road, and the Allies lost the beaches by the causeway. Bennett later said he had not authorised the move."],["Guards armour lands at Kranji; 8th Indian Brigade sent","The withdrawal opened the Kranji shore, and Guards armoured units landed there unopposed and began ferrying across artillery and armour. Key sent the 8th Indian Brigade from reserve to retake the high ground south of the Causeway."],["The Jurong Line collapses","Taylor misread his orders and withdrew the 22nd Brigade east. The 12th Indian Brigade was then pulled back to the road junction near Bukit Panjang, and the 44th Indian Brigade on the left of the line withdrew in the same way."],["X Battalion goes forward to Bukit Timah","Wavell ordered a counter-attack to retake the Jurong Line. Bennett allocated X Battalion, 200 poorly trained and equipped replacements, and it advanced to an assembly area near Bukit Timah, where it camped to wait."],["The Japanese fall on X Battalion","The 5th Division aimed at Bukit Panjang and the 18th Division at Bukit Timah. They fell on X Battalion in its assembly area, and two thirds of it were killed or wounded."],["The Japanese take the Bukit Timah area","The Japanese attacked the 22nd Brigade again around the Reformatory Road. Its fighting strength fell to a few hundred men, and the Japanese captured the Bukit Timah area with the garrison's main food and fuel depots."],["The 27th Brigade's counter-attack is repulsed","The 27th Brigade was ordered to recapture Bukit Panjang as a first step towards retaking Bukit Timah. The Imperial Guards repulsed it, and the brigade was split in half on either side of the Bukit Timah Road."],["The Commonwealth forces pull back to a city perimeter","The Commonwealth forces withdrew from the northern shore and Changi to a perimeter around Singapore City at the eastern end of the island, with harassing attacks all the way. The 44th Brigade held from Buona Vista to Pasir Panjang."]]],
  "Fall of Tenochtitlan": ["fall-of-tenochtitlan","2952a95afb",[["The three camps and the sloops take their places","Cortés sent Alvarado to Tacuba, Olid to Coyohuacan and Sandoval to Iztapalapa, each with Spanish troops and about 8,000 allies, and kept the thirteen sloops under his own command on the lake. The Mexica held the city with their canoes."],["Alvarado and Olid march to Chapultepec","Alvarado and Olid marched first towards Chapultepec to cut the Mexica off from their water supply. Springs there fed much of the city by aqueduct, and the rest of its water came in by canoe."],["Alvarado and Olid are pushed back at Tlacopan","Alvarado and Olid then tried to bring their forces over the causeway at Tlacopan. The Mexica drove them back and halted the assault with a hard-fought counterattack by land and by canoe."],["The sloops meet the canoes on the lake","Cortés launched the thirteen sloops from Texcoco and met more than a thousand canoes. A favourable breeze let him overturn many of them and kill or capture many of their crews."],["A gap is opened and the sloops are shared out","After the first battle on the lake Cortés camped with Olid's forces. He opened a gap in the causeway for the sloops and gave four to Alvarado, six to Olid and two to Sandoval, so that the canoes could no longer attack from the lake side."],["The camps hold the causeways and creep forward","The Spaniards stayed on the causeways at night, since the Mexica occupied any bridge they left. They razed the districts they took and used the rubble to fill the canals and gaps, which let them advance closer to the city."],["The Mexica ambush two Spanish launches","The Mexica set an ambush with thirty pirogues in water where they had fixed impaling stakes. They captured two Spanish launches and killed Captains Juan de la Portilla and Pedro Barba."],["The Spaniards spring a counter-ambush","After capturing two chieftains, Cortés learned of a plot to ambush his launches with forty pirogues. He met it with six launches, killing many warriors and taking many prisoners, and the Mexica laid no more ambushes."],["Cuauhtemoc attacks all three camps","Cuauhtemoc attacked all three Spanish camps at once with his entire army. The Spanish kept their camps on the causeways."],["Alvarado's charge on the Tacuba Causeway is cut off","Alvarado charged his cavalry across a gap in the Tacuba Causeway. As he came out on the other side with the infantry behind, Aztec canoes filled the gap. He was wounded along with eight men, and five of his men were captured."],["Cortés pushes on to the market and is thrown back","Cortés pushed forward towards the Mexican market square but did not fill in a channel as he advanced. The Mexica counter-attacked, Cortés was wounded and almost captured, and sixty-five Spanish soldiers were taken alive."],["Alvarado reaches Tlatelolco first","Cortés ordered all three camps to advance together on the Tlatelolco marketplace. Alvarado's company arrived first, and Gutierrez de Badajoz climbed the Huichilopotzli temple, set it on fire and planted Spanish banners."],["Cortés's and Sandoval's men join Alvarado","The men of Cortés and Sandoval joined Alvarado's company after four more days of fighting. The Spaniards and their allies went on into the city, and the Mexica, though they inflicted heavy casualties, could not halt them."],["Sandoval attacks and the sloops intercept the canoes","Cortés ordered Sandoval to attack the part of the city where Cuauhtemoc had retreated. Hundreds of canoes fled the city and Cortés sent the sloops to intercept them, and the Mexica forces were destroyed."]]],
  "First Battle of the Marne": ["first-battle-of-the-marne","1de417ba57",[["The 6th Army meets the German right flank","French reservists of the 6th Army, probing northeast of Paris, met the German IV Reserve Corps near the Ourcq. Though outnumbered, the corps attacked and held them for 24 hours before it retreated."],["Kluck turns west and the 5th Army attacks Bülow","Kluck turned his army to face west against the 6th Army and moved off its attacks. The right wing of the 5th Army attacked the German 2nd Army and pinned it, while the turn left a gap between the two German armies."],["Bülow's right wing falls back","After attacks by the French 5th Army, Bülow ordered his right wing to retreat 15 km. The 2nd Army, worn and depleted, went on the defensive south of the Marne."],["Hausen attacks Foch in the marshes at night","With Bülow's right retreating, his left wing attacked with Hausen's 3rd Army. Hausen struck Foch's 9th Army at night and pushed it back 13 km, and then the attack bogged down, its soldiers exhausted."],["The BEF and the 5th Army enter the gap","The Allies sent the BEF northwest toward Kluck and the 5th Army northeast toward Bülow, into the gap between the two German armies. The BEF crossed the Petit Morin and established a bridgehead over the Marne."],["Quast breaks through the 6th Army","Kluck had telegraphed that an enveloping attack by General von Quast would bring the decision. Quast fought his way through the 6th Army's defences, and the way to Paris, 50 km away, lay open."],["Hentsch and Bülow agree on a general retreat","Hentsch reached the 2nd Army headquarters and found Bülow's right flank near collapse. Bülow ordered a 20 km withdrawal, and Hentsch agreed that a general retreat would be needed once the Allies crossed the Marne."],["Hentsch reaches Kluck and the 1st Army follows","Bülow ordered another withdrawal on the morning of 9 September. Hentsch reached Kluck's headquarters at 11:30 and said a general retreat was necessary, and Kluck, with Bülow retreating, had no choice but to follow."],["Moltke orders the 3rd, 4th and 5th Armies back","Moltke visited the 3rd, 4th and 5th Armies and ordered them to retreat to the Aisne, in addition to the retreat of the 1st and 2nd Armies already under way."],["The Germans dig in north of the Aisne","The Germans stopped retreating after 65 km and dug in north of the Aisne. The French and British followed, though the exhausted Allied armies averaged only 19 km a day."]]],
  "Normandy landings": ["normandy-landings","7a9a5b6a41",[["Airborne troops land behind the beaches","Pathfinders of the US airborne divisions arrived at 00:15, and British glider troops took the Caen canal and Orne bridges at 00:16. Many paratroopers landed far from their drop zones."],["The flanks are taken","On the western side the 82nd Airborne captured the crossroads at Sainte-Mère-Église within two hours of landing. On the eastern side the 6th Airborne held the bridges over the Caen canal and the Orne."],["The fleet shells the coast","Warships bombarded the ground behind the beaches in the dark and, from 05:50, pre-assigned targets on the beaches themselves. The assault divisions crossed in landing craft, which strong winds blew east of their intended positions."],["Utah and Omaha are landed on","The 8th Infantry Regiment of the 4th Division landed first at Utah at 06:30, about 1,800 metres south of its intended place. At Omaha, where the 1st and 29th Divisions came ashore, obstacles were largely undamaged and fire from the cliffs was heavy."],["Gold, Sword and Juno are landed on","Landings at Gold were set for 07:25 and infantry began disembarking at Sword at 07:30. At Juno the landing was delayed by choppy seas, and the men arrived ahead of their supporting armour and suffered many casualties."],["Combat teams leave Utah Beach","Combat teams began to leave Utah at around 09:00, some infantry wading through flooded fields. They skirmished with the 919th Grenadier Regiment through the day, and the 4th Division made a rendezvous with the airborne troops."],["Barely 600 men reach the heights at Omaha","Exit from Omaha was possible only through five heavily defended gullies, and by late morning barely 600 men had reached the higher ground. By noon the Americans were able to clear some lanes on the beaches."],["Fortified towns are cleared at Gold, Juno and Sword","At Gold, Juno and Sword the fortified towns were cleared in house-to-house fighting. The 2nd Battalion, King's Shropshire Light Infantry advanced on Caen to within a few kilometres of the town but had to withdraw for lack of armour support."],["The 21st Panzer Division counter-attacks","The 21st Panzer Division attacked between Sword and Juno and nearly reached the Channel. It met stiff resistance from the British 3rd Division and was soon recalled to the area between Caen and Bayeux."]]],
  "Second Battle of El Alamein": ["second-battle-of-el-alamein","1223f94b93",[["The armies stand in their lines","The Eighth Army stood in the east with XXX Corps in the north, XIII Corps in the south and X Corps ready to exploit. Rommel split his armour into a northern and a southern group, held the 90th Light Division further back and kept the Trieste Division in reserve near the coast."],["The break-in: infantry advance, armour is held up","After a barrage, the four infantry divisions of XXX Corps advanced into the Axis minefields and the armour crawled up behind. Only about half of the infantry reached their objectives and none of the tanks broke through. In the south XIII Corps met determined resistance."],["Axis tanks counter-attack from the Kidney feature","At dusk tanks of the 15th Panzer and Littorio Divisions advanced from the Kidney feature against the 1st Armoured Division. Over 100 tanks fought and half were destroyed by dark, with neither position altered."],["The Australians attack Point 29","Montgomery turned XXX Corps north towards the coast with the 9th Australian Division. The 26th Australian Brigade attacked Point 29 at midnight with artillery and 30 tanks, and took the position and 240 prisoners."],["Rommel counter-attacks at Point 29","Rommel, convinced that the main assault would come in the north, ordered the 15th Panzer and 164th Light Divisions, with part of the Italian XX Corps, to retake Point 29. Under constant artillery and air attack the counter-attack came to nothing."],["Rommel brings his armour north","Rommel reversed his policy of spreading his armour along the front. The 90th Light Division came forward and the 21st Panzer Division went north with a third of the Ariete Division. A shortage of fuel meant the move could not be reversed."],["A night attack on Woodcock and Snipe","The 2nd Battalion, The Rifle Brigade attacked Snipe and the 2nd Battalion, King's Royal Rifle Corps attacked Woodcock. Both had difficulty finding their way in the dark and dust: the KRRC dug in short of its objective, the Rifle Brigade where it judged Snipe lay."],["Rommel's attacks at Snipe and Point 29 fail","At 16:00 German and Italian tanks attacked the Rifle Brigade at Snipe, which held its ground and destroyed 22 German and 10 Italian tanks, and was withdrawn that evening. The 90th Light Division's attack on Point 29 was broken up by artillery and bombing before it reached the Australians."],["Operation Supercharge and the charge of the 9th Armoured","Two assault brigades cleared a path from 01:05 and gained most of their objectives. At 06:15 the 9th Armoured Brigade advanced on the Axis gun line and failed to create the gap, though it caused great damage."],["The Axis tanks counter-attack and fail","The remains of the 9th Armoured Brigade came under the 1st Armoured Division, which had deployed. The northern and southern armoured groups counter-attacked at 11:00 and failed under shells and bombs at a cost of about 100 tanks."],["The front is broken and Rommel orders a retreat","British armour burst a 19-kilometre hole in the Axis front. Rommel could wait no longer for Hitler's reply and at 17:30 gave the order to retreat. The two armoured groups pulled back to the west."]]],
  "Second Battle of Panipat": ["second-battle-of-panipat","c5a1e2a2b5",[["The armies face each other at Panipat","The Mughals stood with Ali Quli Khan's cavalry in the centre and a wing on each side. Hemu, with the larger army, led from an elephant between his two wings."],["Hemu looses his elephants on the Mughal wings","Hemu began the attack and sent his elephants among the Mughal right and left wings. The Mughal centre advanced and took up a position before a ravine that his elephants and horse could not cross."],["Mughal horsemen turn on the elephants","Men who escaped the elephants veered to the sides and attacked the flanks of Hemu's cavalry with arrows. The Mughal cavalry cut at the elephants' legs and riders, and Hemu pulled his elephants back."],["Ali Quli rides round to the Afghan rear","As the Afghan attack slackened, Ali Quli Khan led his cavalry out, circled round and fell on the Afghan centre from behind. Hemu hurried to meet this charge."],["The Mughal wings are driven back","Hemu kept leading counterattacks, and both Mughal wings were driven back. He moved his elephants and cavalry forward to crush the Mughal centre."],["Hemu is struck by an arrow and his army flees","Hemu was struck in the eye by a chance Mughal arrow and collapsed unconscious. His army panicked, broke formation and fled, and many were killed in flight."]]],
  "Siege of Baghdad (1258)": ["siege-of-baghdad-1258","ea6e888f68",[["The Mongol columns approach Baghdad","Hulegu led the main force, with the Golden Horde princes on the right and Kitbuqa on the left. After a council of war in mid-December Baiju returned to the vanguard at Irbil, while Baghdad held a garrison of its own."],["Baiju crosses at Mosul and marches south","Baiju crossed the Tigris at Mosul with the help of its emir and headed south. He reached the Nahr Isa canal, and his deputy Sughunchaq pushed the advance party to about 40 km from the city."],["The dawatdar's army marches out","The dawatdar met the advance party with 20,000 infantry and forced it to retreat, and the caliphal army pursued."],["The canal dykes are broken and the camp flooded","That night Baiju's forces broke the dykes of the Dujayl Canal and flooded the camp of the celebrating Abbasid army. Many drowned."],["Baiju's army routs the survivors","Baiju's army engaged the remainder the next morning. They were routed, and only a few, the dawatdar among them, got back to Baghdad."],["Hulegu and Kitbuqa close in on the city","Kitbuqa crossed the Tigris to the south and approached the Karkh suburbs, while Hulegu reached the eastern suburbs. The Mongols then enclosed the whole city with a palisade and a moat, completed within a day."],["The first breach is driven back","The assault on the walls began on 29 or 30 January. The first breach, in the southeast Ajami tower near Hulegu's camp, was made on 1 February, but the Mongols were driven back."],["The Mongols take the east battlements","Further breaches over the next two days let the Mongols reach the east battlements, and they seized control of them by 4 February."],["The dawatdar tries to escape by river","The dawatdar sailed down the Tigris to escape. The Mongol preparations on the river held and forced him back into the city with a loss of three ships."],["Unarmed men who came out are executed","A large number of unarmed soldiers and inhabitants came out of the city, apparently hoping to be spared and allowed to settle in Syria. They were divided into groups and executed."],["The caliph surrenders the city","After envoys had failed to win terms, the caliph surrendered the city on 10 February, bringing his family and 3,000 dignitaries."]]],
  "Siege of Jerusalem (1099)": ["siege-of-jerusalem-1099","7764022e5b",[["The crusaders take up positions round the walls","The crusaders could not surround the whole city, so they placed themselves where the walls could be approached. Normandy, Flanders and Godfrey faced the north and north-west, Raymond lay to the south, and the east was left unguarded."],["Raymond moves up to Mount Zion","The valley kept Raymond too far from the walls, so he moved his army up to Mount Zion after two or three days. The army then stood on the hill south-west of the wall."],["The first assault fails","The crusaders attacked the walls with whatever materials they could find. The attack had some early success but failed, which showed that they needed siege engines."],["The armies move into place for the final attack","After almost three weeks of building, the ram and the siege tower were ready. Godfrey moved east behind the ram, the tower came up from Mount Zion, and Iftikhar moved his troops to the north wall."],["The ram and the tower go forward","To the sound of trumpets the crusaders attacked from north and south. Normandy and Flanders shot at the walls, the ram was brought up to the wall, and the tower advanced from Mount Zion while the defenders fired back."],["The ram breaks through but sticks","The ram pierced the wall in the north, but it stuck and the crusaders could not use the breach. They abandoned it, and the general attack was stopped."],["The tower is pushed up against the wall","The attack was resumed at midday on Friday 15 July. The southern army pushed the siege tower right up against the wall, after the ram had been abandoned the day before."],["The crusaders enter the city","The crusaders were able to enter the city, with Godfrey and Tancred in the vanguard. Near Mount Zion the defenders still fought fiercely against Raymond's men."]]],
  "Third Battle of Panipat": ["third-battle-of-panipat","a62091a565",[["The two lines face each other","The Maratha lines stood a little north of Kala Amb with Bhau and the royal guard in the centre, the Gardis on the left and Holkar and Sindhia on the right. The Afghan line stood to the south with Najib on its left and the Rohillas on its right centre."],["The Gardis advance","The Maratha left, under Ibrahim Khan, advanced its infantry in formation against the Rohillas and Shah Pasand Khan. The first salvos from the Maratha artillery went over the Afghans' heads and did little damage."],["The first Afghan attack breaks and the Rohillas fall back","The first Afghan attack was broken by Maratha bowmen, pikemen and Gardi musketeers. Later salvos at point-blank range sent the Rohillas reeling back, and the Gardis held the field for three hours."],["Bhau charges the Afghan left centre","Bhau led a charge against the forces under Shah Wali. The attack nearly broke the Afghan lines, but Shuja-ud-Daulah did not move from his position and the Maratha mounts were exhausted."],["Scindia attacks Najib","The Marathas under Scindia attacked Najib on the Afghan left. Najib fought a defensive action and kept Scindia's forces at bay, so that the Afghan left flank still held its own."],["Ahmad Shah calls up his reserve","Ahmad Shah, who had watched the battle from his tent, sent his bodyguards to call up his 15,000 reserve troops from his camp. He arranged them as a column in front of his cavalry of musketeers."],["Part of the reserve goes to the Rohillas","By then the Afghan right was almost destroyed. Ahmad Shah sent 4,000 of his reserve troops to support the broken ranks of the Rohillas on the right."],["The rest of the reserve charges with Shah Wali","The remaining 10,000 of the reserve were sent to the aid of Shah Wali against Bhau in the centre. Some 7,000 Maratha cavalry and infantry were killed before the hand-to-hand fighting began."],["The Maratha cavalry breaks its position","The cavalry that Bhau had ordered to protect the Gardis broke its position and went all out on the Rohillas. The Rohilla riflemen fired accurately on it, as it carried only swords."],["The Gardis are encircled","The Rohillas then encircled the Gardis and outflanked the Maratha centre while Shah Wali pressed the front. The Gardis were left defenceless and fell one by one."],["Holkar and Scindia leave the field","Realising the battle was lost, Holkar's and Scindia's contingents merged, and one broke from the Maratha right and escaped south through an opening in the Durrani lines. Bhau and the royal forces fought on."]]]
};
